WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.527 --> 00:00:11.360
HECHT: Good morning.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.560
For the two or three of you
out there who don't know me,

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.920
I'm Bill Hecht,
class of '61, and I'm

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.485
your senior employee as head
of the Alumni Association.

00:00:35.485 --> 00:00:37.960
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:45.260
You might wonder how a
guy from the class of '61

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.400
got a red coat.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.960
A number of years
ago the class of '24,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.320
which is celebrating
its 70th this year,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.770
had some issues that
needed settling.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:02.560
And somehow or other I was able
to settle them for that class.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.080
And as a great
privilege and tribute,

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.740
they made me an honorary
member of that class,

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.015
insisting that I always
wear a freshman tie.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.880
So I now have my own two
connections, a six and a one.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.980
My graduate degree was in '76,
and my undergraduate in '61.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.000
And a 24, which I'm
just as proud of.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.090
And I'm busting
my buttons to say

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:29.530
that in '95, my daughter will
get a master's from this place,

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:33.650
so I'll have another
MIT connection.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:36.640
I wonder if you all know that
last night's Pops was not only

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.020
a very special night for
us, but it was actually

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:43.530
a test for the potential
new conductor of the Pops.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.452
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.550
It is said that he practiced
Arthur Fiedler's hand motions

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.750
studiously before the mirror.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:01.250
And I think that
I'm going to take

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.620
on the job of being his
agent and represent him

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.828
because I think we can get
a hell of a good contract.

00:02:07.777 --> 00:02:09.610
Finally, it's my great
pleasure, in addition

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:14.220
to welcoming you all here
today, to introduce Chuck Vest.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:17.060
Chuck is president
of MIT, as you know.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.190
He came to us about
four years ago,

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:23.490
and he may be a
little wistful today.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:28.190
As you know, many people who
come in the fall of four years

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.000
graduated about a week ago.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.260
His class, the class
of '94, did leave.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:36.940
Chuck, I guess fortunately
for us, but perhaps not so

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.480
fortunately for him,
still remains with us.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.930
So let me introduce Chuck
Vest, president of MIT.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.328
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:51.427 --> 00:02:52.760
VEST: Thank you very much, Bill.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:56.830
I'm just signed up for the new
five-year master of engineering

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:58.920
program, that's all.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.320
I've been for years
wanting to get up the nerve

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:05.150
to ask Bill exactly what
this great favor he did

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:08.600
for that class is, but, because
I'm not sure of its legality,

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.575
I've been afraid to ask.

00:03:11.575 --> 00:03:14.240
On a more serious note
and a very happy one,

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.260
I understand that we
have a record number

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.390
of alums and families here for
reunions on Technology Day.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.890
You are over 3,000 strong.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:25.860
My congratulations.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.645
And we all have an
exciting day ahead of us.

00:03:28.645 --> 00:03:30.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:35.980
The speakers for today's
program are truly

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:38.170
a stellar group of
men and women who

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:41.620
will talk about art and
culture and the academy

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.480
and specifically at MIT.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.480
Here at MIT, where the arts
have evolved over 100 years.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.520
From a single extracurricular
activity in 1884,

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:57.780
the MIT Banjo Club, to a
panoply of artistic and cultural

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.190
endeavors that you're going to
have a chance to sample here

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.970
today in 1994.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.650
Special thanks go to
Jorge Rodriguez, chairman

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.130
of the Technology Day Committee,
and to all the members

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:13.780
of the Technology Day Committee,
the Council for the Arts,

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.990
for putting together such
an astounding and exciting

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.709
program.

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:21.839
I might add that the watercolor
reproduced in the Technology

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:25.250
Day poster was painted by
one of our graduate students

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:29.320
in the School of Architecture
and Planning, Lian Quan Zhen,

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.640
and there are other
examples of his work

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:35.600
on display today in the lobby.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.250
Now to some it might seem odd
that in this bastion of science

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:42.940
and engineering we bring
together our alumni

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:46.920
and alumnae to
celebrate the arts.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.040
But most of you probably had
at least a brush with the arts

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.090
at MIT, and many
of you, I'm sure,

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:56.520
became more deeply involved
in our music and theater arts

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.650
programs.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:02.340
And now more than ever, our
students and our faculty

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:06.820
are engaged in a wide range of
artistic and cultural pursuits,

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:11.250
in visual arts, in
architecture, music, theater,

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.790
creative writing,
and photography,

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:16.960
as well as in the emerging
media of interactive video,

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.090
holography, and
hyperinstruments.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:23.680
Still, some of
you might ask why.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.140
Well, it is true that
MIT is best known

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.590
throughout the world for
the caliber of the engineers

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.680
and scientists we send
out from this place.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.280
The Institute has in fact
produced a steady flow

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.560
of artists, architects,
musicians, dancers, actors,

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:43.720
and others who are best known
for their artistic endeavors.

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:47.010
We have string players and
wind players, composers

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:50.340
and conductors in orchestras
around the country.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.460
We have actors on
Broadway and in Hollywood.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.960
And we have MIT graduates on
the faculties of music schools

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.710
in many other universities.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.370
Of course, most MIT
students continue

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:06.270
to pursue traditional careers
in engineering and science.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:09.900
But we are no less serious
about teaching music, theater,

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.620
and the arts to these students.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:15.600
The arts, when taught
seriously and deeply,

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:19.800
offer our students opportunities
to expand their imagination,

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.750
broaden their
perspective, and imagine

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:25.700
ever-widening new possibilities.

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:28.150
This is true for all of us.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:31.250
When we develop our ability
to think creatively,

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:33.200
we are able to
find new solutions

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.460
to the problems on
which we are working,

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:40.510
both in our laboratories and
in other aspects of our lives.

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:42.800
Take, for example, the
fields of engineering

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.680
and particularly
engineering education.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:48.990
We are moving very dramatically
toward a more integrative style

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:52.600
of education, with
increased emphasis on design

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:56.330
and synthesis and a little
bit less emphasis on analysis

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:58.760
and reductionist science.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:01.520
I think that art
and an appreciation

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.800
for the aesthetic components
of all that people do

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.480
is very helpful in this regard.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:10.140
As we work to improve
the quality of designs

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:12.850
and products, it is
going to be increasingly

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:16.290
important for engineers
to cooperate with artists

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:20.960
to tackle the visual, tactile,
and aesthetic qualities

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:22.670
of their work.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:26.360
But quite aside from their role
in scientific and engineering

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.810
endeavors, the arts
should, of course,

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:33.020
still be an important part of
our lives and of our education.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:34.050
Why?

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:36.630
Because they are
part of our humanity.

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.500
They reflect our
human experience.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.120
The arts give us
a different kind

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:43.560
of connection with
the world, and they

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.590
can help us to understand the
many cultures that make up

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:49.170
our community and our world.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:51.780
The arts allow people from
very different perspectives

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:54.980
and backgrounds to
communicate and to interact

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:58.710
with an almost built-in
degree of mutual respect.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:01.130
This is important
for our graduates

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:05.030
as they take their skills into
an increasingly interdependent,

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:08.200
global, and multicultural world.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:11.190
And this is very
important for MIT.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.935
The more innovative we are in
celebrating and encouraging

00:08:13.935 --> 00:08:17.030
the richness and diversity
of our community,

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:20.070
the better off
all of us will be.

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:24.010
In sum, the arts at MIT
are essential elements

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:27.670
of our education and
of our community life.

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:31.440
Not only are they healthy
here, they are thriving.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:34.950
This is great testimony to
the talent, the dedication,

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:40.480
and the enthusiasm of a great
many faculty and students.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:43.870
And it is particular
testimony to one person

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:46.770
who has done an extraordinary
job in stimulating

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.440
and supporting the arts
since she arrived here

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:52.930
at MIT in 1989.

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:55.920
I refer, of course, to my
colleague Professor Ellen

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.710
Harris, associate provost
for the arts at MIT.

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:03.430
Ellen is a distinguished
scholar of music

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:07.100
who is also known widely
as a soprano soloist.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:10.010
She came to us from the
University of Chicago,

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:11.980
where she was a professor
of music and head

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.070
of the Department of Music.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:17.510
As a scholar, she has been
recognized for her work

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:19.930
on the music of Handel.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:22.820
As a singer, she is best
known for her dramatic stage

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:24.550
performances.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:27.580
Those of you who were here
for commencement last week

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:29.620
and heard her sing
the national anthem

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:32.340
know precisely what I mean.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:35.390
Ellen's arrival on campus
was the final fulfillment

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:40.510
of a set of recommendations
made in 1987 by an arts review

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:44.380
committee, which recommended the
appointment of a senior officer

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:47.990
who would also be a faculty
member in either the performing

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:52.330
or visual arts, someone to
take broad responsibility

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:55.290
as an advocate,
leader, and coordinator

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:58.950
for the creative arts
program here at MIT.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:03.270
Believe me, Ellen Harris
serves superbly in that role.

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:06.030
Ladies and gentlemen,
Professor Ellen Harris.

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:09.030
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:18.620
HARRIS: Thank you, Chuck.

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:19.897
Good morning.

00:10:19.897 --> 00:10:21.012
AUDIENCE: Good morning.

00:10:21.012 --> 00:10:21.720
HARRIS: Oh, good.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:29.080
It's often been said that
at MIT, one's education

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:31.550
is like taking a drink
from a fire hose.

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:36.340
What I have noticed is
that so is Tech Day.

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:39.010
We have here before
9:00 in the morning

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:41.560
after having you out
very late last night,

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:45.130
and I appreciate seeing
you all here so bright

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:47.300
and raring to go.

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:49.800
I'm proud and privileged
to welcome you

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:54.020
to The Wonder of It All,
The Arts at MIT, Technology

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:56.650
Day 1994.

00:10:56.650 --> 00:11:00.390
And I want to add my thanks
to Jorge Rodriguez, the chair

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:04.170
of the alumni Tech Day Committee
and also to Eliza [? Dame, ?]

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:08.860
who does so much, more than
I could possibly say, to make

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:12.730
Tech Day work year after year.

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:16.400
Many people like to talk
about the arts at MIT

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:19.510
as if it's an anomaly,
as if it's a surprise,

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:22.050
or as if it's something new.

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:24.830
But of course, you know
better than anyone else

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:26.840
that that is not the case.

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:30.670
The arts have been a part
of MIT since its founding.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:34.690
MIT was the first school
to establish a School

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:37.960
of Architecture in 1868.

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:41.350
It actually began offering
courses in architecture

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:44.700
in its first year, 1865.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:47.480
In that same year, we
were offering courses

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:50.400
in photography and in drawing.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:54.240
In 1884 we not only
established the Banjo Club,

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:57.260
but we established
the Glee Club.

00:11:57.260 --> 00:12:01.270
In 1928 we established
Drama Shop,

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:04.940
the forerunner of our
theater curriculum today.

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:09.730
And in 1948, John
Corley came to MIT

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:14.140
to establish the MIT Band, an
organization that he continues

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:17.148
to conduct today.

00:12:17.148 --> 00:12:20.124
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:27.810
In 1950, the Hayden
Gallery opened,

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:31.220
which was the forerunner of our
now internationally renowned

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:34.040
List Visual Arts Center.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:41.530
But perhaps most importantly,
in 1944, 50 years ago today,

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:47.700
MIT first established its
eight-semester sequence

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:49.780
in humanities.

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:53.880
Those of you who have been
at the Institute since 1944

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:56.940
will remember your eight
courses in the humanities.

00:12:56.940 --> 00:12:58.960
They used to be different
than they are today.

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:05.020
But in 1944 they
included, I think,

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:08.900
a first-year course in writing
and English literature,

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:12.810
second-year course in history,
third-year course where

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:15.250
you could choose from
a number of subjects,

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:16.830
including economics.

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:19.930
And in the fourth year, a
wider selection of courses,

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:24.210
including a yearlong
course in music.

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:27.160
Because of this
eight-semester sequence

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:31.120
of courses, which parallels
the eight semester

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:36.160
courses in science, MIT
has had for many years

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:39.920
a program in education
which has been referred

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:42.160
to here as dual literacy.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:45.060
MIT students have been
asked to be literate

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:49.400
not only in science but in
humanities and in the arts.

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:54.470
In fact, MIT may be one of the
few educational institutions

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:58.360
in the country that asks
this of its students.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.250
It is very, very easy
at many liberal arts

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:04.970
institutions to
avoid the sciences.

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:08.360
Not so easy at MIT to
avoid the humanities.

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:16.120
In fact, this dual
literacy has meant

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:21.570
that we have within
our students and alums

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:25.490
many people who have gone
on to careers in the arts.

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:30.440
In fact, in the class of 1994,
I can think of four cases

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:32.390
immediately.

00:14:32.390 --> 00:14:34.770
Two young men who
have double majored

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:37.840
in mathematics and
music, both of whom

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:40.750
are going on right now
to graduate school, one

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:44.850
to the University of Chicago
and one to Duke in music

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:47.080
composition.

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:49.100
We have another
student who majored

00:14:49.100 --> 00:14:52.900
in civil engineering
who has received

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:58.700
a full scholarship to pursue
writing at Boston University.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:02.440
And we have another student
who majored in theater

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:08.300
at MIT who is going on to pursue
his work in theater who hopes

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:12.190
to establish his own theater
company in the countryside

00:15:12.190 --> 00:15:12.695
of Mexico.

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:17.310
I sometimes think
we must graduate

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:19.880
the most scientifically
literate artists in the world.

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:24.400
But this is not
something that was

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:27.150
just true of the class of 1994.

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:29.400
And I was looking at
the reunion classes

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:31.420
for some other examples.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:34.540
And obviously, we have
a lot of people today

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:38.050
here from the class of 1944.

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:38.953
Right?

00:15:38.953 --> 00:15:41.368
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:45.670
Well, one of your members,
John [? Bevicki, ?]

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:48.630
who graduated with a
major in management,

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:51.320
has gone on to be a
very well-known composer

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:53.900
and conductor who has
been for many years

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:56.950
on the faculty of the
Berkeley School of Music.

00:15:56.950 --> 00:16:00.290
From the class of '64.

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:01.490
'64?

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:03.030
Do I hear the class of '64?

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:09.910
We have John Miller, who
double majored in humanities

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:14.320
and engineering who has made his
career as principal bassoonist

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:17.060
with the Minnesota Orchestra.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:18.320
Class of '69.

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:20.600
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:26.700
You had with you for
some time James Woods,

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:28.520
who was majoring in
political science

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:32.240
before he went on to become
a very well-known actor.

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:34.510
In '79--

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:36.860
[SPARSE APPLAUSE]

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:40.360
I think we need
more energy, folks.

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:48.480
Liz Maruska, who went on
to become a practicing

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:50.720
artist on the West
Coast who paints

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:53.880
beautiful canvases in
watercolor and gouache,

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:56.610
expressing time and space.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:59.695
And finally, class
of '89, five years.

00:16:59.695 --> 00:17:01.050
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:06.950
Ken Goodson, who majored in
both mechanical engineering

00:17:06.950 --> 00:17:09.349
and music, received
his master of science

00:17:09.349 --> 00:17:13.710
in '91 in mechanical
engineering, his PhD in '93,

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:16.760
is now studying and
performing as a baritone

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:20.130
soloist in Germany.

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:25.849
I love about Ken that when
he performed at Tanglewood

00:17:25.849 --> 00:17:28.270
as an MIT grad, it
is only appropriate

00:17:28.270 --> 00:17:32.370
that, when singing in
Stravinsky's work "The Flood,"

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:34.615
Ken Goodson played
the role of God.

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:44.880
But obviously, MIT does not
graduate all of its students

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:46.010
to go on in the arts.

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:50.670
You don't need to be
an arts professional

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:52.720
to benefit from the arts.

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:55.730
And whether you worked in Doc
Edgerton's strobe photography

00:17:55.730 --> 00:17:59.340
lab, or perhaps
became interested

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:01.110
in physics through
Berenice Abbot's

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:04.030
extraordinary photographs
of physical phenomena that

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:06.520
were published in many
high school textbooks

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:09.740
and that were taken
at MIT, or whether you

00:18:09.740 --> 00:18:12.510
played in the symphony,
or sang in the Glee Club,

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:15.510
or acted in a
theatrical production,

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:19.370
I'm sure that at some point
art touched your life at MIT

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:22.510
and has continued to touch
your life thereafter.

00:18:22.510 --> 00:18:24.960
Arts offer us discipline.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:28.870
Anyone who has practiced
the piano knows that.

00:18:28.870 --> 00:18:32.280
Arts offer us imagination.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:36.010
They offer us the
flexibility of a pursuit

00:18:36.010 --> 00:18:38.810
where there is not
one right answer.

00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:41.870
But they also offer us
new ways of organization,

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:46.750
of organization of sound
and space and time.

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:51.440
The arts offer us a doorway
into cultures other than our own

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:54.930
that enable us not only
to tolerate but respect

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:56.590
difference.

00:18:56.590 --> 00:19:01.210
The arts challenge us, surprise
us, sometimes they anger us.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:04.820
But the arts also bring
us wonder and joy.

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:08.320
And today we hope to explore
through our presentations

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:11.800
the wonder and joy of
the arts today at MIT.

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:17.050
This, of course,
leads me directly

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:21.200
to our first speaker, Philip
Morrison, Institute professor

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:23.415
emeritus at MIT.

00:19:23.415 --> 00:19:26.910
You are probably familiar
with his work in astrophysics

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:30.180
and cosmology, maybe less
familiar with his work

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:32.540
as a filmmaker.

00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:36.340
I first came to know
Philip through a copy

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:39.010
of a keynote
address that he gave

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:44.480
in 1986 for the 15th anniversary
of the Council for the Arts

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:48.690
at MIT, an organization
founded in 1971

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:54.640
to advise and support MIT in the
expansion of its arts programs.

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:59.730
At that time he
said, and I quote,

00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:03.870
"It's clear that if an
institution like this one which

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:09.160
deals with the material world
did not have the side of art,

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:12.370
it would be a self-defeating
proposition which in the end

00:20:12.370 --> 00:20:17.560
would be all genetic seed
and no fruit to the apple.

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:21.720
And an apple is
not made that way."

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:25.820
I think that I can do no
better in introducing Professor

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:30.200
Morrison than to quote his
colleague Victor Weisskopf, who

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:34.080
said of him, "Scientific
knowledge and understanding is

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:36.820
not a purely cerebral affair.

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:39.700
It is soaked with
emotion, excitement,

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:42.130
and nervous tension,
as everybody

00:20:42.130 --> 00:20:46.410
knows who has ever heard
Professor Morrison speak."

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:47.910
Ladies and gentlemen,
please help me

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:49.960
to welcome Professor Morrison.

00:20:49.960 --> 00:20:53.411
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:28.722
MORRISON: Thank you very much.

00:21:28.722 --> 00:21:32.173
I'm going to talk to
them about it, I think.

00:21:32.173 --> 00:21:37.230
I have much equipment here,
all of which seems to work.

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:39.800
Especially impressed by the
video projection system.

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:47.590
The Wonder of It All
at MIT, Arts at MIT

00:21:47.590 --> 00:21:49.030
is a very nice title.

00:21:49.030 --> 00:21:50.150
Doesn't say much.

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:52.120
Commits very few speakers.

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:55.580
I would certainly like to
affix firmly onto the word

00:21:55.580 --> 00:22:00.240
"wonder" because clearly
that's the kind of motive which

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:04.030
lies behind nearly all the
things that go on at MIT.

00:22:04.030 --> 00:22:07.780
Translated, as eventually
they are, into the hard tasks

00:22:07.780 --> 00:22:09.905
of earning a living and
making this world work.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:15.860
The old classical
Chinese scholars

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:19.580
always like to pretend that the
ideas they had were very old.

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:21.160
So they found some
mysterious figure

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:23.220
of the past who nobody
knew much about, typically

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:24.261
the Yellow Emperor.

00:22:24.261 --> 00:22:26.760
And they said, well, the Yellow
Emperor first said, and then

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:29.936
they made up their own books.

00:22:29.936 --> 00:22:30.810
We don't do that now.

00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:32.050
We have a much more
forward looking.

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:33.670
And we don't look
back in general.

00:22:33.670 --> 00:22:35.170
It's always new.

00:22:35.170 --> 00:22:37.710
That's the same
error in reverse.

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:41.370
People have this tendency
for bipolar positioning,

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:44.240
bipolar stability of large
axes because everything

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:46.630
is both-- has something
old and something new

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:48.670
or it's not been
pursued very far.

00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:50.490
That's the typical lesson.

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:52.890
Ask a historian, he'll
take it back for you.

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:56.970
Ask a projector of the
future, he'll push it forward.

00:22:56.970 --> 00:22:58.530
Both things lie in time.

00:23:01.370 --> 00:23:04.470
I wanted, however, and I
didn't always know this.

00:23:04.470 --> 00:23:06.743
And I became more and
more convinced of it

00:23:06.743 --> 00:23:10.590
in the past recent
times, a few years,

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:19.360
maybe, the proposition that
this issue that we now debate

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:22.020
is extraordinarily old.

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:24.820
I could invoke no
newer a phrase for it

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:33.270
than this Latin tag of the
Institute, mens et manus, mind

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:35.630
and hand, to talk about it.

00:23:35.630 --> 00:23:39.400
Because, of course, that is the
exquisite pleasure of the MIT

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:44.900
ambiance that minds and hands
are equally well occupied.

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:49.750
That means to say that a
lot of mechanical things

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:51.930
are done, a lot of things
in the material world

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:55.100
or with the refractory symbols
of the world that are hammered

00:23:55.100 --> 00:23:59.000
away at, whether it be metal
or melodies or whatever it is,

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:01.770
but that's not done
by and large by rote.

00:24:01.770 --> 00:24:04.560
Obviously some rote goes
into all operations, experts

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:09.640
become routinized, but
thought is at the basis.

00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:13.170
And it turns out that
that unity, which

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:21.830
is a profound unity between
art and the triune quality

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:26.910
of modifying and knowing the
world physically-- craft,

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:30.230
engineering and science
in their relationships--

00:24:30.230 --> 00:24:31.970
goes back a very long time.

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:35.360
And I brought along a few
slides to justify that

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:37.060
and to get us well started.

00:24:37.060 --> 00:24:39.730
So it's not a new task
that we're looking into.

00:24:39.730 --> 00:24:43.320
This wonderful picture in the
center, collection of rocks,

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:45.470
deserves some close attention.

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:47.324
Those rocks are
not natural rocks.

00:24:47.324 --> 00:24:49.240
The fact that they are
quite similar, I think,

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:50.660
is pretty evident.

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:54.070
It's also quite evident
if you look more closely,

00:24:54.070 --> 00:24:55.670
not easy to see, perhaps.

00:24:55.670 --> 00:24:57.450
It's better to handle them.

00:24:57.450 --> 00:24:58.459
We can't do that.

00:24:58.459 --> 00:25:00.000
But I think it's
enough to suggest it

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.030
in a context like this.

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:06.170
You can see the three edges
here, the double ones there.

00:25:06.170 --> 00:25:09.870
Every one has faceted edges.

00:25:09.870 --> 00:25:13.850
This one has, in fact, a slight
double kind of blade-like edge

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:14.640
and so on.

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:17.780
This is a pile of rocks,
a section, a collection,

00:25:17.780 --> 00:25:21.030
made by the excavators
in Olduvai Gorge

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:22.900
in the rift of East Africa.

00:25:22.900 --> 00:25:24.240
These were not found that way.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:27.740
They were found stratum after
stratum carefully located,

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:30.550
carefully arranged in position
so they could be dated.

00:25:30.550 --> 00:25:32.980
But the photographer had
simply gathered a lot of them

00:25:32.980 --> 00:25:34.940
to give us an impression
of how many hundreds,

00:25:34.940 --> 00:25:40.430
indeed many thousands, of
these stone tools are found.

00:25:40.430 --> 00:25:42.430
Now the remarkable
part about them.

00:25:42.430 --> 00:25:45.400
This, of course,
is mens et manus.

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:47.740
Mens is pretty thin still.

00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:50.650
These are not even
our own species.

00:25:50.650 --> 00:25:52.660
These are our distant forebears.

00:25:52.660 --> 00:25:54.560
They were poor.

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:58.520
They first walked the Earth
fearful of the beasts.

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:01.130
Pretty soon that
stopped, and the beasts

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:02.570
tended to avoid them.

00:26:02.570 --> 00:26:04.410
Before that, perhaps
even they scavenged

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:06.230
on the kill of the lion.

00:26:06.230 --> 00:26:12.610
But sooner or later, this kind
of operation symbolized here,

00:26:12.610 --> 00:26:16.480
mind and hand, began to
change where they were.

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:20.590
And they did marvelous things
on a very slow time scale.

00:26:20.590 --> 00:26:23.500
The pieces represented
in this collection,

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:26.590
just that arbitrary piece
of a large collection,

00:26:26.590 --> 00:26:32.210
are measured not by centuries
but by thousands of centuries.

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:34.260
Yes, by hundreds of
thousands of years.

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:36.940
A few hundred thousand
years corresponds

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:39.020
to a typical
collection at Olduvai,

00:26:39.020 --> 00:26:41.290
which might extend a
million years or more.

00:26:41.290 --> 00:26:46.050
But to be generous, we'll
say a few thousand centuries.

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:48.510
Now the striking part of
this is that these tools,

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:50.620
without very much
change, are found

00:26:50.620 --> 00:26:52.370
over a very large
part of the world,

00:26:52.370 --> 00:26:55.490
as the next slide will show.

00:26:55.490 --> 00:26:55.990
There it is.

00:26:55.990 --> 00:26:57.010
Here are famous sites.

00:26:57.010 --> 00:26:59.930
Anyone who has read anything
about early archaeology

00:26:59.930 --> 00:27:03.750
will recognize these
kinds of locations.

00:27:03.750 --> 00:27:07.410
From Britain up here into
Europe, France at the Pyrenees,

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:09.620
especially very
important in there,

00:27:09.620 --> 00:27:11.530
all across Africa, East Africa.

00:27:11.530 --> 00:27:13.780
Here we have Olduvai itself.

00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:15.570
And down to Cape Town.

00:27:15.570 --> 00:27:19.440
So from Britain to Cape Town
and from Cape Town across Asia,

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:25.750
Central Asia, India, North
China, Peking, further

00:27:25.750 --> 00:27:29.580
north still, and into Java.

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:31.930
All these, the major
sites, each site

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:34.790
feathers out into many,
many little excavations.

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:37.930
And from all of these places
about the same kind of tools

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:38.730
are collected.

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:40.870
Yes, the experts argue a
bit, and they can somewhat

00:27:40.870 --> 00:27:41.595
classify them.

00:27:41.595 --> 00:27:43.220
But it's really quite
hard to date them

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:47.440
except by the attendant
pieces of mineral,

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:49.920
which can help you to date
them by radioactive means.

00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:53.000
But you'll notice that
not one site is found

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:56.740
in these islands or Australia.

00:27:56.740 --> 00:27:58.450
And I've left out the Americas.

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:00.960
No use to put it on
the map because not one

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:05.590
site of this kind has ever
surely been found in America.

00:28:05.590 --> 00:28:09.500
No, Australia, the great
islands near Australia,

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:12.480
and the two Americas
do not belong

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:15.400
to these people, who
I don't wish to say

00:28:15.400 --> 00:28:17.350
are-- they are our ancestors.

00:28:17.350 --> 00:28:19.200
Cannot speak ill of them.

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:22.370
They were, in fact,
the Prometheans.

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:24.890
Because somewhere in the
middle of their span,

00:28:24.890 --> 00:28:29.830
Homo erectus figured out how
to make and control fire.

00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:32.460
And the hammer at
MIT is entirely

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:35.820
secondary to the
fire and all that

00:28:35.820 --> 00:28:36.987
goes with that symbolically.

00:28:36.987 --> 00:28:39.111
So I don't show you those
sites, though, of course,

00:28:39.111 --> 00:28:40.810
there are very many
sites in America.

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:44.040
But none of them show
the bones or the tools

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:45.730
of these ancient folk.

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:49.920
We truly, like the Australians,
Australian Aboriginals,

00:28:49.920 --> 00:28:54.620
the Native Americans,
are the modern humans,

00:28:54.620 --> 00:28:56.640
like all the rest of
this place has also been

00:28:56.640 --> 00:28:57.780
filled in by modern humans.

00:28:57.780 --> 00:29:00.030
I don't mean to say that
we're, of course, we're not--

00:29:00.030 --> 00:29:02.430
most modern humans live
in this part of the world.

00:29:02.430 --> 00:29:05.450
But the same species
occupies this whole region.

00:29:05.450 --> 00:29:09.800
And only after a long time,
after thousands of centuries,

00:29:09.800 --> 00:29:13.960
it managed, they managed, in the
course of some internal change,

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:16.090
which is what we would
call a change in species

00:29:16.090 --> 00:29:18.980
to Homo sapiens,
they managed by means

00:29:18.980 --> 00:29:20.440
that are not-- can
be well debated,

00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:22.450
interesting to
study-- they managed

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:26.770
to be able to cross the then
much shallower coastal seas.

00:29:26.770 --> 00:29:29.040
And finally the great
gulf of the Timor

00:29:29.040 --> 00:29:32.820
Trench and come somehow by
canoe or raft to Australia.

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:36.550
And in the same way go
across the relenting Arctic,

00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:39.250
where the glaciers were melting,
and follow the coastline

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:40.050
of America.

00:29:40.050 --> 00:29:43.450
And go down the full length of
both continents rather quickly.

00:29:43.450 --> 00:29:46.230
And that completed the
occupation of the world

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:48.550
by modern humans.

00:29:48.550 --> 00:29:50.970
So we are the new world,
not because of Columbus

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:52.950
or any feature of
historical importance.

00:29:52.950 --> 00:29:54.250
Those are small matters.

00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:57.610
The real thing was, we were
new because the new people, who

00:29:57.610 --> 00:29:59.930
filled up the old
lands very well,

00:29:59.930 --> 00:30:03.730
had a way also to get to new
lands never before occupied.

00:30:03.730 --> 00:30:09.260
So our histories go back only
a few hundred centuries, not

00:30:09.260 --> 00:30:10.494
a few thousand centuries.

00:30:10.494 --> 00:30:12.160
There's an order of
magnitude difference

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:17.420
between the kind of thing you
saw in the Olduvai rocks--

00:30:17.420 --> 00:30:20.670
bifaces, choppers, hand
axes, they're called--

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:24.500
and the kind of thing that
came when a new set of persons

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:25.990
of whom we are all descended.

00:30:25.990 --> 00:30:31.850
We can prove by the
deepest of biological means

00:30:31.850 --> 00:30:36.700
how united the whole world
is in our single species,

00:30:36.700 --> 00:30:40.240
with subspecies,
as we well know.

00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:42.060
And the next slide
will show what

00:30:42.060 --> 00:30:44.590
you find in these new sites.

00:30:44.590 --> 00:30:48.290
Now please compare these,
which are nice American pieces.

00:30:48.290 --> 00:30:50.703
They were made about
10,000 or 12,000,

00:30:50.703 --> 00:30:53.350
well, 12,000 or
14,000 years ago.

00:30:53.350 --> 00:30:56.270
These are found in
a region in Montana.

00:30:56.270 --> 00:30:59.900
And of course, they're
specialized for killing

00:30:59.900 --> 00:31:00.994
big game.

00:31:00.994 --> 00:31:03.410
And if you'd like to know what
the big game was-- probably

00:31:03.410 --> 00:31:06.240
you do know-- it was elephants.

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:11.090
Pieces quite like this,
spear points like this,

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:16.860
have been found in contact with
the bones of bison and mammoth.

00:31:16.860 --> 00:31:21.010
And of course, where the
other rocks had 10 or 20

00:31:21.010 --> 00:31:23.740
rather cunning blows struck,
which you and I could not do,

00:31:23.740 --> 00:31:26.760
which people can train
themselves to do today,

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:31.530
these have many hundreds
of knowing strokes

00:31:31.530 --> 00:31:33.112
to chip the blade
just correctly.

00:31:33.112 --> 00:31:34.570
And even along the
edge you can see

00:31:34.570 --> 00:31:38.160
to sharpen it with a wonderful
thing, very detailed marks

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:40.360
up there, all of which are
significant in producing

00:31:40.360 --> 00:31:42.520
a handy and useful blade.

00:31:42.520 --> 00:31:46.780
So I have no doubt to say this
is craftsmanship, possibly even

00:31:46.780 --> 00:31:47.370
engineering.

00:31:47.370 --> 00:31:49.440
At least it's
high-quality craftsmanship

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:51.700
because we know now that
many of these blades

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:53.730
have to be heat treated
in order to allow

00:31:53.730 --> 00:31:56.155
the chert and the flint to
respond to the chipping.

00:31:56.155 --> 00:31:58.780
But now I'd like to go a little
farther and show what they did.

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:00.780
And it happens that the
preservations I have now

00:32:00.780 --> 00:32:02.750
are again the same new
people, our people,

00:32:02.750 --> 00:32:04.780
but now coming
from France and not

00:32:04.780 --> 00:32:08.030
from Montana, the following.

00:32:08.030 --> 00:32:11.830
A bone object used
for working sinew

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:13.730
and straightening arrow shafts.

00:32:13.730 --> 00:32:15.010
You can see the hole there.

00:32:15.010 --> 00:32:17.760
But it wasn't enough
to make it utilitarian.

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:20.420
It has this marvelous
leaping horse

00:32:20.420 --> 00:32:22.970
worked into the same material.

00:32:22.970 --> 00:32:28.120
And this is about
200 or 250 centuries

00:32:28.120 --> 00:32:32.710
old from Bruniquel
Cave in France.

00:32:32.710 --> 00:32:34.480
We have art so much
from France and Spain

00:32:34.480 --> 00:32:37.130
because the caverns are
so well protected there.

00:32:37.130 --> 00:32:39.330
They guarded the art
behind stalactites

00:32:39.330 --> 00:32:41.250
for many thousands of years.

00:32:41.250 --> 00:32:44.330
And the next slide perhaps
even more striking.

00:32:44.330 --> 00:32:48.740
An image itself, an
elk of the period.

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:51.860
The biologists will find
fossils that fit it quite well.

00:32:51.860 --> 00:32:56.310
Beautifully drawn in
charcoal and ocher

00:32:56.310 --> 00:32:57.950
on the wall of the
French cave, this

00:32:57.950 --> 00:32:59.660
on the famous cave of Lascaux.

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:06.340
So I rest the case
that the relationship,

00:33:06.340 --> 00:33:08.640
the change, that
came to human beings,

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:10.280
we don't exactly
know what it is.

00:33:10.280 --> 00:33:12.049
Most likely it was
the acquisition

00:33:12.049 --> 00:33:13.590
of a powerful
language, which enabled

00:33:13.590 --> 00:33:16.570
them to work cooperatively
and to model the future,

00:33:16.570 --> 00:33:19.590
gave rise to the
craft and the art,

00:33:19.590 --> 00:33:21.400
which you unmistakably
see here which

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:26.270
goes back not merely thousands
of years but tens of thousands

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:27.000
of years.

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:29.650
This particular piece is dated
about 15,000 or 18,000 years

00:33:29.650 --> 00:33:32.850
ago, the previous one
about 20,000 years ago.

00:33:32.850 --> 00:33:36.380
And all from that
same flood of persons

00:33:36.380 --> 00:33:39.440
who occupied the world maybe
in all not more than half

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:41.900
a million of them by the time
the Americas were settled.

00:33:41.900 --> 00:33:46.770
And we've grown, then, from that
half million to half trillion,

00:33:46.770 --> 00:33:51.310
a very large number of
powers of 10 in population.

00:33:55.530 --> 00:33:56.690
Thank you.

00:33:56.690 --> 00:34:02.040
Now the linkage between the
craft-- engineering, science,

00:34:02.040 --> 00:34:06.880
art-- between hand and
mind expressed in this way,

00:34:06.880 --> 00:34:11.060
I maintain this is
demonstrably old.

00:34:11.060 --> 00:34:13.810
Indeed, we can see it even
in the beginnings of science,

00:34:13.810 --> 00:34:17.150
in the time of the Enlightenment
in the late Renaissance.

00:34:17.150 --> 00:34:18.850
You couldn't buy
a microscope that

00:34:18.850 --> 00:34:22.540
wasn't worked up and chased
and engraved to bear some art.

00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:24.530
Partly that was style,
I'm not denying it.

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:27.389
Partly that was royal patronage,
I'm not denying that either.

00:34:27.389 --> 00:34:30.850
Nevertheless, the work of
the hand was to be admired.

00:34:30.850 --> 00:34:33.730
And it was rare to have
such skilled craftsmanship

00:34:33.730 --> 00:34:36.199
in these new materials
with a new purpose.

00:34:36.199 --> 00:34:39.489
And to some extent, we've
lost that feeling because

00:34:39.489 --> 00:34:43.350
of a high utilitarian quality
and the already great expense

00:34:43.350 --> 00:34:45.739
of making the complex
instruments, which

00:34:45.739 --> 00:34:50.570
now replace the sledgehammer
of the MIT seal.

00:34:50.570 --> 00:34:52.361
Or augment the sledgehammer.

00:34:52.361 --> 00:34:54.110
I don't want to run
down the sledgehammer.

00:34:54.110 --> 00:34:56.895
Still a very
indispensable instrument.

00:34:56.895 --> 00:34:58.520
What I would like to
emphasize, though,

00:34:58.520 --> 00:35:04.616
is that this connection
is a profound connection.

00:35:04.616 --> 00:35:06.990
And I'd like to describe a
little bit why I think it's so

00:35:06.990 --> 00:35:11.550
and why it parts the
hand/mind relationship

00:35:11.550 --> 00:35:17.660
from a more symbolic one, which
is not absent from either.

00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:19.980
Any of you who have tried
to learn a craft or a skill

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:23.700
or, above all, an art knows
how very close the reaction

00:35:23.700 --> 00:35:26.030
of a hand and the mind
must be to make anything

00:35:26.030 --> 00:35:27.680
work the way you choose.

00:35:27.680 --> 00:35:32.360
And if you don't do that,
another draft is to be made.

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:35.320
Now of course, those
who deal in symbols

00:35:35.320 --> 00:35:38.100
alone have some of
the same quality,

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:40.940
but the hand is
much less present.

00:35:40.940 --> 00:35:43.880
The symbols are, by
nature, more conventional.

00:35:43.880 --> 00:35:47.440
What is characteristic of art
and science and engineering

00:35:47.440 --> 00:35:51.740
together is they represent--
almost always they're

00:35:51.740 --> 00:35:53.700
presented to us, and
they need to be presented

00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:56.190
to us, in concrete form.

00:35:56.190 --> 00:35:58.490
In single concrete examples.

00:35:58.490 --> 00:36:00.720
Perhaps something more
than the poet Blake

00:36:00.720 --> 00:36:03.160
called "minute
particulars," which

00:36:03.160 --> 00:36:06.380
he felt all art and
science were compacted.

00:36:06.380 --> 00:36:08.610
But certainly not generalities.

00:36:08.610 --> 00:36:11.460
Generalities edge towards
philosophical description.

00:36:11.460 --> 00:36:13.270
True, there's a big
domain in between.

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:14.728
There are theorems
and propositions

00:36:14.728 --> 00:36:16.810
that are indispensable
that come out of thought

00:36:16.810 --> 00:36:18.960
and which govern many
of the things we do.

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:21.300
But I think it's fair
to say that works of art

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:24.950
and the data of science
and works of the engineer

00:36:24.950 --> 00:36:26.610
are all concrete examples.

00:36:26.610 --> 00:36:30.730
And if you don't have those, you
have a very dry subject indeed

00:36:30.730 --> 00:36:33.490
that consists of nothing
of theorems about mechanics

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:36.844
without motions or diagrams
or examples or particulars,

00:36:36.844 --> 00:36:38.760
which I suffered through
as a graduate student

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:42.640
once long ago at
another university

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:44.480
when I was studying mechanics.

00:36:44.480 --> 00:36:46.080
We couldn't ask
questions, and we also

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:47.640
couldn't understand
the propositions.

00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:51.840
We sure could learn
the words quite well.

00:36:51.840 --> 00:36:56.495
Now there are
differences, of course.

00:37:01.520 --> 00:37:03.810
I think of the
principal characteristic

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:07.175
of art, which I admit is
somewhat rapidly losing

00:37:07.175 --> 00:37:08.300
that hasn't [? lost ?] yet.

00:37:08.300 --> 00:37:10.280
It can be seen in
almost all parts of it.

00:37:10.280 --> 00:37:13.460
Its products, its
particulars are

00:37:13.460 --> 00:37:16.190
perceptual to the human being.

00:37:16.190 --> 00:37:20.680
It's very hard to imagine art
translated through-- it's not

00:37:20.680 --> 00:37:22.690
impossible, we see
it today-- translated

00:37:22.690 --> 00:37:26.830
through some intermediate
device, which turns it

00:37:26.830 --> 00:37:28.404
into printouts and numbers.

00:37:28.404 --> 00:37:29.570
Then you have to regraph it.

00:37:29.570 --> 00:37:30.640
This is hardly art.

00:37:30.640 --> 00:37:32.680
The immediacy is gone,
though, of course,

00:37:32.680 --> 00:37:34.530
a great deal of the
world of science,

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:36.080
great deal of the
data of science

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:39.120
go beyond human perception,
involve intermediation

00:37:39.120 --> 00:37:41.330
by instrumentation
of complicated kinds

00:37:41.330 --> 00:37:44.110
and auxiliary calculations
to bring together

00:37:44.110 --> 00:37:46.810
the reading of the instruments
or program inside the computer,

00:37:46.810 --> 00:37:48.380
which is doing the same thing.

00:37:48.380 --> 00:37:50.490
And by and large, that's
the biggest distinction

00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:54.790
I can see on the side
of how the work is done.

00:37:54.790 --> 00:37:58.840
It is also true that
they are united in this.

00:37:58.840 --> 00:38:01.860
Niels Bohr defined
science not only as effort

00:38:01.860 --> 00:38:04.980
to order the world, which surely
it is, but there it does not

00:38:04.980 --> 00:38:08.520
deal-- it does not differ from
philosophy or, for that matter,

00:38:08.520 --> 00:38:10.040
from poetry.

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:12.581
But it seeks to bring in
new experience, which again

00:38:12.581 --> 00:38:14.580
is close to art, though
perhaps rather different

00:38:14.580 --> 00:38:16.360
from the philosophers' work.

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:18.330
And if we talk
about engineering,

00:38:18.330 --> 00:38:21.090
I think we have to say to bring
new means for the game you've

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:21.840
experienced.

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:23.240
This too is necessary.

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:25.340
But all of this
definitional style

00:38:25.340 --> 00:38:29.830
would very much connect these
two or three-- it's hard for me

00:38:29.830 --> 00:38:32.140
to think of them as
three-- but certainly

00:38:32.140 --> 00:38:35.680
multiple view of these matters.

00:38:35.680 --> 00:38:38.470
I'd like to go still further
and talk about their internal--

00:38:38.470 --> 00:38:40.310
the way it appears to
practitioners a bit.

00:38:40.310 --> 00:38:42.200
All of you, as students,
were practitioners.

00:38:42.200 --> 00:38:45.370
All of you now are practitioners
in some direction or another.

00:38:45.370 --> 00:38:47.710
And I think you'll
recognize, feel sympathy

00:38:47.710 --> 00:38:49.760
with what I say when
I say that there

00:38:49.760 --> 00:38:53.810
are a couple of
apparently poor divisions,

00:38:53.810 --> 00:38:57.570
contradictions, opposites,
which have to be

00:38:57.570 --> 00:38:59.730
united in each of these things.

00:38:59.730 --> 00:39:06.330
A curious but quite realistic
account of what people do.

00:39:06.330 --> 00:39:10.360
For example, the passive
absorption of information

00:39:10.360 --> 00:39:13.590
to learn a language, to learn
mathematical formulation,

00:39:13.590 --> 00:39:16.970
to learn the use of
a pen or a brush,

00:39:16.970 --> 00:39:20.330
to learn the language of the
stage, whatever it might be.

00:39:20.330 --> 00:39:24.480
To learn the notes of
the scale and the keys.

00:39:24.480 --> 00:39:26.890
All of those things
are done, can

00:39:26.890 --> 00:39:29.220
be done as a passive
game where someone

00:39:29.220 --> 00:39:33.920
feeds the information, a book, a
program, a person, and you gain

00:39:33.920 --> 00:39:34.950
it.

00:39:34.950 --> 00:39:38.040
But of the other side, activity
is indispensable, especially

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:41.180
if you learn those things
with that beautiful word

00:39:41.180 --> 00:39:42.310
productivity.

00:39:42.310 --> 00:39:45.030
So it can again recombine
them for yourselves.

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:48.620
We know people who teach physics
at MIT and everywhere else

00:39:48.620 --> 00:39:51.850
that the solving of problems,
onerous as it may be,

00:39:51.850 --> 00:39:55.390
is a way to secure the active
knowledge of the things

00:39:55.390 --> 00:39:57.440
passively observed,
underlined in

00:39:57.440 --> 00:40:01.267
yellow marker, the big
propositions in the textbook.

00:40:01.267 --> 00:40:02.600
You've got to put them together.

00:40:02.600 --> 00:40:04.415
You've got to make
them work for you.

00:40:04.415 --> 00:40:07.510
You have to feel somewhat
what we say at home with them.

00:40:07.510 --> 00:40:09.830
Until that's happened,
no genuine understanding

00:40:09.830 --> 00:40:10.700
will come in.

00:40:10.700 --> 00:40:15.280
The answering of multiple choice
tests in familiar language,

00:40:15.280 --> 00:40:17.590
choosing the right
one of six, is not

00:40:17.590 --> 00:40:19.800
a very powerful
test for science,

00:40:19.800 --> 00:40:22.114
for engineering, or for art.

00:40:22.114 --> 00:40:23.530
It may be good
enough for learning

00:40:23.530 --> 00:40:26.395
certain received
bodies of truth, fine.

00:40:26.395 --> 00:40:28.180
I'm not against it entirely.

00:40:28.180 --> 00:40:30.710
But it is clearly in our day,
it's a basis of controversy

00:40:30.710 --> 00:40:33.060
and surely overused.

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:35.590
So we have to combine the
passive and the active,

00:40:35.590 --> 00:40:40.500
and activity is the sine qua
non of the hand and the mind.

00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:51.480
The same similar dichotomy
goes between the analytic

00:40:51.480 --> 00:40:54.070
and the synthetic.

00:40:54.070 --> 00:40:56.180
It was said in popular
psychology only a few years

00:40:56.180 --> 00:40:57.960
ago-- it was quite
popular in the magazines--

00:40:57.960 --> 00:41:00.251
there were halves of the
brain, the left and the right.

00:41:00.251 --> 00:41:02.650
And one attended to
the holistic things,

00:41:02.650 --> 00:41:04.540
the organization, the concepts.

00:41:04.540 --> 00:41:10.110
And one attended to the details,
the rigorous, logical thinking.

00:41:10.110 --> 00:41:12.740
One was suitable for
children, one for grownups,

00:41:12.740 --> 00:41:14.200
and that was the general idea.

00:41:14.200 --> 00:41:16.684
People who said that, of
course, were mostly writers.

00:41:16.684 --> 00:41:18.100
They understood
writing very well.

00:41:18.100 --> 00:41:19.840
They knew how hard it was
to formulate good sentences

00:41:19.840 --> 00:41:20.514
and paragraphs.

00:41:20.514 --> 00:41:22.180
And they said, well,
in the kindergarten

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:24.270
they can draw quite
well and make images,

00:41:24.270 --> 00:41:25.600
and that's enough for that.

00:41:25.600 --> 00:41:27.220
Of course, this is a caricature.

00:41:27.220 --> 00:41:28.870
It's a very bad proposition.

00:41:28.870 --> 00:41:31.560
Analytics and synthetic
again are together.

00:41:31.560 --> 00:41:34.410
The analyst indeed
may take things apart.

00:41:34.410 --> 00:41:37.570
But the tools he uses were
themselves synthesized

00:41:37.570 --> 00:41:40.570
by earlier designers
who synthesized

00:41:40.570 --> 00:41:45.400
the scalpel or the method
of least squares, whatever

00:41:45.400 --> 00:41:47.840
it might be, by putting
together something new that

00:41:47.840 --> 00:41:49.380
had not existed before.

00:41:49.380 --> 00:41:53.210
So I reject that as a
harsh division of the world

00:41:53.210 --> 00:41:56.900
and say no, both halves
of the mind are necessary.

00:41:56.900 --> 00:42:00.010
All parts of the
mind, like both hands.

00:42:00.010 --> 00:42:08.660
And the unitarity of this
is visible in the sense

00:42:08.660 --> 00:42:11.700
that we all know that for
every geometrical statement

00:42:11.700 --> 00:42:14.020
in Euclid, an algebraic
equivalent can

00:42:14.020 --> 00:42:16.190
be formed and vice versa.

00:42:16.190 --> 00:42:19.460
The symbols of language, the
symbols of x and y and plus,

00:42:19.460 --> 00:42:22.817
are no more foreign than
the lines and the angles.

00:42:22.817 --> 00:42:24.650
They're just different
ways of presentation,

00:42:24.650 --> 00:42:27.426
and they have a
one-to-one correspondence.

00:42:27.426 --> 00:42:29.086
Of course, the
symbolism of our world

00:42:29.086 --> 00:42:30.460
is so powerful
now it extends, it

00:42:30.460 --> 00:42:32.024
goes beyond what
we can easily draw.

00:42:32.024 --> 00:42:33.190
You can do n dimensionality.

00:42:33.190 --> 00:42:36.150
I accept that and use it daily.

00:42:36.150 --> 00:42:38.870
But this is the issue.

00:42:38.870 --> 00:42:45.330
Now there is a feeling that
there's a difference in tone.

00:42:45.330 --> 00:42:46.724
And there is some difference.

00:42:46.724 --> 00:42:48.640
The scientists and the
engineers seem serious.

00:42:48.640 --> 00:42:56.090
They're concerned with matters
of prosperity and penury

00:42:56.090 --> 00:43:01.700
or sometimes life and death
or sometimes peace and war.

00:43:01.700 --> 00:43:03.630
And this seems more
serious, perhaps,

00:43:03.630 --> 00:43:06.860
than the more playful
activity of the artist.

00:43:06.860 --> 00:43:10.400
But I recommend you, thinking
through our own language,

00:43:10.400 --> 00:43:13.960
the word "play" has
wide connotations.

00:43:13.960 --> 00:43:17.290
The player may play
Medea or Hamlet, hardly

00:43:17.290 --> 00:43:19.970
a lighthearted affair,
something plumbing

00:43:19.970 --> 00:43:22.650
the depths of human behavior.

00:43:22.650 --> 00:43:24.540
Lightness of heart is
not the whole story.

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:27.880
The primary colors and
the cube, the sphere,

00:43:27.880 --> 00:43:30.670
are fine things for
the elementary school,

00:43:30.670 --> 00:43:33.170
indispensable for
the small child.

00:43:33.170 --> 00:43:35.520
But they are not the
whole story of play.

00:43:35.520 --> 00:43:38.710
Play, which is unique
in the vertebrates

00:43:38.710 --> 00:43:43.590
and best developed in those
closest to us, is profound.

00:43:43.590 --> 00:43:46.047
I worked a lot
with Charles Ames,

00:43:46.047 --> 00:43:48.380
who said that one of the most
serious activities in life

00:43:48.380 --> 00:43:52.850
was play, and he demonstrated
it every day by what he did.

00:43:52.850 --> 00:43:56.510
Play is a means of-- clearly
what the word implies--

00:43:56.510 --> 00:43:57.940
model making of the future.

00:43:57.940 --> 00:44:00.010
What would it be
to do like that?

00:44:00.010 --> 00:44:00.980
Not to carry it out?

00:44:00.980 --> 00:44:02.640
Not to make that
full investment?

00:44:02.640 --> 00:44:07.740
But to acquire the mastery in a
more benign, a more acceptable,

00:44:07.740 --> 00:44:09.960
a more easily
entered environment.

00:44:09.960 --> 00:44:11.250
And that is what play is.

00:44:11.250 --> 00:44:17.930
And since you cannot master
the world in a stroke,

00:44:17.930 --> 00:44:21.530
play is an indispensable
feature of human behavior.

00:44:21.530 --> 00:44:25.030
From word play of the
infant and the mother

00:44:25.030 --> 00:44:29.460
in the home to the most
profound thought of an Einstein

00:44:29.460 --> 00:44:35.350
or a composer of great value,
Bach in the course of his work,

00:44:35.350 --> 00:44:38.900
as any exposure to
art demonstrates,

00:44:38.900 --> 00:44:41.860
these things have and the
scientists too must have it.

00:44:41.860 --> 00:44:45.000
I'd like to say a word for
playful engineering, which

00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:47.460
I think is an important
issue of our time

00:44:47.460 --> 00:44:52.050
that we have not
yet fully engaged.

00:44:52.050 --> 00:44:53.130
It's not new.

00:44:53.130 --> 00:44:55.710
There always has
been such a thing.

00:44:55.710 --> 00:44:59.351
I'd like to show an
example of it in a slide.

00:44:59.351 --> 00:45:00.850
I hope you can
recognize this slide.

00:45:00.850 --> 00:45:02.115
It's a very unusual slide.

00:45:02.115 --> 00:45:04.360
It was made in 1936.

00:45:04.360 --> 00:45:06.620
It shows the greatest--
one of the greatest--

00:45:06.620 --> 00:45:09.910
works of playful engineering,
the Eiffel Tower.

00:45:09.910 --> 00:45:13.504
You can perhaps recognize the
Avenue [INAUDIBLE] down here

00:45:13.504 --> 00:45:14.920
and the arches of
the Eiffel Tower

00:45:14.920 --> 00:45:20.810
there, all heavily illuminated
in this time exposure from 1936

00:45:20.810 --> 00:45:22.300
where, of course,
what you see is

00:45:22.300 --> 00:45:25.410
a pyrotechnic display,
fireworks, off all

00:45:25.410 --> 00:45:26.997
parts of the Eiffel Tower.

00:45:26.997 --> 00:45:29.080
But of course, the Eiffel
Tower was built strictly

00:45:29.080 --> 00:45:32.640
as a playful operation,
a decoration for Paris

00:45:32.640 --> 00:45:38.300
for the 1889 World's Fair there
by a great structural engineer.

00:45:38.300 --> 00:45:41.560
It worked 10,000 tons of
wrought iron, not of steel,

00:45:41.560 --> 00:45:43.290
of wrought iron and rivets.

00:45:43.290 --> 00:45:47.190
And made playfully.

00:45:47.190 --> 00:45:50.830
Of course, managing to extract
a fee from the people who

00:45:50.830 --> 00:45:53.030
come so it can keep going,
keep itself painted,

00:45:53.030 --> 00:45:54.800
and, indeed, pay the
shareholders quite

00:45:54.800 --> 00:45:57.300
a considerable income, I
understand, over the years.

00:45:57.300 --> 00:46:00.510
But nevertheless,
that's its foundation.

00:46:00.510 --> 00:46:04.660
It doesn't do anything but
stand there magnificently.

00:46:04.660 --> 00:46:07.290
At first, of course, attacked
by half the artists and writers

00:46:07.290 --> 00:46:13.390
in Paris and now inseparable
from the nature of the city.

00:46:13.390 --> 00:46:16.050
And I'm sure that you could
not possibly tear it down

00:46:16.050 --> 00:46:19.270
without a major
revolution swirling

00:46:19.270 --> 00:46:22.340
about the base of the tower.

00:46:22.340 --> 00:46:26.390
Now playful engineering is not
enough practiced in our time.

00:46:26.390 --> 00:46:27.295
It is practiced some.

00:46:27.295 --> 00:46:28.170
We all know about it.

00:46:28.170 --> 00:46:31.130
We know about it in sports,
where it's become a specialty

00:46:31.130 --> 00:46:32.610
and almost lost
its playful quality

00:46:32.610 --> 00:46:35.680
because of its heavy rewards.

00:46:35.680 --> 00:46:42.460
And at MIT, from the impromptu
and very lighthearted hack,

00:46:42.460 --> 00:46:46.090
of which I need not
give you any examples,

00:46:46.090 --> 00:46:49.950
through the pedal-driven
aircraft of great renown.

00:46:49.950 --> 00:46:52.980
To the wonderful stroboscopic
work of Papa Flash,

00:46:52.980 --> 00:46:54.970
the late Doc Edgerton.

00:46:54.970 --> 00:46:59.000
To the sophisticated work,
whose 25th anniversary

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:03.510
is being celebrated tomorrow at
the Center of Advanced Visual

00:47:03.510 --> 00:47:05.960
Studies, which I remember
for sort of three

00:47:05.960 --> 00:47:08.570
or four works of grand
works, not single

00:47:08.570 --> 00:47:12.640
works, but whole campaigns of
playful engineering, mechanisms

00:47:12.640 --> 00:47:15.820
of extraordinary
wit and pleasure.

00:47:15.820 --> 00:47:18.570
The use of aerial
photography as an art form,

00:47:18.570 --> 00:47:21.740
not as a geodetic study or
a way of measuring crops.

00:47:21.740 --> 00:47:26.960
And sky art, lasers and balloons
on grand scale just beginning

00:47:26.960 --> 00:47:30.110
in this world in
the last few years.

00:47:30.110 --> 00:47:33.420
And I'm quite sure also
that the Palais du Louvre

00:47:33.420 --> 00:47:37.600
has been recently subjected to
something which is architecture

00:47:37.600 --> 00:47:41.610
and playful in its qualities
and at the same time profoundly

00:47:41.610 --> 00:47:42.570
purposeful.

00:47:42.570 --> 00:47:47.660
And I'm sure that IM Pei and
Dean Mitchell will describe

00:47:47.660 --> 00:47:50.770
something about that in
their talk of recent work

00:47:50.770 --> 00:47:53.140
just to come.

00:47:53.140 --> 00:47:55.980
Now I'm going to
close, and I want

00:47:55.980 --> 00:48:03.530
to close with a somewhat novel
end, which I had not really

00:48:03.530 --> 00:48:05.610
planned but it was
forced out of me as I

00:48:05.610 --> 00:48:08.680
went through these thoughts
in the last few weeks.

00:48:08.680 --> 00:48:10.980
I see one danger.

00:48:10.980 --> 00:48:13.630
The danger is an
extraordinarily clear one,

00:48:13.630 --> 00:48:16.310
and I think we have found
the best way to avoid it.

00:48:16.310 --> 00:48:19.284
I'll tell you what that is
and see if you agree with me.

00:48:19.284 --> 00:48:20.200
Please think about it.

00:48:20.200 --> 00:48:21.400
It's something novel.

00:48:21.400 --> 00:48:23.520
It's something of great
importance to all of us.

00:48:23.520 --> 00:48:27.930
And it needs
widespread attention.

00:48:27.930 --> 00:48:31.960
And that is, it's
clear that nowadays art

00:48:31.960 --> 00:48:35.000
produces its own truths.

00:48:35.000 --> 00:48:42.720
The artwork is not judged by its
comparison with another canon,

00:48:42.720 --> 00:48:44.090
necessarily.

00:48:44.090 --> 00:48:47.080
Yes, it might be of the School
of Beaux-Art, it might not.

00:48:47.080 --> 00:48:48.520
The schools are various.

00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:50.570
The techniques are innumerable.

00:48:50.570 --> 00:48:52.460
The subject matter,
the minute particulars,

00:48:52.460 --> 00:48:53.931
are as wide as can be.

00:48:53.931 --> 00:48:55.930
And I think we should
accept that as the freedom

00:48:55.930 --> 00:48:57.336
that the artist has.

00:48:57.336 --> 00:48:58.960
Of course, he may
follow a strict form.

00:48:58.960 --> 00:48:59.918
He may wish to do that.

00:48:59.918 --> 00:49:02.400
Or he may break loose
into something quite new.

00:49:02.400 --> 00:49:04.640
But in any case, there's
no other criteria,

00:49:04.640 --> 00:49:07.240
no external one,
the scientist must--

00:49:07.240 --> 00:49:08.480
the input is what counts.

00:49:08.480 --> 00:49:10.940
The data must be
reliably presented.

00:49:10.940 --> 00:49:12.890
The engineer, the
object must work

00:49:12.890 --> 00:49:14.650
for the purpose of
which it was intended

00:49:14.650 --> 00:49:16.720
or some purpose close to that.

00:49:16.720 --> 00:49:19.390
But the artist, it is
the output that counts.

00:49:19.390 --> 00:49:21.410
Does it work for the
purpose he intended it,

00:49:21.410 --> 00:49:24.072
to amuse, to entertain, to
teach, whatever it might be?

00:49:24.072 --> 00:49:25.530
It doesn't matter
how he got there.

00:49:25.530 --> 00:49:29.380
He isn't responsible to show
you the data on which he worked.

00:49:29.380 --> 00:49:31.930
And on the other hand, this
leads to a great danger,

00:49:31.930 --> 00:49:33.640
of which a very tiny example.

00:49:33.640 --> 00:49:36.160
My wife, Phyllis Morrison,
has helped me produce

00:49:36.160 --> 00:49:39.320
for the next, last slide.

00:49:39.320 --> 00:49:42.550
Well, it's the Eiffel
Tower again, right?

00:49:42.550 --> 00:49:44.560
In fact, it's the
same-- you see it's

00:49:44.560 --> 00:49:46.580
the same pyrotechnic picture.

00:49:46.580 --> 00:49:50.710
But spontaneously, with the
matter of a few seconds,

00:49:50.710 --> 00:49:54.020
Phyllis has managed to
give the kind of impression

00:49:54.020 --> 00:49:56.770
that there's a colorful
pyrotechnic display coming

00:49:56.770 --> 00:49:58.410
from the great tower.

00:49:58.410 --> 00:50:00.835
And of course, you are
all well sophisticated

00:50:00.835 --> 00:50:02.280
enough for the
images and with now

00:50:02.280 --> 00:50:04.209
markers to recognize
that of course that's

00:50:04.209 --> 00:50:06.000
nothing to do with the
Eiffel Tower at all.

00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:07.265
What does it have to do with?

00:50:07.265 --> 00:50:10.060
It has to do with a
35-millimeter slide that

00:50:10.060 --> 00:50:13.530
represented the Eiffel Tower
in 1936, which she has then

00:50:13.530 --> 00:50:18.960
modified with just a very
small, spontaneous artistic work

00:50:18.960 --> 00:50:20.600
to make the point here.

00:50:20.600 --> 00:50:23.010
The image, however, is
itself, it is there,

00:50:23.010 --> 00:50:25.970
and it means something that
is actually quite striking.

00:50:25.970 --> 00:50:29.000
And now the trouble is, we
make images so well today.

00:50:29.000 --> 00:50:30.180
We make so many.

00:50:30.180 --> 00:50:32.130
We can make them so seamlessly.

00:50:32.130 --> 00:50:35.380
We can change their appearance
in every way, not only

00:50:35.380 --> 00:50:38.195
visual images but even, speaking
more metaphorically, auditory

00:50:38.195 --> 00:50:43.190
and the rest, that there is
a cut-off between the world

00:50:43.190 --> 00:50:45.295
represented, may
not be represented,

00:50:45.295 --> 00:50:47.990
but even those things
when they are represented,

00:50:47.990 --> 00:50:50.191
well represented of the
image, are no longer

00:50:50.191 --> 00:50:51.190
good maps of each other.

00:50:51.190 --> 00:50:52.940
The artist's intervention
is so powerful

00:50:52.940 --> 00:50:55.510
that he has ended that.

00:50:55.510 --> 00:50:57.690
Now that is not new to us.

00:50:57.690 --> 00:51:00.440
We all experience it
internally, and it

00:51:00.440 --> 00:51:03.120
is probably indispensable to
the human mind and the way

00:51:03.120 --> 00:51:04.650
we have evolved.

00:51:04.650 --> 00:51:07.680
It is what we call
the world of dream.

00:51:07.680 --> 00:51:10.600
In dream, visual
images are produced,

00:51:10.600 --> 00:51:14.470
which have maybe some
semblance of your experience,

00:51:14.470 --> 00:51:16.010
sometimes not perhaps.

00:51:16.010 --> 00:51:18.520
But when they have
semblance, we know very well

00:51:18.520 --> 00:51:24.280
that those dreams are not the
relatively close approximations

00:51:24.280 --> 00:51:27.470
to what is going on, that the
eyes, which are always limited,

00:51:27.470 --> 00:51:31.110
and the video screen, which is
equally and similarly limited,

00:51:31.110 --> 00:51:34.990
bring to you approximations
of the real world.

00:51:34.990 --> 00:51:37.130
Now what I am trying
to say is a danger

00:51:37.130 --> 00:51:41.160
that, if we have, as we do
have, the growth of the greatest

00:51:41.160 --> 00:51:43.720
20th century arts, the one
that attracts most people,

00:51:43.720 --> 00:51:46.100
and I think has gradually,
gradually increased

00:51:46.100 --> 00:51:48.640
its salience is the cinema.

00:51:48.640 --> 00:51:51.270
And I strongly suspect
the 21st century

00:51:51.270 --> 00:51:54.430
will make the video equivalent
of the cinema, with its music,

00:51:54.430 --> 00:51:57.450
with its changes, with
its probable 3D nature,

00:51:57.450 --> 00:52:01.140
and its motions and change,
will probably transcend

00:52:01.140 --> 00:52:05.510
all previous visual experiences,
will make an enormous push

00:52:05.510 --> 00:52:06.491
upon the world.

00:52:06.491 --> 00:52:07.865
Whereas the capital
of this world

00:52:07.865 --> 00:52:10.920
is only going to double
or triple and plateau out.

00:52:10.920 --> 00:52:12.350
There isn't room for more.

00:52:12.350 --> 00:52:14.600
There's not room in
the atmospheric balance

00:52:14.600 --> 00:52:16.630
and the heat sources for more.

00:52:16.630 --> 00:52:19.370
These sources of information
and image are enormous,

00:52:19.370 --> 00:52:20.980
and they will continue.

00:52:20.980 --> 00:52:25.810
And the real question is that we
not be swamped in a dream world

00:52:25.810 --> 00:52:30.020
where we no longer pay attention
to what the representation was

00:52:30.020 --> 00:52:32.750
of but only the nature of
the representation itself.

00:52:32.750 --> 00:52:34.540
That is, after all,
the artist's task.

00:52:34.540 --> 00:52:38.090
And I cannot think of a better
way of understanding that

00:52:38.090 --> 00:52:41.770
problem, of conquering it,
than by having people know both

00:52:41.770 --> 00:52:45.020
sides of the operation, who
have studied the arts to see how

00:52:45.020 --> 00:52:48.230
much you can do to make
entertaining and wonderful

00:52:48.230 --> 00:52:51.540
things that didn't exist,
but also how much you can do

00:52:51.540 --> 00:52:54.840
to represent scrupulously
and meticulously the data

00:52:54.840 --> 00:52:59.320
of the real world, the actual
performance of engineering

00:52:59.320 --> 00:53:03.420
without hyperbole and public
relations and the rest.

00:53:03.420 --> 00:53:05.250
To take only one
side, well, stay away

00:53:05.250 --> 00:53:07.700
from the other,
that will not work

00:53:07.700 --> 00:53:10.970
because people are too clever
and they'll transcend it.

00:53:10.970 --> 00:53:14.440
What is the best is to expose
them both, come right up front

00:53:14.440 --> 00:53:16.740
and say, yes, this can be done.

00:53:16.740 --> 00:53:20.680
The artist has one direction,
the engineer scientist

00:53:20.680 --> 00:53:21.600
has another.

00:53:21.600 --> 00:53:24.780
Both recognize their
enormous similarity.

00:53:24.780 --> 00:53:28.230
But by their mutual
understanding,

00:53:28.230 --> 00:53:32.330
we can have a world which both
contains dreams but is not

00:53:32.330 --> 00:53:33.744
solely based on it.

00:53:33.744 --> 00:53:36.410
And I think that's the only kind
of a world which is safe for us

00:53:36.410 --> 00:53:41.057
to try to carry on the legacy
of the canyon at Olduvai.

00:53:41.057 --> 00:53:42.051
Thank you.

00:53:42.051 --> 00:53:45.530
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:16.110 --> 00:54:17.360
HARRIS: Can I help in any way?

00:54:17.360 --> 00:54:19.026
MORRISON: I think
just by holding there.

00:54:33.400 --> 00:54:36.900
HARRIS: Well, I thought I was
being really smart to take back

00:54:36.900 --> 00:54:39.760
the relationship
between mens et manus,

00:54:39.760 --> 00:54:44.670
mind and hand, to the
establishment of MIT.

00:54:44.670 --> 00:54:48.520
But as has happened to me
many times since coming here

00:54:48.520 --> 00:54:53.330
five years ago, I was put to
shame by one of my colleagues

00:54:53.330 --> 00:54:58.490
who has now taken it back
tens of thousands of years

00:54:58.490 --> 00:55:01.640
and has put that relationship
into the perspective

00:55:01.640 --> 00:55:03.420
that we must view it.

00:55:03.420 --> 00:55:07.990
And has not only taken it back
tens of thousands of years,

00:55:07.990 --> 00:55:13.510
but has told us and
warned us of the future

00:55:13.510 --> 00:55:16.060
and of the continuing
importance of the relationship.

00:55:16.060 --> 00:55:18.976
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:34.070 --> 00:55:37.740
May we remember Professor
Morrison's words

00:55:37.740 --> 00:55:41.460
and continue to pursue playful
engineering and serious art.

00:55:46.300 --> 00:55:51.020
Our next speakers
will in fact talk

00:55:51.020 --> 00:55:55.390
about a specific relationship
between mens et manus,

00:55:55.390 --> 00:55:59.730
between art and engineering
in the world of architecture.

00:55:59.730 --> 00:56:01.580
We are very honored
today to have

00:56:01.580 --> 00:56:06.720
with us architect IM
Pei, class of 1940,

00:56:06.720 --> 00:56:10.960
formally with the firm of
Pei Cobb Freed &amp; Partners.

00:56:10.960 --> 00:56:16.680
Of course, Mr. Pei is very well
known for many, many buildings,

00:56:16.680 --> 00:56:20.150
and I will not list them all.

00:56:20.150 --> 00:56:24.070
Some of them are particularly
important for the arts,

00:56:24.070 --> 00:56:27.010
including, of course,
the Louvre, the East

00:56:27.010 --> 00:56:30.690
Wing of the National
Gallery in Washington, DC.

00:56:30.690 --> 00:56:34.510
And many MIT alums are familiar
particularly with the Morrison

00:56:34.510 --> 00:56:37.220
Hall for the Dallas
Symphony Orchestra,

00:56:37.220 --> 00:56:40.120
a remarkable building.

00:56:40.120 --> 00:56:45.010
At MIT, Mr. Pei has not only
designed the Wiesner Building

00:56:45.010 --> 00:56:49.650
on Ames Street but the arch
to that building, which

00:56:49.650 --> 00:56:53.880
is a gateway into East Campus
as you cross Ames Street.

00:56:53.880 --> 00:56:57.270
Also, not, I think,
coincidentally,

00:56:57.270 --> 00:57:00.010
when you are walking
toward the campus,

00:57:00.010 --> 00:57:05.670
frame his three buildings
in the main quadrangle

00:57:05.670 --> 00:57:10.370
with, in perspective,
the MIT Dome overall.

00:57:10.370 --> 00:57:12.210
The three buildings
on the main campus

00:57:12.210 --> 00:57:16.130
with their very stark geometry
of verticals and horizontals,

00:57:16.130 --> 00:57:19.490
triangles and rectangles.

00:57:19.490 --> 00:57:22.150
Most recently, or
not most recently,

00:57:22.150 --> 00:57:25.760
but among Mr. Pei's
most recent works

00:57:25.760 --> 00:57:28.070
are the Bank of
China in Hong Kong

00:57:28.070 --> 00:57:31.100
and the Four Seasons
Hotel in New York City.

00:57:31.100 --> 00:57:34.200
And I think we will be hearing
about some of these works

00:57:34.200 --> 00:57:35.380
today.

00:57:35.380 --> 00:57:40.070
Mr. Pei has also been
affiliated with MIT since 1972

00:57:40.070 --> 00:57:43.740
as a member of the Council
for the Arts at MIT.

00:57:43.740 --> 00:57:49.090
And we appreciate his years
of membership on that group.

00:57:49.090 --> 00:57:55.040
Talking to Mr. Pei today will
be Dean William Mitchell.

00:57:55.040 --> 00:57:59.590
Dean Mitchell has been
at MIT since 1992,

00:57:59.590 --> 00:58:01.420
and he is particularly
well known

00:58:01.420 --> 00:58:03.890
for his book, published
by the MIT Press,

00:58:03.890 --> 00:58:08.390
The Reconfigured Eye, which
deals with digital photography

00:58:08.390 --> 00:58:12.460
and in fact visual
images that do not

00:58:12.460 --> 00:58:14.090
represent the real world.

00:58:17.580 --> 00:58:19.740
Dean Mitchell is
working very hard

00:58:19.740 --> 00:58:22.970
in the School of Architecture to
lead the School of Architecture

00:58:22.970 --> 00:58:27.710
into the 21st century with
his virtual design studios

00:58:27.710 --> 00:58:30.660
and the design studios of
the future using computer

00:58:30.660 --> 00:58:33.910
technology to create
a seamless process

00:58:33.910 --> 00:58:37.240
from the initial designs
through to the communications

00:58:37.240 --> 00:58:40.000
with clients,
engineers, and others.

00:58:40.000 --> 00:58:44.457
Please help me to welcome IM
Pei and Dean William Mitchell.

00:58:44.457 --> 00:58:47.936
[APPLAUSE]

00:59:19.870 --> 00:59:23.349
MITCHELL: Well, it's truly
a wonderful privilege

00:59:23.349 --> 00:59:28.319
to have you here and talk
about architecture with IM Pei.

00:59:28.319 --> 00:59:33.289
We're going to [INAUDIBLE]
we're going to after a while.

00:59:33.289 --> 00:59:36.768
And then if there are
questions from the audience,

00:59:36.768 --> 00:59:39.750
we will be delighted to
take them. [INAUDIBLE]

00:59:39.750 --> 00:59:41.738
conversation ongoing.

00:59:41.738 --> 00:59:51.181
Now when we were talking
about [INAUDIBLE]

00:59:51.181 --> 00:59:59.630
So we decided we would be
[INAUDIBLE] that project

00:59:59.630 --> 01:00:01.618
was unfolded over
a number of years

01:00:01.618 --> 01:00:03.606
before the Louvre in Paris.

01:00:03.606 --> 01:00:10.564
Now I think everybody who
knows the arts at all knows

01:00:10.564 --> 01:00:15.034
that at the Louvre, IM Pei
created an extraordinarily

01:00:15.034 --> 01:00:15.534
[INAUDIBLE]

01:00:23.683 --> 01:00:25.474
CREW: Should we just
walk them out or what?

01:00:25.474 --> 01:00:33.426
MITCHELL: What made [INAUDIBLE]
so obvious [INAUDIBLE]

01:00:33.426 --> 01:00:36.408
extraordinarily large
and complex [INAUDIBLE].

01:00:36.408 --> 01:00:39.208
Redoing the Louvre is in fact--

01:00:39.208 --> 01:00:41.688
CREW: Excuse me, can I
just place this microphone?

01:00:41.688 --> 01:00:44.168
[APPLAUSE]

01:00:47.606 --> 01:00:49.564
MITCHELL: Could anybody
hear what I was saying?

01:00:49.564 --> 01:00:50.421
AUDIENCE: No.

01:00:50.421 --> 01:00:51.046
MITCHELL: Okay.

01:00:56.400 --> 01:01:01.630
Redoing the Louvre is--
this sounds much better.

01:01:01.630 --> 01:01:04.930
Redoing the Louvre
is an extraordinarily

01:01:04.930 --> 01:01:09.430
large and complex project
involving technical problems,

01:01:09.430 --> 01:01:13.440
organizational problems,
political problems, certainly.

01:01:13.440 --> 01:01:16.530
Problems of very complex
cultural symbolism.

01:01:16.530 --> 01:01:19.100
So what we thought
we'd do today is

01:01:19.100 --> 01:01:24.020
to ask Mr. Pei to describe
a little bit of his thought

01:01:24.020 --> 01:01:27.960
processes, what the problem
is about, the solution that he

01:01:27.960 --> 01:01:30.780
finally derived.

01:01:30.780 --> 01:01:34.740
And then we'll take some
time to discuss this truly

01:01:34.740 --> 01:01:35.920
magnificent project.

01:01:35.920 --> 01:01:39.370
So Mr. Pei.

01:01:39.370 --> 01:01:44.570
PEI: Professor Morrison talked
about hundreds of centuries.

01:01:44.570 --> 01:01:48.030
I'm talking today on the
subject of the Louvre

01:01:48.030 --> 01:01:53.100
about eight centuries,
the history of 800 years.

01:01:53.100 --> 01:01:57.480
But these are very important 800
years, for the French at least.

01:01:57.480 --> 01:02:00.270
[LAUGHTER]

01:02:04.000 --> 01:02:07.970
It's the building of the period
during which French nation was

01:02:07.970 --> 01:02:10.920
born.

01:02:10.920 --> 01:02:17.950
It's a period that I would
say is roughly parallel,

01:02:17.950 --> 01:02:21.250
the recent French history.

01:02:21.250 --> 01:02:27.370
In fact, it's also a wonderful
symbol, for them at least,

01:02:27.370 --> 01:02:29.910
of French civilization.

01:02:29.910 --> 01:02:35.340
So therefore, the problem of
the Louvre for the architect

01:02:35.340 --> 01:02:41.030
is not just a technical
problem, architectural problem.

01:02:41.030 --> 01:02:45.025
But it's a problem that
has many, many challenges.

01:02:47.630 --> 01:02:50.320
I would like to start
off by telling you

01:02:50.320 --> 01:02:52.984
something about the
history of the Louvre.

01:02:52.984 --> 01:02:54.650
Now I don't have too
much time, but I'll

01:02:54.650 --> 01:02:58.570
try to make it very brief
and therefore incomplete.

01:02:58.570 --> 01:03:04.380
It started in 1202 by I
think you can call him

01:03:04.380 --> 01:03:07.320
a French king,
Philippe Auguste, who

01:03:07.320 --> 01:03:12.720
built this fortress on the
right side of the Seine

01:03:12.720 --> 01:03:16.770
to protect the Ile-de-France,
which is now the Ile de la

01:03:16.770 --> 01:03:20.980
Cite, which is the place
where the French, the heart

01:03:20.980 --> 01:03:27.330
of French life at that
time in Paris took place.

01:03:27.330 --> 01:03:31.440
It was built as a fortress,
la dungeon, as they call it.

01:03:31.440 --> 01:03:33.950
And it's important
because I wish

01:03:33.950 --> 01:03:37.770
I had taken some slides to
show you what it is like today.

01:03:37.770 --> 01:03:41.900
But anyway, it was
a wonderful building

01:03:41.900 --> 01:03:47.610
where they put prisoners,
where they put ammunition.

01:03:47.610 --> 01:03:50.497
It's a very important
place to protect,

01:03:50.497 --> 01:03:52.205
let's say, something
that they considered

01:03:52.205 --> 01:03:54.210
to be very important.

01:03:54.210 --> 01:03:59.810
It lasted as a dungeon or
as a fortress for roughly,

01:03:59.810 --> 01:04:03.050
I would say, maybe 200 years.

01:04:03.050 --> 01:04:07.180
It did not become really
a place for the kings

01:04:07.180 --> 01:04:11.760
to live in until about the
end of the 14th century.

01:04:11.760 --> 01:04:14.750
And I think Charles
V was the king

01:04:14.750 --> 01:04:19.450
that one should remember because
that's the beginning of Louvre.

01:04:19.450 --> 01:04:21.670
Not only as a place
for the king to live in

01:04:21.670 --> 01:04:25.940
but also the beginning
of French art.

01:04:25.940 --> 01:04:28.590
The first library
of France was there

01:04:28.590 --> 01:04:32.760
and the collection of many,
many objects of Charles V

01:04:32.760 --> 01:04:35.630
was displayed there.

01:04:35.630 --> 01:04:39.590
Now for how many
years since then?

01:04:39.590 --> 01:04:44.590
1400 to today, I would
say, 500 years, 600 years.

01:04:47.175 --> 01:04:48.800
Nearly all of the
French kings that you

01:04:48.800 --> 01:04:53.950
know of of any importance have
either lived there, died there,

01:04:53.950 --> 01:05:00.010
married there, and born there.

01:05:00.010 --> 01:05:02.960
So therefore it's
an important place.

01:05:02.960 --> 01:05:05.790
So therefore this is
not really surprising

01:05:05.790 --> 01:05:11.700
that when someone
like myself-- I

01:05:11.700 --> 01:05:17.220
guess I can consider myself an
American of Chinese descent--

01:05:17.220 --> 01:05:20.490
asked to tamper with
a very important part

01:05:20.490 --> 01:05:22.530
of French history.

01:05:22.530 --> 01:05:25.890
So I'm going to go
through this in two phases

01:05:25.890 --> 01:05:27.820
to show you what I went through.

01:05:30.570 --> 01:05:36.130
Phase one lasted five years,
from 1983 to 19-- six years,

01:05:36.130 --> 01:05:38.720
to 1989.

01:05:38.720 --> 01:05:41.980
And during those
six years the plan

01:05:41.980 --> 01:05:45.760
was laid, how to deal
with the problem as a very

01:05:45.760 --> 01:05:46.545
complex problem.

01:05:49.250 --> 01:05:54.790
And the pyramid was built. But
before the pyramid was built,

01:05:54.790 --> 01:05:55.970
we had a lot of trouble.

01:05:55.970 --> 01:05:59.470
We wasted two years.

01:05:59.470 --> 01:06:03.060
It's the media that I had
to deal with at that time,

01:06:03.060 --> 01:06:05.050
and I was totally unprepared.

01:06:05.050 --> 01:06:08.700
And my French language is just
not adequate for that purpose.

01:06:08.700 --> 01:06:10.350
But I had to deal
with the media to try

01:06:10.350 --> 01:06:16.130
to convince the French that
this was the right thing to do.

01:06:16.130 --> 01:06:19.190
So therefore, even though
it took six years, but only

01:06:19.190 --> 01:06:21.180
four of those six
years were devoted

01:06:21.180 --> 01:06:26.710
to architecture and the
building of the phase one.

01:06:26.710 --> 01:06:30.490
Now phase two is less
spectacular in the sense

01:06:30.490 --> 01:06:33.670
of public information.

01:06:33.670 --> 01:06:36.770
But it's perhaps the
more important of the two

01:06:36.770 --> 01:06:42.080
because it completed a
wing called Richelieu.

01:06:42.080 --> 01:06:45.610
And without that wing, there
would be no Louvre today.

01:06:45.610 --> 01:06:50.860
And we couldn't get
that wing until 1985.

01:06:50.860 --> 01:06:53.120
The wing, I'll tell
you what the wing is.

01:06:53.120 --> 01:06:56.890
The wing was occupied by
the Ministry of Finance ever

01:06:56.890 --> 01:06:59.290
since, oh, I would
say, Napoleon III.

01:07:03.810 --> 01:07:07.210
Only 200 years, 150 years.

01:07:07.210 --> 01:07:13.680
No, less, 1856, 150 years, yes.

01:07:13.680 --> 01:07:14.810
And they refused to move.

01:07:19.990 --> 01:07:22.820
And at that time the pyramid
was already discussed

01:07:22.820 --> 01:07:26.080
and some people would say,
okay, if you must have it.

01:07:26.080 --> 01:07:27.500
Mitterrand must want it.

01:07:27.500 --> 01:07:28.700
Okay.

01:07:28.700 --> 01:07:29.990
But I said, look here.

01:07:29.990 --> 01:07:32.450
There's no point to build
the pyramid if we don't

01:07:32.450 --> 01:07:35.070
have the Ministry of Finance.

01:07:35.070 --> 01:07:37.230
So that gave the president
another headache.

01:07:41.460 --> 01:07:44.000
But a long story short.

01:07:44.000 --> 01:07:48.880
We moved them out in 1989.

01:07:48.880 --> 01:07:53.390
So the second phase is the
building of the Richelieu Wing.

01:07:53.390 --> 01:07:56.060
It's architecturally not
at all spectacular but very

01:07:56.060 --> 01:07:57.190
important because why?

01:07:57.190 --> 01:07:59.300
Because I had to
keep the facade.

01:07:59.300 --> 01:08:01.600
Only thing I could do is inside.

01:08:01.600 --> 01:08:03.430
And we demolished
everything inside

01:08:03.430 --> 01:08:07.300
except a big suite
of Second Empire

01:08:07.300 --> 01:08:10.700
rooms, very beautiful rooms--
you must go and see it--

01:08:10.700 --> 01:08:13.500
and two or three staircases.

01:08:13.500 --> 01:08:16.779
And the rest of Richelieu
was completely gutted.

01:08:16.779 --> 01:08:19.529
So it was a major
engineering project,

01:08:19.529 --> 01:08:21.934
but not very
spectacular except there

01:08:21.934 --> 01:08:23.939
are some interesting
things inside, which

01:08:23.939 --> 01:08:26.229
you will see when you come.

01:08:26.229 --> 01:08:31.472
So because of time, I'm going
to start with phase one quickly.

01:08:31.472 --> 01:08:33.180
MITCHELL: Do you have
your slide changer?

01:08:33.180 --> 01:08:33.938
PEI: Oh, yes.

01:08:33.938 --> 01:08:35.729
I'm going to have to
learn how to use this.

01:08:35.729 --> 01:08:37.214
MITCHELL: That's the top there.

01:08:40.679 --> 01:08:41.180
PEI: No.

01:08:41.180 --> 01:08:42.007
Oh, yeah.

01:08:42.007 --> 01:08:43.756
MITCHELL: And this one
for the other side.

01:08:47.580 --> 01:08:48.753
PEI: Excuse me.

01:08:48.753 --> 01:08:52.189
MITCHELL: We have to sort
out the technology here.

01:08:52.189 --> 01:08:53.910
PEI: Oh.

01:08:53.910 --> 01:08:54.443
Oh, I see.

01:08:54.443 --> 01:08:55.359
MITCHELL: There we go.

01:08:55.359 --> 01:08:55.760
Right.

01:08:55.760 --> 01:08:56.439
PEI: Oh, I see.

01:08:56.439 --> 01:08:57.291
Now I understand.

01:08:57.291 --> 01:08:57.790
Okay.

01:09:01.410 --> 01:09:08.340
Now Louvre as you
know it, perhaps

01:09:08.340 --> 01:09:11.450
without too much debate among
architects and planners,

01:09:11.450 --> 01:09:15.970
is perhaps the most important
urban composition in the world

01:09:15.970 --> 01:09:16.470
today.

01:09:19.880 --> 01:09:22.930
There's no other that
can compare with it.

01:09:22.930 --> 01:09:25.779
Its history, 800
years, in the building.

01:09:25.779 --> 01:09:27.790
It started as a
fortress and then was

01:09:27.790 --> 01:09:30.540
added on by kings after kings.

01:09:30.540 --> 01:09:32.000
But the important
kings to remember

01:09:32.000 --> 01:09:34.850
that built the roof are
probably, I would say,

01:09:34.850 --> 01:09:41.340
Charles V, Francis I, Henry
II, Henry IV, Louis XIII,

01:09:41.340 --> 01:09:47.620
Louis XIV, and eventually
Napoleon I and Napoleon III.

01:09:47.620 --> 01:09:50.560
And there are many other kings
in between that added something

01:09:50.560 --> 01:09:53.720
to it, but those were
the important kings.

01:09:53.720 --> 01:09:56.240
And it can also be
said that perhaps

01:09:56.240 --> 01:09:59.190
every important French
architect, nearly every,

01:09:59.190 --> 01:10:06.150
from Lescot to Mansart
to Le Vau and Louis

01:10:06.150 --> 01:10:11.430
XIV, many, many others.

01:10:11.430 --> 01:10:14.440
And then eventually
[INAUDIBLE], they all

01:10:14.440 --> 01:10:16.570
participated in
the building of it.

01:10:16.570 --> 01:10:18.410
So therefore it's not
surprising that you

01:10:18.410 --> 01:10:20.880
come into this
wonderful complex, which

01:10:20.880 --> 01:10:24.970
was already formed, and to
try to do something with it.

01:10:24.970 --> 01:10:27.800
Now the reasons why something
had to be done to the Louvre

01:10:27.800 --> 01:10:29.890
is for the reason
that you know, that it

01:10:29.890 --> 01:10:32.300
was built first as a fortress.

01:10:32.300 --> 01:10:35.170
And it was added on
and added on to try

01:10:35.170 --> 01:10:39.690
to make it more comfortable
for king after king.

01:10:39.690 --> 01:10:44.030
And to turn it into a museum
in 1793 at a convention

01:10:44.030 --> 01:10:46.740
is a move that was
correct, interestingly,

01:10:46.740 --> 01:10:50.750
because Louvre became a public
museum for the first time 200

01:10:50.750 --> 01:10:53.260
years ago.

01:10:53.260 --> 01:10:56.360
But it was not at all
suitable for a museum

01:10:56.360 --> 01:11:02.280
because it was meant for
life, for kings to live in.

01:11:02.280 --> 01:11:05.710
So Louvre has never
really worked as a museum.

01:11:05.710 --> 01:11:09.120
I was there for the first
time on a fellowship,

01:11:09.120 --> 01:11:13.390
not MIT but Harvard fellowship.

01:11:13.390 --> 01:11:16.110
I did receive MIT fellowship,
but it was during the war.

01:11:16.110 --> 01:11:17.606
I couldn't do
anything with it so.

01:11:20.800 --> 01:11:22.840
I lived across the
street from the Louvre

01:11:22.840 --> 01:11:26.730
in a tiny little hotel, and
I went over there every day

01:11:26.730 --> 01:11:27.990
to look at it.

01:11:27.990 --> 01:11:29.960
And I tell you, in
those years you really

01:11:29.960 --> 01:11:31.940
have to have time to see
the Louvre because you

01:11:31.940 --> 01:11:33.540
get lost in it.

01:11:33.540 --> 01:11:38.630
And you don't know
where anything was.

01:11:38.630 --> 01:11:41.640
There are no toilets,
no restaurants,

01:11:41.640 --> 01:11:43.330
nothing of that sort.

01:11:43.330 --> 01:11:46.000
But it had a
wonderful collection,

01:11:46.000 --> 01:11:49.680
and you have to go back time and
time again to find surprises.

01:11:49.680 --> 01:11:53.790
And that was the way Louvre was
to all of you, to many others,

01:11:53.790 --> 01:11:57.170
until something
happened in 1989.

01:11:57.170 --> 01:12:00.820
So therefore Louvre was a
wonderful complex of buildings.

01:12:00.820 --> 01:12:03.950
But Louvre Museum was not.

01:12:03.950 --> 01:12:08.200
Louvre Museum happened to
be a tenant in the Louvre.

01:12:08.200 --> 01:12:09.340
That's all.

01:12:09.340 --> 01:12:12.590
It was occupied by the
Ministry of Culture

01:12:12.590 --> 01:12:14.400
running the Museum of France.

01:12:14.400 --> 01:12:18.760
It was occupied by the
Ministry of Finance.

01:12:18.760 --> 01:12:20.810
Occupied by many others.

01:12:20.810 --> 01:12:26.160
Louvre Museum occupied a long,
long wing along the Seine,

01:12:26.160 --> 01:12:29.850
and it's about, I would
say, 800 meters long.

01:12:29.850 --> 01:12:33.330
And that wing was
almost impossible to go

01:12:33.330 --> 01:12:36.710
from one end to the other
without going up and down.

01:12:36.710 --> 01:12:40.270
And so therefore most people
who went to the Louvre

01:12:40.270 --> 01:12:46.160
as I did probably only
saw maybe 25% of it.

01:12:46.160 --> 01:12:48.180
The rest you just miss.

01:12:48.180 --> 01:12:50.120
You have to have a guide.

01:12:50.120 --> 01:12:52.010
And I didn't have a
guide, so therefore I

01:12:52.010 --> 01:12:54.360
had to go back time and time
again to find new things,

01:12:54.360 --> 01:12:55.880
new surprises.

01:12:55.880 --> 01:13:01.060
So Louvre did not work as a
museum, and the French knew it.

01:13:01.060 --> 01:13:03.420
But they wanted finally,
under Mitterrand,

01:13:03.420 --> 01:13:05.160
to do something about it.

01:13:05.160 --> 01:13:08.437
And the one move that was
perhaps the most important move

01:13:08.437 --> 01:13:10.770
of all, I mentioned
earlier, it was

01:13:10.770 --> 01:13:14.492
the recapturing of
the Richelieu Wing

01:13:14.492 --> 01:13:15.700
from the Ministry of Finance.

01:13:21.570 --> 01:13:27.300
Now this is perhaps
not a very good slide.

01:13:27.300 --> 01:13:28.190
I apologize.

01:13:28.190 --> 01:13:30.880
But you can see the
importance of Louvre

01:13:30.880 --> 01:13:33.010
in the heart of Paris.

01:13:33.010 --> 01:13:36.720
Because it's situated perhaps
in the most important, really,

01:13:36.720 --> 01:13:39.660
the center, the heart of Paris.

01:13:39.660 --> 01:13:43.300
And yet it's separated, the
left bank from the right bank.

01:13:43.300 --> 01:13:47.030
There's no way to
penetrate except by car

01:13:47.030 --> 01:13:52.930
from the left bank, which
is below, to the right bank.

01:13:52.930 --> 01:13:56.310
So it became,
actually, a barrier.

01:13:56.310 --> 01:14:00.320
And that became an urban design
problem of first magnitude.

01:14:00.320 --> 01:14:02.420
Because we have to
open up the Louvre.

01:14:02.420 --> 01:14:06.630
To open up the Louvre, then you
rejoin the two parts of Paris.

01:14:06.630 --> 01:14:11.310
Not by car, you can always
drive around, but on foot.

01:14:11.310 --> 01:14:13.510
And this is one of the
challenges that is least

01:14:13.510 --> 01:14:16.325
talked about, but perhaps, in
my opinion, the most important.

01:14:20.540 --> 01:14:25.390
The axis of the Louvre leads
all the way to Saint Germain.

01:14:25.390 --> 01:14:28.500
And it all probably
is, again, when

01:14:28.500 --> 01:14:31.050
I say the most important
urban composition,

01:14:31.050 --> 01:14:34.930
I really should say that
it's the most important axis

01:14:34.930 --> 01:14:36.230
of the world.

01:14:36.230 --> 01:14:38.160
There's no axis like
that, that leads

01:14:38.160 --> 01:14:41.480
all the way from the
Louvre to Saint Germain.

01:14:41.480 --> 01:14:44.650
It went through the garden of
the Louvre, which you all know,

01:14:44.650 --> 01:14:47.746
Place de la Concorde,
up Champs-Elysees

01:14:47.746 --> 01:14:50.860
to the Arc de Triomphe,
and then on to La Defense,

01:14:50.860 --> 01:14:52.220
and then La Defense beyond.

01:14:56.600 --> 01:14:58.660
Now the Louvre.

01:14:58.660 --> 01:15:00.610
This is a diagram of
the Louvre to show you.

01:15:04.560 --> 01:15:08.000
The grey area on top was
occupied by the Ministry

01:15:08.000 --> 01:15:08.500
of Finance.

01:15:13.000 --> 01:15:19.600
The ocher, the yellowish
color wing, 800 meters long,

01:15:19.600 --> 01:15:23.200
half a mile long occupied
not just by the museum,

01:15:23.200 --> 01:15:26.580
by the museum and by the museum
administration of France.

01:15:26.580 --> 01:15:29.690
And French bureaucracy, I tell
you, takes up a lot of space.

01:15:33.760 --> 01:15:34.640
So it's not all busy.

01:15:37.370 --> 01:15:45.880
You can see that people come by
the one side of the courtyard

01:15:45.880 --> 01:15:47.970
and enter there
while you're waiting

01:15:47.970 --> 01:15:52.950
in line if you're lucky, a
good day, you don't mind.

01:15:52.950 --> 01:15:55.280
But then you'll be
pestered by people

01:15:55.280 --> 01:15:59.010
trying to sell you all kinds
of things that you don't want.

01:15:59.010 --> 01:16:03.240
But nonetheless, there are
people, 3 million people,

01:16:03.240 --> 01:16:04.725
continue to come.

01:16:04.725 --> 01:16:05.225
Why?

01:16:05.225 --> 01:16:07.090
For its collection.

01:16:07.090 --> 01:16:09.470
But you really have
to go through really

01:16:09.470 --> 01:16:12.820
a very uncomfortable
experience in the process.

01:16:12.820 --> 01:16:15.250
Now what I saw at
that time when I

01:16:15.250 --> 01:16:18.390
was asked by the French
government to say,

01:16:18.390 --> 01:16:21.326
can you do something
about the museum?

01:16:21.326 --> 01:16:22.600
I said, I don't know.

01:16:22.600 --> 01:16:24.580
But anyway, I'll try.

01:16:24.580 --> 01:16:27.100
And the one thing that
became apparent to me

01:16:27.100 --> 01:16:30.820
when I looked at this problem,
I said, the Ministry of Finance

01:16:30.820 --> 01:16:31.320
has to go.

01:16:35.850 --> 01:16:38.830
And I said, the
reason is simple.

01:16:38.830 --> 01:16:43.360
I said, when you want to
build a modern museum,

01:16:43.360 --> 01:16:46.260
what Louvre is-- why it
is not a modern museum

01:16:46.260 --> 01:16:49.790
for a simple reason, and
that is, in modern museums,

01:16:49.790 --> 01:16:53.250
which I knew well then because
I finished the National

01:16:53.250 --> 01:16:57.180
Gallery already, so about
50% of exhibit space

01:16:57.180 --> 01:17:03.500
has to be matched by 50%
of supporting spaces.

01:17:03.500 --> 01:17:08.000
Reserve, conservation
laboratories, restaurants,

01:17:08.000 --> 01:17:14.450
auditorium, lecture hall,
public's reception spaces,

01:17:14.450 --> 01:17:17.080
toilets, things like that,
which it didn't have.

01:17:17.080 --> 01:17:18.980
Tiny little toilets.

01:17:18.980 --> 01:17:22.350
In fact, I remember very
well when I went there,

01:17:22.350 --> 01:17:24.853
I frequently had to leave
the Louvre because I

01:17:24.853 --> 01:17:28.805
had to find a place to go.

01:17:28.805 --> 01:17:30.300
And then when you
leave the Louvre,

01:17:30.300 --> 01:17:32.760
some people don't come
back again, you know.

01:17:32.760 --> 01:17:38.430
So they lost a lot of people.

01:17:38.430 --> 01:17:45.130
And it's not really surprising
that the average stay

01:17:45.130 --> 01:17:48.010
of visitors to the Louvre
is only one hour and 1/2.

01:17:50.950 --> 01:17:54.070
Whereas the National
Gallery is 3 and 1/2 hours.

01:17:54.070 --> 01:17:57.190
Metropolitan Museum,
about the same.

01:17:57.190 --> 01:18:00.750
So I said that the
key to making Louvre

01:18:00.750 --> 01:18:04.900
function as a museum is to
change the Louvre from 800

01:18:04.900 --> 01:18:09.830
meters long, up and
down, up and down,

01:18:09.830 --> 01:18:13.080
to something very compact.

01:18:13.080 --> 01:18:15.260
But you can give up this
wing, the floor where

01:18:15.260 --> 01:18:19.750
the Spanish paintings
were, or still are,

01:18:19.750 --> 01:18:23.600
and give it to other uses,
but you must move the Louvre

01:18:23.600 --> 01:18:25.100
to that part.

01:18:25.100 --> 01:18:27.490
In that way you
accomplish two things,

01:18:27.490 --> 01:18:30.850
which is essential to making
Louvre Museum function.

01:18:30.850 --> 01:18:33.830
Number one, you can
excavate that court,

01:18:33.830 --> 01:18:38.270
which is in grey area,
the Napoleon Court.

01:18:38.270 --> 01:18:42.130
We can go down two levels,
to the level of the Seine,

01:18:42.130 --> 01:18:44.560
which is about 10 meters down.

01:18:44.560 --> 01:18:46.910
You can go down
two levels, and you

01:18:46.910 --> 01:18:49.470
recapture half a million
square feet of space

01:18:49.470 --> 01:18:51.310
just by excavating that.

01:18:51.310 --> 01:18:52.370
And why not?

01:18:52.370 --> 01:18:54.400
You can use that
space for reserve.

01:18:54.400 --> 01:18:57.510
You can use that space
for all the infrastructure

01:18:57.510 --> 01:19:00.350
support that a
modern museum needs.

01:19:00.350 --> 01:19:02.570
Because under the old Louvre
there's no foundation.

01:19:02.570 --> 01:19:04.230
There's no space.

01:19:04.230 --> 01:19:06.290
There are some pipes
and that's about all.

01:19:06.290 --> 01:19:07.040
But nothing there.

01:19:07.040 --> 01:19:10.660
There are some sewers
that go through.

01:19:10.660 --> 01:19:15.890
But that is the key
to making the Louvre

01:19:15.890 --> 01:19:17.620
into the modern museum.

01:19:17.620 --> 01:19:22.380
Second reason is that if you
put the center of the Louvre not

01:19:22.380 --> 01:19:25.030
on one side, down below,
but really in the middle

01:19:25.030 --> 01:19:30.770
of the grey area, you've got the
center of gravity of the Louvre

01:19:30.770 --> 01:19:31.900
right there.

01:19:31.900 --> 01:19:35.000
And from that point you
can go to the three wings.

01:19:35.000 --> 01:19:38.940
Louvre has three wings,
Denon-- Venus de Milo is there

01:19:38.940 --> 01:19:46.110
and Mona Lisa is there-- and
to the Richelieu and to Sully.

01:19:46.110 --> 01:19:47.040
Very short distance.

01:19:47.040 --> 01:19:49.210
Not 800 meters anymore.

01:19:49.210 --> 01:19:51.930
It's going to be only about
something like 50 meters.

01:19:51.930 --> 01:19:54.470
The difference, so that
you can divide Louvre

01:19:54.470 --> 01:19:57.340
into really three parts,
even though they're

01:19:57.340 --> 01:19:59.040
interconnected upstairs.

01:19:59.040 --> 01:20:04.370
Three parts, and each part is
at least several days' visit.

01:20:04.370 --> 01:20:07.060
So a big museum, you
may wonder whether you

01:20:07.060 --> 01:20:08.630
need a museum this size.

01:20:08.630 --> 01:20:11.720
But be that as it may,
it's a big museum,

01:20:11.720 --> 01:20:13.990
and that's the only way
to solve the problem.

01:20:13.990 --> 01:20:18.060
So therefore I
told the president,

01:20:18.060 --> 01:20:19.980
I said, you've got
to do two things.

01:20:19.980 --> 01:20:25.200
You've got to let us
dig under the court

01:20:25.200 --> 01:20:26.710
because there may
be relics there.

01:20:26.710 --> 01:20:28.168
The project could
have been stopped

01:20:28.168 --> 01:20:30.920
if they'd found something
important there.

01:20:30.920 --> 01:20:32.810
And then you also
have to find a way

01:20:32.810 --> 01:20:34.600
to move the Ministry of Finance.

01:20:39.580 --> 01:20:42.280
At this point-- it's not
so funny at that time

01:20:42.280 --> 01:20:47.750
because-- but this
tells you something

01:20:47.750 --> 01:20:55.200
about the man who is still
the president of France.

01:20:55.200 --> 01:20:56.650
I don't know of
any heads of state

01:20:56.650 --> 01:21:02.280
in the world that has the kind
of breadth of understanding

01:21:02.280 --> 01:21:06.455
of the history and
culture not only of France

01:21:06.455 --> 01:21:07.840
but of the world.

01:21:07.840 --> 01:21:09.620
He's really a remarkable man.

01:21:09.620 --> 01:21:13.290
He may not be the greatest
politician, I don't know.

01:21:13.290 --> 01:21:14.830
Time will tell.

01:21:14.830 --> 01:21:18.820
But he was and he is a really
exceptional leader when

01:21:18.820 --> 01:21:20.970
it comes to art and culture.

01:21:20.970 --> 01:21:25.483
And I could not have done
anything there without him.

01:21:25.483 --> 01:21:26.429
That's obvious.

01:21:32.110 --> 01:21:34.520
The court, before we
did anything with it,

01:21:34.520 --> 01:21:37.780
was a parking lot.

01:21:37.780 --> 01:21:43.080
It's a parking lot for
the bureaucrats that

01:21:43.080 --> 01:21:44.440
occupy the Ministry of Finance.

01:21:51.088 --> 01:21:55.970
The court, after excavation,
finding-- one year

01:21:55.970 --> 01:21:58.740
archaeologists were digging,
with really literally

01:21:58.740 --> 01:22:00.300
with brushes.

01:22:00.300 --> 01:22:02.200
And they found
things, all right,

01:22:02.200 --> 01:22:04.390
but nothing terribly important.

01:22:04.390 --> 01:22:07.250
Because this court,
unlike the court

01:22:07.250 --> 01:22:10.410
which I wish I had slides to
show you, with a marvelous

01:22:10.410 --> 01:22:14.020
dungeon foundation, is
now on display, which

01:22:14.020 --> 01:22:15.620
when you go there you see it.

01:22:15.620 --> 01:22:20.820
This court was inhabited by
people who served the kings.

01:22:20.820 --> 01:22:24.650
Their bakers, their cobblers,
there's a little church,

01:22:24.650 --> 01:22:25.970
and that's about all.

01:22:25.970 --> 01:22:29.390
And therefore there's
nothing that is so important

01:22:29.390 --> 01:22:31.220
that they say, stop.

01:22:31.220 --> 01:22:32.770
We were very fortunate.

01:22:32.770 --> 01:22:36.610
From '84 to '85,
that year we were all

01:22:36.610 --> 01:22:38.760
waiting to see what happened.

01:22:38.760 --> 01:22:40.830
We wished they
discovered nothing.

01:22:40.830 --> 01:22:42.630
And the archaeologists,
of course,

01:22:42.630 --> 01:22:45.960
wished they found something.

01:22:45.960 --> 01:22:48.470
But anyway, they found
nothing of importance,

01:22:48.470 --> 01:22:53.630
and we proceeded to
get the green light.

01:22:53.630 --> 01:22:57.640
Now here I want to show you
the organization of the Louvre.

01:22:57.640 --> 01:22:58.562
This is the court.

01:22:58.562 --> 01:23:00.270
The rest of the Louvre
is not shown here.

01:23:00.270 --> 01:23:02.490
It's only the Napoleon Court.

01:23:02.490 --> 01:23:08.800
By putting an object, something,
in the center of this court,

01:23:08.800 --> 01:23:10.890
let's say, in the
Napoleon Court,

01:23:10.890 --> 01:23:14.000
you can connect the
three pavilions in a very

01:23:14.000 --> 01:23:15.940
short and direct way.

01:23:15.940 --> 01:23:17.130
And very understandable.

01:23:17.130 --> 01:23:17.820
You can see it.

01:23:17.820 --> 01:23:21.970
When you're in the court
or in the hall below,

01:23:21.970 --> 01:23:27.080
you can look out and you can
see Denon, Richelieu, and Sully.

01:23:27.080 --> 01:23:31.828
And that clarity of orientation
is key to a big museum.

01:23:38.680 --> 01:23:42.710
This is an urban studies.

01:23:42.710 --> 01:23:46.120
To try to ventilate
Paris, you must first

01:23:46.120 --> 01:23:47.532
ventilate the Louvre.

01:23:47.532 --> 01:23:51.310
To ventilate the Louvre you
have to do several things.

01:23:51.310 --> 01:23:55.460
You can come over from the left
bank over Pont des Arts, which

01:23:55.460 --> 01:23:57.700
is a pedestrian bridge,
wonderful bridge

01:23:57.700 --> 01:23:59.490
architecturally.

01:23:59.490 --> 01:24:02.140
From an engineering point
of view, a wonderful bridge.

01:24:02.140 --> 01:24:08.000
And then you enter into cour
carree and then turn left.

01:24:08.000 --> 01:24:12.200
But you have to do two things
in order to make it truly open.

01:24:12.200 --> 01:24:16.660
One is that you have to open
that passage under Richelieu,

01:24:16.660 --> 01:24:18.740
called Passage
Richelieu, to lead you

01:24:18.740 --> 01:24:23.070
to the Place du Palais-Royal,
which is right there.

01:24:23.070 --> 01:24:24.930
And then from then
on to Palais-Royal,

01:24:24.930 --> 01:24:30.440
and then to Pompidou
Centre, which you know.

01:24:30.440 --> 01:24:34.670
Second thing that you must also
do is to open up the garden.

01:24:34.670 --> 01:24:37.050
A new bridge is
being built. I think

01:24:37.050 --> 01:24:39.630
it's about to start
construction here,

01:24:39.630 --> 01:24:42.890
so that you can walk
from Orsay, which

01:24:42.890 --> 01:24:46.110
is where the wonderful
impressionist collection is

01:24:46.110 --> 01:24:46.820
today.

01:24:46.820 --> 01:24:48.992
From Orsay all the
way to the Louvre.

01:24:48.992 --> 01:24:52.120
And that will open
up the Louvre.

01:24:52.120 --> 01:24:55.650
And once that Louvre is opened
up, it no longer is a barrier.

01:24:55.650 --> 01:24:57.385
It becomes a connector.

01:25:02.150 --> 01:25:05.250
This was a drawing
done by Steve,

01:25:05.250 --> 01:25:08.620
who's here, Steve [? Ohs ?].

01:25:08.620 --> 01:25:13.550
I showed this drawing
to the president,

01:25:13.550 --> 01:25:15.520
and he was not shocked.

01:25:15.520 --> 01:25:19.150
Perhaps he was the only
man that was not shocked.

01:25:19.150 --> 01:25:24.960
But when I showed it to the
press, they were shocked.

01:25:24.960 --> 01:25:28.330
And I tell you, from
that point on, as I said,

01:25:28.330 --> 01:25:33.740
18 months of just nothing
but harassment by the media.

01:25:38.170 --> 01:25:42.740
But still, we got
the green light.

01:25:42.740 --> 01:25:48.920
And the president as
well as his official,

01:25:48.920 --> 01:25:52.620
who was in charge of the Louvre,
an important man, Biasini,

01:25:52.620 --> 01:25:54.180
said, let's dig.

01:25:54.180 --> 01:25:58.400
Because the archaeologist
has already said it's okay.

01:25:58.400 --> 01:25:59.470
Let's dig.

01:25:59.470 --> 01:26:01.590
And this was what you see.

01:26:01.590 --> 01:26:05.780
And that was the beginning
of another furor.

01:26:05.780 --> 01:26:08.730
And people demonstrating
and everything,

01:26:08.730 --> 01:26:11.410
except trying to--
they didn't do

01:26:11.410 --> 01:26:15.030
anything really serious like
committing suicide or anything

01:26:15.030 --> 01:26:15.530
like that.

01:26:15.530 --> 01:26:18.700
But very close, very close.

01:26:18.700 --> 01:26:19.870
There were a few.

01:26:19.870 --> 01:26:22.095
[LAUGHTER]

01:26:23.430 --> 01:26:26.000
Now I'm going to go quickly
because of time, quickly.

01:26:26.000 --> 01:26:30.220
To show you how the
building processed.

01:26:30.220 --> 01:26:32.690
And the building of this
part is remarkably fast.

01:26:32.690 --> 01:26:35.480
French contractors
are incredibly good.

01:26:35.480 --> 01:26:39.070
I have to-- they're
very, very good.

01:26:39.070 --> 01:26:42.640
These are slides that shows the
construction, the whole-- this

01:26:42.640 --> 01:26:44.210
is the Napoleon
Court, by the way.

01:26:44.210 --> 01:26:45.400
This is not the rest.

01:26:45.400 --> 01:26:47.750
Only one part, the
important part.

01:26:47.750 --> 01:26:50.360
This is the part where
the pyramid must emerge.

01:26:50.360 --> 01:26:54.070
And the square grid that you
see, the structural steel

01:26:54.070 --> 01:26:56.830
member there, they're
all on bearings.

01:26:56.830 --> 01:26:59.410
It can move.

01:26:59.410 --> 01:27:01.510
They move.

01:27:01.510 --> 01:27:04.540
And they're supported on
four columns, and that's all.

01:27:10.430 --> 01:27:15.840
We had about 60 or some sides
because from one vantage point

01:27:15.840 --> 01:27:18.990
they took pictures
every so many months.

01:27:18.990 --> 01:27:20.700
But I'm only showing a few.

01:27:23.920 --> 01:27:26.320
So that's very quick.

01:27:26.320 --> 01:27:28.990
But it took about--

01:27:28.990 --> 01:27:30.335
[APPLAUSE]

01:27:30.335 --> 01:27:30.834
Thank you.

01:27:30.834 --> 01:27:33.600
[APPLAUSE]

01:27:33.600 --> 01:27:37.580
It took 18 months
to reach this stage.

01:27:37.580 --> 01:27:43.270
And at this point I would say
it's the end of phase one.

01:27:43.270 --> 01:27:48.020
It was opened in spring 1989.

01:27:48.020 --> 01:27:52.730
And Mitterrand was
there to cut the ribbon.

01:27:52.730 --> 01:27:56.480
And it was rather
well received by then.

01:27:56.480 --> 01:28:03.430
But still, I would say there
were maybe 30% or 40% people

01:28:03.430 --> 01:28:05.530
still not very happy with it.

01:28:05.530 --> 01:28:08.790
And for any number of reasons.

01:28:08.790 --> 01:28:17.870
But nevertheless, it was
built. And since this is MIT,

01:28:17.870 --> 01:28:19.510
I thought I have to
bring this diagram.

01:28:22.210 --> 01:28:24.100
If I show it to
another audience,

01:28:24.100 --> 01:28:25.610
they would not be
interested in it.

01:28:28.200 --> 01:28:30.470
And I'm going to show
you some of the slides

01:28:30.470 --> 01:28:32.260
of the construction.

01:28:32.260 --> 01:28:34.470
But one thing that
should also interest you,

01:28:34.470 --> 01:28:37.810
that the French government
made no secret of it.

01:28:37.810 --> 01:28:40.620
They want everything
to be made in France.

01:28:40.620 --> 01:28:44.410
And the only exception,
which I asked the president

01:28:44.410 --> 01:28:50.630
to allow us, is to have all
the tension elements made

01:28:50.630 --> 01:28:57.740
in Massachusetts by a
small rigging company that

01:28:57.740 --> 01:29:05.510
did the America's Cup yachts.

01:29:05.510 --> 01:29:07.675
I don't know whether
Bill Koch is here or not,

01:29:07.675 --> 01:29:10.600
but he would know.

01:29:10.600 --> 01:29:12.670
And we are the best.

01:29:12.670 --> 01:29:16.090
And I showed him some
samples of the rigging,

01:29:16.090 --> 01:29:19.790
how the turn buckle was fit
into the cable and all that.

01:29:19.790 --> 01:29:22.010
And he was fascinated with it.

01:29:22.010 --> 01:29:25.070
Because he liked it so much that
he didn't even bother to say,

01:29:25.070 --> 01:29:27.980
is there a French
equivalent to it?

01:29:27.980 --> 01:29:29.800
We just say, go ahead.

01:29:29.800 --> 01:29:32.360
So we did it.

01:29:32.360 --> 01:29:38.060
On the other hand, as a
passing-- the glass problem.

01:29:38.060 --> 01:29:42.490
You see, glass has to be
white, has to be clear.

01:29:42.490 --> 01:29:45.180
If the glass is not
clear, this is laminated,

01:29:45.180 --> 01:29:46.894
you know, it's double layer.

01:29:46.894 --> 01:29:47.560
It's very thick.

01:29:47.560 --> 01:29:49.960
It's about almost
3/4 of an inch thick.

01:29:49.960 --> 01:29:51.980
If it's not clear
it would be green.

01:29:51.980 --> 01:29:54.960
And if you see it through
the corner of this glass,

01:29:54.960 --> 01:29:57.900
it will be very dark
green, bottle green.

01:29:57.900 --> 01:30:00.755
So therefore that is not
acceptable because the Louvre,

01:30:00.755 --> 01:30:03.820
you see, the composition
of the Louvre must be seen.

01:30:03.820 --> 01:30:07.040
And the ocher-colored
stone shouldn't look green.

01:30:07.040 --> 01:30:12.880
So therefore I requested
the French manufacturer

01:30:12.880 --> 01:30:15.150
Saint-Gobain to make this glass.

01:30:15.150 --> 01:30:17.470
They say, no, we no
longer make them.

01:30:17.470 --> 01:30:20.770
And then finally they say, well,
if you built 1,000 pyramids,

01:30:20.770 --> 01:30:21.860
we'll make them for you.

01:30:25.570 --> 01:30:29.200
So I didn't report
this to the president.

01:30:29.200 --> 01:30:32.060
I went to a German
firm, SCHOTT Glass.

01:30:32.060 --> 01:30:33.250
I say, can you make it?

01:30:33.250 --> 01:30:34.620
Yes, we can.

01:30:34.620 --> 01:30:36.190
Saint-Gobain said,
we'll make it.

01:30:36.190 --> 01:30:39.082
[LAUGHTER]

01:30:43.420 --> 01:30:45.104
I'm going to go very quickly.

01:30:45.104 --> 01:30:46.020
Making of the pyramid.

01:30:46.020 --> 01:30:48.540
The pyramid has one virtue.

01:30:48.540 --> 01:30:51.520
It's a very stable
form, you know that.

01:30:51.520 --> 01:30:55.010
And consequently, it requires
the least amount of steel

01:30:55.010 --> 01:30:56.810
in order to support it.

01:30:56.810 --> 01:31:02.890
And consequently, if you
use the finest technology

01:31:02.890 --> 01:31:05.720
available to you,
technologically, to build it,

01:31:05.720 --> 01:31:08.610
it will be the most
transparent of form.

01:31:08.610 --> 01:31:11.090
And it must be the
most transparent form

01:31:11.090 --> 01:31:13.540
because you want to
see that composition.

01:31:13.540 --> 01:31:16.170
That composition is so
important to the world,

01:31:16.170 --> 01:31:17.610
not just to France.

01:31:17.610 --> 01:31:30.600
So you may ask, do you need
something that projects?

01:31:30.600 --> 01:31:33.680
Many architects in
France as well as abroad

01:31:33.680 --> 01:31:39.150
suggested, why not just have
a glass sheet on the ground?

01:31:39.150 --> 01:31:41.325
And you bring light
in just as well.

01:31:41.325 --> 01:31:42.010
But I said, no.

01:31:42.010 --> 01:31:45.250
I said, you have to have space.

01:31:45.250 --> 01:31:50.500
You go into the lower level
10 meters down, 9 meters down.

01:31:50.500 --> 01:31:53.140
And you don't want to
have a glass ceiling.

01:31:53.140 --> 01:31:57.915
That word may mean
something to some.

01:31:57.915 --> 01:31:59.670
Don't want a glass ceiling.

01:31:59.670 --> 01:32:01.200
You want space.

01:32:01.200 --> 01:32:04.450
And so something has to project.

01:32:04.450 --> 01:32:07.280
And I defended the pyramid.

01:32:07.280 --> 01:32:10.550
And the pyramid, for those
of you who know France,

01:32:10.550 --> 01:32:13.470
is a very important
symbol to French.

01:32:13.470 --> 01:32:17.260
I think the French are probably
more conscious of pyramid

01:32:17.260 --> 01:32:22.960
as a form than any other people
because from Napoleon, I guess.

01:32:22.960 --> 01:32:25.030
They really went to Egypt.

01:32:25.030 --> 01:32:26.780
They took a lot of
things away from Egypt.

01:32:29.670 --> 01:32:32.770
So the pyramid form is
necessary to give you

01:32:32.770 --> 01:32:35.790
space, so when you--
give you light,

01:32:35.790 --> 01:32:40.260
give you space, but at
the same time transparent.

01:32:40.260 --> 01:32:42.280
You can see through it.

01:32:42.280 --> 01:32:44.560
And also you need it simple.

01:32:44.560 --> 01:32:46.920
Because if this is going
to be the main entrance

01:32:46.920 --> 01:32:50.280
to the Louvre, it cannot
be just a subway entrance.

01:32:50.280 --> 01:32:51.530
It just simply cannot be.

01:32:54.740 --> 01:32:58.390
Glass was put in this manner.

01:32:58.390 --> 01:33:01.240
And for a long time
there was a debate,

01:33:01.240 --> 01:33:03.500
can you clean the glass?

01:33:03.500 --> 01:33:06.160
And we tried different ways.

01:33:06.160 --> 01:33:11.100
They hired some Indians
from Canada to clean it.

01:33:11.100 --> 01:33:12.710
They even tried robot.

01:33:12.710 --> 01:33:14.140
Eventually we cleaned by robot.

01:33:14.140 --> 01:33:18.220
But finally we
got some alpinists

01:33:18.220 --> 01:33:21.590
to get up there, hang the rope
from the tip of the pyramid,

01:33:21.590 --> 01:33:22.680
and wash it.

01:33:22.680 --> 01:33:24.240
And it only took
two days to wash it.

01:33:24.240 --> 01:33:25.350
But now it took less.

01:33:25.350 --> 01:33:27.320
Now one day is all we need.

01:33:27.320 --> 01:33:30.410
So the washing problem
no longer a problem.

01:33:30.410 --> 01:33:32.365
But for a while that
became also a problem.

01:33:32.365 --> 01:33:33.740
Because the French
wanted to find

01:33:33.740 --> 01:33:35.910
any reason to object to it.

01:33:35.910 --> 01:33:39.620
And cleaning the pyramid
also kept us quite involved.

01:33:42.280 --> 01:33:44.010
Now you're inside the space.

01:33:44.010 --> 01:33:47.984
And you can see the Louvre.

01:33:47.984 --> 01:33:49.400
You can see the
Louvre through it.

01:33:57.543 --> 01:33:59.140
Ah, that was D-Day.

01:34:04.670 --> 01:34:07.120
But no booing, no booing.

01:34:07.120 --> 01:34:08.410
Lots of applause.

01:34:08.410 --> 01:34:08.950
No booing.

01:34:12.878 --> 01:34:24.692
That was-- ah, I
like that photograph.

01:34:24.692 --> 01:34:25.650
You don't see anything.

01:34:33.020 --> 01:34:41.540
There's a story about this
statue during Louis XIV's time.

01:34:41.540 --> 01:34:44.820
Ah, I'm short of time.

01:34:44.820 --> 01:34:46.860
I will try to go quickly.

01:34:46.860 --> 01:34:50.320
Louis XIV's time.

01:34:50.320 --> 01:34:53.640
They invited-- this
shows that in those years

01:34:53.640 --> 01:34:57.880
the kings of France are
already very cultured people.

01:34:57.880 --> 01:35:02.905
He invited Benini
from Rome to come.

01:35:02.905 --> 01:35:11.830
Benini had just finished the
Saint Peter's arcade, the wing

01:35:11.830 --> 01:35:14.260
that enclosed that space.

01:35:14.260 --> 01:35:16.490
Perhaps [? Dandonne ?],
he and Borromini

01:35:16.490 --> 01:35:19.145
were the two most important
architects of that time.

01:35:19.145 --> 01:35:21.170
And the French king
wanted the best,

01:35:21.170 --> 01:35:24.420
so he invited Bernini
to come to do something

01:35:24.420 --> 01:35:26.140
in the back of the
Louvre, not this part,

01:35:26.140 --> 01:35:28.330
in the back of the Louvre.

01:35:28.330 --> 01:35:31.470
And he was there six
months, and he did not

01:35:31.470 --> 01:35:34.700
survive the French architects.

01:35:34.700 --> 01:35:38.350
And the only thing he
left behind-- there

01:35:38.350 --> 01:35:41.530
are a few things he left
behind, not of importance--

01:35:41.530 --> 01:35:44.160
but the only big thing
that he left behind

01:35:44.160 --> 01:35:46.410
is the statue of Louis XIV.

01:35:46.410 --> 01:35:49.540
But that was made in Rome,
and it was shipped to France

01:35:49.540 --> 01:35:50.750
afterwards.

01:35:50.750 --> 01:35:54.510
And Louis XIV never liked
it because it was his image,

01:35:54.510 --> 01:35:56.570
at that time, was 25 years ago.

01:35:56.570 --> 01:35:57.660
He was a young man then.

01:35:57.660 --> 01:35:59.530
He's no longer young.

01:35:59.530 --> 01:36:03.320
So he banished it to Versailles
and it remained there.

01:36:03.320 --> 01:36:06.127
And because it was not known,
it was never vandalized

01:36:06.127 --> 01:36:08.210
and never broke-- never,
I mean, never vandalized.

01:36:08.210 --> 01:36:10.040
It was never destroyed
during the war

01:36:10.040 --> 01:36:11.960
because it was just left there.

01:36:11.960 --> 01:36:14.195
So, but it was marble.

01:36:14.195 --> 01:36:19.750
It was vandalized by a man
from Brittany, anarchists,

01:36:19.750 --> 01:36:23.790
I guess, and no
longer salvageable.

01:36:23.790 --> 01:36:28.990
But I persuaded the
conservator of Versailles

01:36:28.990 --> 01:36:32.800
to let us make a cast
of it, the only way.

01:36:32.800 --> 01:36:33.700
And they did.

01:36:33.700 --> 01:36:35.100
And it's made in lead.

01:36:35.100 --> 01:36:39.380
And I thought he should put
it there to remember him

01:36:39.380 --> 01:36:40.390
as someone who tried.

01:36:45.490 --> 01:36:48.330
But very important,
that location.

01:36:48.330 --> 01:36:50.320
I needed something
there to terminate

01:36:50.320 --> 01:36:53.300
the access of Champs-Elysees
because Champs-Elysees--

01:36:53.300 --> 01:36:57.500
that axis of La [? Note ?],
the La [? Note ?] axis

01:36:57.500 --> 01:36:58.910
was not terminated
at the Louvre.

01:36:58.910 --> 01:37:02.050
Because the Louvre is a little
bit like this, like this.

01:37:02.050 --> 01:37:05.830
So it has to be terminated
not by the pyramid,

01:37:05.830 --> 01:37:08.210
by something else
and something strong.

01:37:08.210 --> 01:37:12.840
And I was fearful of
commissioning a French sculptor

01:37:12.840 --> 01:37:13.440
at that time.

01:37:13.440 --> 01:37:16.470
You don't know what's
going to happen.

01:37:16.470 --> 01:37:19.270
I'm perfectly frank about it.

01:37:19.270 --> 01:37:23.010
You just don't know what
to-- so that was my escape

01:37:23.010 --> 01:37:24.203
from a responsibility.

01:37:28.070 --> 01:37:30.750
Now second phase of the Louvre.

01:37:30.750 --> 01:37:35.970
Not spectacular, not
at all polemical,

01:37:35.970 --> 01:37:37.490
but extremely important.

01:37:37.490 --> 01:37:41.540
Because now it opened
only a few months ago.

01:37:41.540 --> 01:37:44.690
If you go there
now, you understand

01:37:44.690 --> 01:37:47.910
why the pyramid was put
there in the first place.

01:37:47.910 --> 01:37:53.070
And I think the vindication
of the whole plan

01:37:53.070 --> 01:37:57.410
is now made possible through
the completion of this wing.

01:37:57.410 --> 01:37:59.410
And for that reason, even
though architecturally

01:37:59.410 --> 01:38:03.030
it's not spectacular because
the facade had to be kept

01:38:03.030 --> 01:38:05.820
and everything has to be
internalized, done inside.

01:38:05.820 --> 01:38:08.940
Number two, I had to work with
two French architects, which

01:38:08.940 --> 01:38:11.470
is reasonable because,
after all, you know,

01:38:11.470 --> 01:38:12.490
can't hog it all.

01:38:21.720 --> 01:38:25.609
Now you see the end
of the Napoleon Court,

01:38:25.609 --> 01:38:26.900
and this is what it looks like.

01:38:26.900 --> 01:38:29.920
This is 10 meters below ground.

01:38:29.920 --> 01:38:32.210
You go up three
sets of escalators

01:38:32.210 --> 01:38:34.870
to an intermediate level,
still below ground,

01:38:34.870 --> 01:38:39.130
and you can enter into the
three wings of the Louvre.

01:38:39.130 --> 01:38:42.430
And the fourth
wing going this way

01:38:42.430 --> 01:38:48.150
goes to shops,
parking, bus terminal.

01:38:48.150 --> 01:38:51.440
Eventually, all the buses
that you see on Rue de Rivoli

01:38:51.440 --> 01:38:54.800
as well as on the Quay will
disappear, as they have now.

01:38:54.800 --> 01:38:56.150
They're all underground now.

01:38:56.150 --> 01:38:58.590
So urbanistically, that's
another very important

01:38:58.590 --> 01:39:00.690
contribution.

01:39:00.690 --> 01:39:02.905
And below at this low
level, we have auditorium,

01:39:02.905 --> 01:39:05.210
we have restaurants, we
have a reception area

01:39:05.210 --> 01:39:07.970
for the young
people, and we have

01:39:07.970 --> 01:39:13.720
a bookstore, enormous bookstore,
and shops, and meeting rooms,

01:39:13.720 --> 01:39:15.700
and conference center.

01:39:15.700 --> 01:39:16.715
Everything is there.

01:39:16.715 --> 01:39:19.430
But below this level
is all circulation.

01:39:19.430 --> 01:39:23.050
There's a truck way to
connect all the departments

01:39:23.050 --> 01:39:23.990
underground.

01:39:23.990 --> 01:39:27.970
And there's a large reserve so
that all the collection, nearly

01:39:27.970 --> 01:39:30.286
all the collection, of the
Louvre now comes back home.

01:39:34.510 --> 01:39:36.840
Now we have to talk briefly
about Richelieu Wing,

01:39:36.840 --> 01:39:40.280
even though architecturally, as
I say, is not very spectacular.

01:39:40.280 --> 01:39:47.110
The two courts were proposed
way back to be covered.

01:39:47.110 --> 01:39:51.940
They were parking and trucking
for the Ministry of Finance

01:39:51.940 --> 01:39:53.650
before, so not used.

01:39:53.650 --> 01:39:57.490
By covering it, we can
turn it into an exhibition

01:39:57.490 --> 01:40:01.800
space for French sculpture.

01:40:01.800 --> 01:40:05.380
And further, we also proposed
to dig down below the Richelieu

01:40:05.380 --> 01:40:08.000
Wing, as you see, so
that the two courts are

01:40:08.000 --> 01:40:09.730
connected at the lower level.

01:40:14.860 --> 01:40:18.570
And this was a
trucking area, which

01:40:18.570 --> 01:40:21.581
now become exhibition
area for French sculpture.

01:40:38.000 --> 01:40:43.720
This wing housed four
departments, the sculpture

01:40:43.720 --> 01:40:49.670
department, the Oriental
antiquities department,

01:40:49.670 --> 01:40:52.620
the objet d'art,
which is probably

01:40:52.620 --> 01:40:56.080
the most important objet
d'art collection in the world,

01:40:56.080 --> 01:40:57.580
and paintings.

01:41:05.430 --> 01:41:12.850
I put this in mostly,
again, because this is MIT.

01:41:12.850 --> 01:41:19.160
This is a pyramid about
50 feet square inverted.

01:41:19.160 --> 01:41:20.000
Why inverted?

01:41:20.000 --> 01:41:23.320
Because its position
at the place

01:41:23.320 --> 01:41:27.580
where the circular rotary is.

01:41:27.580 --> 01:41:29.310
And you don't want
to see any projection

01:41:29.310 --> 01:41:33.550
there coming up because one
pyramid is quite enough.

01:41:33.550 --> 01:41:37.710
But we used the same theme, and
we wanted to bring light in.

01:41:37.710 --> 01:41:39.140
This is the intersection.

01:41:39.140 --> 01:41:42.580
If you go to the bus
terminal, to the parking,

01:41:42.580 --> 01:41:44.910
and to the shops, you have
to go through this point.

01:41:44.910 --> 01:41:47.270
So therefore it's
nice to have something

01:41:47.270 --> 01:41:49.800
to make people feel they're
still in the Louvre.

01:41:49.800 --> 01:41:56.430
So now this suspended pyramid
is a major engineering project.

01:41:56.430 --> 01:41:59.950
It was designed by
a man, Peter Rice

01:41:59.950 --> 01:42:02.570
of Arup Associates in London.

01:42:02.570 --> 01:42:04.930
And too bad I don't
have the drawings.

01:42:04.930 --> 01:42:07.520
It's actually a very
brilliant design.

01:42:07.520 --> 01:42:09.220
The whole thing
is-- there are only

01:42:09.220 --> 01:42:13.090
four rods in the
center all suspended,

01:42:13.090 --> 01:42:15.100
and the rest are all cables.

01:42:15.100 --> 01:42:19.110
It's cables and four
rods, and that's all.

01:42:19.110 --> 01:42:22.815
And it has one other
very exciting byproduct.

01:42:28.870 --> 01:42:32.000
Clearly I was very proud of it.

01:42:32.000 --> 01:42:33.860
Another very important
byproduct of this

01:42:33.860 --> 01:42:37.630
is the prismatic
effect of the glass.

01:42:37.630 --> 01:42:39.750
You see the glass.

01:42:39.750 --> 01:42:43.840
Because we don't have to keep
water or rain out of this

01:42:43.840 --> 01:42:47.570
because it's inside, we can
polish the edges of the glass

01:42:47.570 --> 01:42:48.890
and bevel it.

01:42:48.890 --> 01:42:52.500
By beveling it, a spectrum
of colors came out.

01:42:52.500 --> 01:42:57.650
So at times you see on
a sunny day like this,

01:42:57.650 --> 01:42:59.640
it's just a rainbow inside.

01:42:59.640 --> 01:43:00.890
It's really quite spectacular.

01:43:00.890 --> 01:43:04.320
If you go there, make sure you
go there on a good, sunny day.

01:43:07.110 --> 01:43:11.300
Now the Richelieu Wing.

01:43:11.300 --> 01:43:14.440
One thing has to be done
to the Richelieu Wing

01:43:14.440 --> 01:43:17.650
because it's about the
painting collection.

01:43:17.650 --> 01:43:20.820
It's perhaps the most important
French painting collection

01:43:20.820 --> 01:43:21.410
in the world.

01:43:21.410 --> 01:43:22.970
Not perhaps, definitely.

01:43:22.970 --> 01:43:27.260
And yet people don't go there
because the French conservators

01:43:27.260 --> 01:43:30.100
want it upstairs
because of daylight.

01:43:30.100 --> 01:43:34.770
They are very, very
insistent on using daylight.

01:43:34.770 --> 01:43:37.060
Our conservators are less so.

01:43:37.060 --> 01:43:38.450
Very insistent.

01:43:38.450 --> 01:43:41.260
And for that reason they
take the attic space.

01:43:41.260 --> 01:43:43.280
They have a ceiling
not very high.

01:43:43.280 --> 01:43:48.010
But to get up there, you have to
walk up 75 feet vertical space.

01:43:48.010 --> 01:43:50.420
And most people
don't walk up there.

01:43:50.420 --> 01:43:52.860
So they miss a lot of visitors.

01:43:52.860 --> 01:43:56.400
I was told only about maybe at
the most 10% or 15% of people

01:43:56.400 --> 01:43:59.080
go up to the top floor,
and that's a great pity.

01:43:59.080 --> 01:44:01.400
So I proposed to
put in escalators.

01:44:01.400 --> 01:44:02.350
I hate to do that.

01:44:02.350 --> 01:44:04.350
It's a 19th century
building, and you

01:44:04.350 --> 01:44:07.090
don't install
something like that

01:44:07.090 --> 01:44:08.750
unless you have a good reason.

01:44:08.750 --> 01:44:11.710
That was a big battle,
but it was won.

01:44:11.710 --> 01:44:15.870
And today nobody disagrees
that it is absolutely needed

01:44:15.870 --> 01:44:19.640
because otherwise the
interconnection up

01:44:19.640 --> 01:44:24.200
and vertically, it's
very, very difficult.

01:44:24.200 --> 01:44:24.800
Object d'art.

01:44:24.800 --> 01:44:26.300
I showed you the
sculpture already.

01:44:26.300 --> 01:44:27.549
I'll show you the objet d'art.

01:44:30.110 --> 01:44:33.740
These are the
Maximilian Tapestries. .

01:44:33.740 --> 01:44:35.210
Never shown before.

01:44:35.210 --> 01:44:36.430
No place to show it.

01:44:36.430 --> 01:44:37.970
Now they have a place.

01:44:37.970 --> 01:44:42.300
And it's a must, the Maximilian
Tapestries are a must to see.

01:44:47.500 --> 01:44:50.634
They're very dimly lit because
of the color of the threads.

01:44:54.930 --> 01:44:58.510
Now before I go
into this, this work

01:44:58.510 --> 01:45:04.355
here was done by architect
Villemard of France,

01:45:04.355 --> 01:45:06.110
of Paris, France.

01:45:06.110 --> 01:45:09.660
And my role in the
sculpture garden and this

01:45:09.660 --> 01:45:12.960
is what they call,
really, a mandaterre,

01:45:12.960 --> 01:45:14.360
so like a coordinator.

01:45:14.360 --> 01:45:17.220
I participated in all
the decision making.

01:45:17.220 --> 01:45:20.540
I chose them as my architects.

01:45:20.540 --> 01:45:21.880
I had that responsibility.

01:45:21.880 --> 01:45:26.390
But they should get
the credit for it.

01:45:26.390 --> 01:45:28.195
Now this was old Louvre.

01:45:31.420 --> 01:45:37.045
The lighting of paintings is
really a very special science,

01:45:37.045 --> 01:45:38.090
it really is.

01:45:38.090 --> 01:45:40.170
And it's extremely important.

01:45:40.170 --> 01:45:41.910
It's never studied enough.

01:45:41.910 --> 01:45:42.850
It just hasn't been.

01:45:42.850 --> 01:45:46.360
We don't have good daylight
galleries in America.

01:45:46.360 --> 01:45:48.390
We don't have it in
the National Gallery.

01:45:48.390 --> 01:45:50.700
We don't have it in Museum
of Fine Arts in Boston.

01:45:50.700 --> 01:45:52.050
They are all like this.

01:45:52.050 --> 01:45:56.320
They are lay lights
with skylight on top.

01:45:56.320 --> 01:45:59.710
And even though it could
have been artificially

01:45:59.710 --> 01:46:01.990
lit and you wouldn't
know the difference.

01:46:01.990 --> 01:46:06.310
And another disadvantage is that
the skylight is very bright.

01:46:06.310 --> 01:46:09.460
And therefore the
brightness is the ceiling.

01:46:09.460 --> 01:46:11.710
The second brightest
is the floor.

01:46:11.710 --> 01:46:16.280
The walls, where you want
the light to be, looks dark.

01:46:16.280 --> 01:46:19.600
And consequently,
we decided this

01:46:19.600 --> 01:46:22.840
is something we want to
do a piece of research on.

01:46:22.840 --> 01:46:26.310
And I think one of the
major breakthroughs,

01:46:26.310 --> 01:46:28.980
I consider, in this
wing is in the lighting.

01:46:33.540 --> 01:46:38.100
The lighting solution is this.

01:46:38.100 --> 01:46:42.750
We make the ceiling
into three layers.

01:46:42.750 --> 01:46:46.550
The first layer is glass,
skylight, with a UV filter,

01:46:46.550 --> 01:46:48.100
of course.

01:46:48.100 --> 01:46:52.390
And then below the
glass is an egg crate.

01:46:52.390 --> 01:46:57.830
An egg crate is
carefully calculated so

01:46:57.830 --> 01:46:59.950
that the orientation is such.

01:46:59.950 --> 01:47:03.460
So no direct sun
rays will come in.

01:47:03.460 --> 01:47:05.890
It would have been better
if they were movable

01:47:05.890 --> 01:47:09.880
because then we get good light
all year around, all seasons.

01:47:09.880 --> 01:47:12.470
Unfortunately, the
French have experience

01:47:12.470 --> 01:47:15.400
with maintenance crew.

01:47:15.400 --> 01:47:16.800
They say, it won't work here.

01:47:20.440 --> 01:47:24.260
And they're right, they're
right, they're right.

01:47:24.260 --> 01:47:27.590
So we use fixed louvers that
don't have to be touched.

01:47:27.590 --> 01:47:30.850
We cut off a lot of light, a lot
more light than we wanted to.

01:47:30.850 --> 01:47:39.880
But it does remove the headaches
of the gardien doing their job.

01:47:39.880 --> 01:47:41.870
And the reason of
the big cross is

01:47:41.870 --> 01:47:43.760
that people have to
walk there, so it

01:47:43.760 --> 01:47:46.030
has to be wide enough for
people to walk, to clean,

01:47:46.030 --> 01:47:48.430
to re-lamp, and
that sort of thing.

01:47:48.430 --> 01:47:52.600
But you can see the sky if
you are walking on one side.

01:47:52.600 --> 01:47:57.450
But the light, as you see now,
is deflected to the walls.

01:47:57.450 --> 01:48:00.430
It's no longer coming
down to the floor.

01:48:00.430 --> 01:48:05.270
So the walls now are
bright, and they get light.

01:48:05.270 --> 01:48:09.780
And that has turned
out to be something

01:48:09.780 --> 01:48:12.790
that the French conservators
are very conservative,

01:48:12.790 --> 01:48:14.070
and they accepted this.

01:48:14.070 --> 01:48:17.610
And they now claim this
is the best in the world.

01:48:17.610 --> 01:48:22.280
They have this-- something has
to always-- and this they like.

01:48:22.280 --> 01:48:26.810
And this is the
very important suite

01:48:26.810 --> 01:48:32.050
of paintings by Rubens
celebrating Marie de' Medici's

01:48:32.050 --> 01:48:37.520
journey and eventually
to apotheosis.

01:48:37.520 --> 01:48:39.870
All the way, and
you can see again,

01:48:39.870 --> 01:48:42.000
the light is no longer
bright on the ceiling.

01:48:42.000 --> 01:48:45.270
It's directed to the walls.

01:48:45.270 --> 01:48:46.820
And this is another
version of it.

01:48:46.820 --> 01:48:48.480
Depending-- this is
an octagonal room.

01:48:48.480 --> 01:48:50.150
That's a long room.

01:48:50.150 --> 01:48:54.170
And the previous one I
think is a square room.

01:48:54.170 --> 01:48:56.110
And this is the way
the light looks.

01:48:56.110 --> 01:48:57.850
No reflection.

01:48:57.850 --> 01:48:58.850
No reflection.

01:48:58.850 --> 01:49:00.945
I guarantee you
that, no reflection.

01:49:12.110 --> 01:49:13.240
All right.

01:49:13.240 --> 01:49:20.030
Richelieu Wing was finished in
November, opened in November 9,

01:49:20.030 --> 01:49:25.070
1993, exactly 200 years after
the founding of the Louvre.

01:49:25.070 --> 01:49:29.970
And the Richelieu Wing, together
with the Napoleon Court,

01:49:29.970 --> 01:49:31.860
is now complete.

01:49:31.860 --> 01:49:35.960
And therefore Louvre
finally functions as the way

01:49:35.960 --> 01:49:38.610
we had planned to.

01:49:38.610 --> 01:49:41.560
And some of these
slides are mostly

01:49:41.560 --> 01:49:47.030
to show you what it looks
like when it's all finished.

01:49:47.030 --> 01:49:52.360
And young lady is
celebrating the event.

01:49:52.360 --> 01:49:54.075
So there you are.

01:49:56.620 --> 01:49:58.800
I think that's-- oh!

01:49:58.800 --> 01:50:00.367
I used up too much time.

01:50:00.367 --> 01:50:01.361
Well, sorry.

01:50:01.361 --> 01:50:04.343
[APPLAUSE]

01:50:07.822 --> 01:50:11.301
I'm sorry.

01:50:11.301 --> 01:50:14.283
Ellen, I apologize.

01:50:14.283 --> 01:50:16.271
Sorry.

01:50:16.271 --> 01:50:18.259
I think we better go, huh?

01:50:18.259 --> 01:50:19.750
We'd better go.

01:50:19.750 --> 01:50:21.241
We're running out of time.

01:50:21.241 --> 01:50:22.235
Oh, oh.

01:50:22.235 --> 01:50:25.217
[APPLAUSE]

01:50:35.654 --> 01:50:38.139
I'm not a professional,
as you see.

01:50:38.139 --> 01:50:39.630
Like Professor Morrison.

01:50:39.630 --> 01:50:40.624
Absolutely on time.

01:50:40.624 --> 01:50:41.615
HARRIS: Thank you.

01:50:41.615 --> 01:50:42.115
Thank you.

01:50:47.110 --> 01:50:50.960
Well, you will see that we
are slowly moving through MIT.

01:50:50.960 --> 01:50:54.430
We've had the
theoretical construct

01:50:54.430 --> 01:50:56.500
for the relationship
between art and science

01:50:56.500 --> 01:50:58.270
from the School of Science.

01:50:58.270 --> 01:51:01.740
Now we've had our
presentation from the School

01:51:01.740 --> 01:51:02.880
of Architecture.

01:51:02.880 --> 01:51:07.200
And we are moving on to
the School of Engineering.

01:51:07.200 --> 01:51:10.190
There's probably no better
representative from the School

01:51:10.190 --> 01:51:15.550
of Engineering and representing
mens et manus, mind and hand,

01:51:15.550 --> 01:51:19.510
than Richard Polich,
class of 1965,

01:51:19.510 --> 01:51:22.490
who is president of the Tallix
Foundry for Arts Casting.

01:51:25.110 --> 01:51:27.680
At the Tallix
Foundry, sculptures,

01:51:27.680 --> 01:51:32.450
monumental sculptures, by many
of the best known artists have

01:51:32.450 --> 01:51:34.730
been casted, are being casted.

01:51:34.730 --> 01:51:38.900
Works by de Kooning, Stella,
Nancy Graves, Joel Shapiro,

01:51:38.900 --> 01:51:41.410
and many, many others.

01:51:41.410 --> 01:51:45.960
I think to describe
what Dick does there,

01:51:45.960 --> 01:51:48.620
I've been privileged
to be to Tallix twice.

01:51:48.620 --> 01:51:50.430
And I'd really like
to take all of you

01:51:50.430 --> 01:51:53.910
to Tallix to see
the foundry, but we

01:51:53.910 --> 01:51:57.080
will have Dick come and
show us pictures of it.

01:51:57.080 --> 01:51:59.160
Maybe next time we
should go to the Louvre

01:51:59.160 --> 01:52:01.628
and go to the foundry and--

01:52:01.628 --> 01:52:06.120
[APPLAUSE]

01:52:06.120 --> 01:52:09.630
But there was a profile about
Dick in the current Technology

01:52:09.630 --> 01:52:12.370
Review, which I hope that
you were able to see.

01:52:12.370 --> 01:52:16.870
And I want to quote
one passage from that.

01:52:16.870 --> 01:52:19.740
It says, "Dick is multilingual.

01:52:19.740 --> 01:52:26.390
He speaks structural engineer,
sculptor, and welder fluently."

01:52:26.390 --> 01:52:29.885
Please help me to
welcome Dick Polich.

01:52:29.885 --> 01:52:33.350
[APPLAUSE]

01:52:57.815 --> 01:52:58.940
POLICH: Well, good morning.

01:53:04.550 --> 01:53:07.400
We all need a little second
to let all that settle down.

01:53:07.400 --> 01:53:09.587
That was just a wonderful talk.

01:53:09.587 --> 01:53:13.840
And what I'm going to talk
about is a little bit different.

01:53:13.840 --> 01:53:16.820
I'm an engineer, a metallurgist.

01:53:16.820 --> 01:53:20.240
30 years ago I worked on a
project with Merton Flemings,

01:53:20.240 --> 01:53:23.770
head of the materials science
department, the goal of which

01:53:23.770 --> 01:53:29.680
modestly stated was to
unite technology and art.

01:53:29.680 --> 01:53:33.750
We did indeed have a
resident sculptor, Al Duca,

01:53:33.750 --> 01:53:36.480
who still lives here in Boston.

01:53:36.480 --> 01:53:41.510
We all worked together
to adapt and modify

01:53:41.510 --> 01:53:45.210
industrial techniques
to the making of art.

01:53:45.210 --> 01:53:47.890
24 years ago I started
Tallix, a place

01:53:47.890 --> 01:53:51.250
to make art, whose very name
comes from the material we

01:53:51.250 --> 01:53:53.910
deal with, metallics.

01:53:53.910 --> 01:53:55.920
In that 24 years,
Tallix has grown

01:53:55.920 --> 01:53:58.810
to be the largest caster
fabricator in the world,

01:53:58.810 --> 01:54:03.420
with over 100 people working
in 85,000 square feet

01:54:03.420 --> 01:54:07.406
making art for artists
all over the world.

01:54:07.406 --> 01:54:09.280
In fact, in the past
year we've installed art

01:54:09.280 --> 01:54:14.880
in Luxembourg; Munster, Germany;
Rome; Vancouver; and Tokyo.

01:54:14.880 --> 01:54:18.280
My hope today is to
accomplish three things.

01:54:18.280 --> 01:54:20.720
First, to give you a tour
of the foundry and show you

01:54:20.720 --> 01:54:23.110
what we do.

01:54:23.110 --> 01:54:26.530
Secondly, to talk about a
specific problem for artists,

01:54:26.530 --> 01:54:31.300
going from a small maquette
to a monument, the problem

01:54:31.300 --> 01:54:32.840
of dealing with scale.

01:54:32.840 --> 01:54:35.930
And then finally, end
up with a discussion

01:54:35.930 --> 01:54:41.260
of how artists, engineers,
and fabricators work together.

01:54:41.260 --> 01:54:43.100
So let's begin.

01:54:43.100 --> 01:54:45.130
The artist who wants
to make metal sculpture

01:54:45.130 --> 01:54:48.580
needs to find at least
four things, space, tools,

01:54:48.580 --> 01:54:50.800
technology, and craft.

01:54:50.800 --> 01:54:54.120
The artist will always
make art, but where

01:54:54.120 --> 01:54:57.930
he makes that art,
the place he picks,

01:54:57.930 --> 01:55:03.291
will affect how the art
looks and how it is made.

01:55:03.291 --> 01:55:04.540
Let's look at the first slide.

01:55:07.630 --> 01:55:09.152
Is there a pointer here?

01:55:09.152 --> 01:55:10.920
CREW: Yes, right on there.

01:55:10.920 --> 01:55:12.250
Underneath the equipment.

01:55:12.250 --> 01:55:13.041
POLICH: This is it.

01:55:15.860 --> 01:55:18.250
Okay, space we have plenty of.

01:55:18.250 --> 01:55:21.330
We have about
85,000 square feet.

01:55:21.330 --> 01:55:23.640
We have 46-foot ceilings.

01:55:23.640 --> 01:55:27.176
The next slide.

01:55:27.176 --> 01:55:28.450
Let me see now.

01:55:31.330 --> 01:55:32.650
Not sure where that is.

01:55:32.650 --> 01:55:33.370
Oh, there we go.

01:55:33.370 --> 01:55:34.530
Thank you.

01:55:34.530 --> 01:55:37.900
The next slide shows
the main bay of Tallix.

01:55:37.900 --> 01:55:40.620
We have some Frank
Stella stuff over here.

01:55:40.620 --> 01:55:42.980
There's a Lichtenstein
over there.

01:55:42.980 --> 01:55:44.980
That's Audrey Flack.

01:55:44.980 --> 01:55:48.400
That's a stainless steel
sculpture by Jeff Koons.

01:55:48.400 --> 01:55:50.740
The scale of the place.

01:55:50.740 --> 01:55:54.530
It's about 65 feet across
here and about 300 feet down

01:55:54.530 --> 01:55:56.460
to the other end of the foundry.

01:55:56.460 --> 01:56:00.410
The cranes have about 35
foot clear under the hook

01:56:00.410 --> 01:56:07.220
and can lift up
to 100,000 pounds.

01:56:07.220 --> 01:56:08.860
Tools also we have plenty of.

01:56:08.860 --> 01:56:11.560
The next slide.

01:56:11.560 --> 01:56:15.080
Here you see us out in the
yard assembling a sculpture

01:56:15.080 --> 01:56:17.220
by Alex Lieberman.

01:56:17.220 --> 01:56:21.230
It's a Cor-Ten steel sculpture.

01:56:21.230 --> 01:56:24.950
Its dimensions are
50 by 50 by 40 feet.

01:56:24.950 --> 01:56:27.580
It's an extraordinary
sculpture in that you

01:56:27.580 --> 01:56:32.590
can walk inside this
sculpture, and it's

01:56:32.590 --> 01:56:36.610
very unusual in that way.

01:56:36.610 --> 01:56:37.790
The next slide.

01:56:40.710 --> 01:56:42.770
We show some technology.

01:56:42.770 --> 01:56:47.060
This is a sculpture that's in
the National Gallery, a quite

01:56:47.060 --> 01:56:48.862
famous sculpture.

01:56:48.862 --> 01:56:51.080
And its surface is terrible.

01:56:51.080 --> 01:56:54.890
And this is one of
the things that we

01:56:54.890 --> 01:56:59.820
have worked on at Tallix to help
sculptors achieve their goals.

01:56:59.820 --> 01:57:00.600
The next slide.

01:57:03.240 --> 01:57:07.800
These are the grain sizes of
three different cast bronze

01:57:07.800 --> 01:57:08.300
alloys.

01:57:08.300 --> 01:57:13.210
And while I was at MIT, I
did work on bearing steels.

01:57:13.210 --> 01:57:17.460
And it turns out
that the same kinds

01:57:17.460 --> 01:57:20.120
of things that make
better bearing steels also

01:57:20.120 --> 01:57:22.460
make better surfaces
for sculptors.

01:57:22.460 --> 01:57:26.910
And so on the next
slide, you see

01:57:26.910 --> 01:57:31.460
some of the stuff
we have done where

01:57:31.460 --> 01:57:36.360
the cosmetic appearance of
the surface, the perfection,

01:57:36.360 --> 01:57:39.170
is so vital to what the artist
is trying to get across.

01:57:42.050 --> 01:57:45.830
In the next slide you see craft.

01:57:45.830 --> 01:57:54.280
I mean, this kind of story is
really the story of Tallix.

01:57:54.280 --> 01:57:59.680
We have analyzed and
identified those interactions

01:57:59.680 --> 01:58:04.790
between artists and craftsmen,
where they are really

01:58:04.790 --> 01:58:07.550
working together to make art.

01:58:07.550 --> 01:58:12.450
We have also got processes,
steps in the process,

01:58:12.450 --> 01:58:16.391
that really are very little
different than making an engine

01:58:16.391 --> 01:58:16.890
block.

01:58:16.890 --> 01:58:19.950
And so we have tried to
separate those processes,

01:58:19.950 --> 01:58:23.250
mechanize them, use
the latest techniques,

01:58:23.250 --> 01:58:28.390
and try to keep this marriage
of technology and craft

01:58:28.390 --> 01:58:31.420
going along.

01:58:31.420 --> 01:58:36.340
In the next slide
you see a triptych

01:58:36.340 --> 01:58:38.280
by Helen Frankenthaler.

01:58:38.280 --> 01:58:41.170
And these really
are lithographs set

01:58:41.170 --> 01:58:44.130
in a cast, a
stainless steel frame.

01:58:44.130 --> 01:58:47.380
These are about 7 feet high.

01:58:47.380 --> 01:58:50.660
One of the problems
in this sculpture

01:58:50.660 --> 01:58:52.780
was what to do with
the back sides.

01:58:52.780 --> 01:58:56.450
And we tried putting
fabric and putting

01:58:56.450 --> 01:58:59.780
wood and various
other kinds of things.

01:58:59.780 --> 01:59:03.960
And then-- the next
slide, please--

01:59:03.960 --> 01:59:07.580
we went to using patinas.

01:59:07.580 --> 01:59:10.870
And patinas are very
painterly kinds of things.

01:59:10.870 --> 01:59:13.720
And you use brushes
and solutions.

01:59:13.720 --> 01:59:17.550
The solutions are chemical, but
the techniques are the same.

01:59:17.550 --> 01:59:25.890
In the next slide you see Helen
working on the bronze backs

01:59:25.890 --> 01:59:28.300
that went onto the triptychs.

01:59:28.300 --> 01:59:35.150
And this process is very
much like using watercolors.

01:59:35.150 --> 01:59:37.500
The colors are transparent.

01:59:37.500 --> 01:59:39.490
They blend into each other.

01:59:39.490 --> 01:59:46.260
And there's ample opportunity
for accidents, happy accidents,

01:59:46.260 --> 01:59:47.620
to occur.

01:59:47.620 --> 01:59:52.270
In the next slide you
see one of the triptychs.

01:59:52.270 --> 01:59:55.450
And this now is the
back side, and you

01:59:55.450 --> 01:59:58.870
can see the incredible
designs and colors

01:59:58.870 --> 02:00:03.470
that she has done for
the back of the screens.

02:00:03.470 --> 02:00:08.090
In the next slide
is another one.

02:00:08.090 --> 02:00:12.785
And then in the next slide
again another design.

02:00:12.785 --> 02:00:13.550
And colors.

02:00:13.550 --> 02:00:23.400
And I must say that at the
exhibit where these were shown,

02:00:23.400 --> 02:00:27.130
only one of the triptychs had
the lithographs facing out,

02:00:27.130 --> 02:00:29.700
and then the lithographs
were all facing the wall

02:00:29.700 --> 02:00:31.090
as they showed all the backs.

02:00:31.090 --> 02:00:35.020
And so there were some happy
foundrymen at that show.

02:00:37.850 --> 02:00:46.210
In the next slide, this again
is a little bit of technology

02:00:46.210 --> 02:00:48.170
and a lot of craft.

02:00:48.170 --> 02:00:50.900
One of the things
that is going on here

02:00:50.900 --> 02:00:55.950
is that we are brush plating
gold onto the bronze.

02:00:55.950 --> 02:00:58.780
What happens then is
that the patinas will not

02:00:58.780 --> 02:01:01.840
take on the gold, and
so the artist gets

02:01:01.840 --> 02:01:08.240
this kind of extraordinary
transition from here, a green,

02:01:08.240 --> 02:01:11.120
into the rich goldy color.

02:01:11.120 --> 02:01:14.610
I think the next slide
shows a-- oh, no.

02:01:14.610 --> 02:01:20.930
This is another case
of craft coming through

02:01:20.930 --> 02:01:22.760
to help the artist.

02:01:22.760 --> 02:01:29.250
The woman there is Mary Frank,
who does clay sculpture.

02:01:29.250 --> 02:01:34.520
One of the things that
really troubled Mary was

02:01:34.520 --> 02:01:38.380
that she wanted to do
marble, and, of course, fired

02:01:38.380 --> 02:01:44.670
clay is very fragile and the
possibilities for putting it

02:01:44.670 --> 02:01:47.380
outdoors are limited.

02:01:47.380 --> 02:01:50.250
She wanted to do
metal sculpture,

02:01:50.250 --> 02:01:55.460
but the surfaces were
hard and just reflective

02:01:55.460 --> 02:01:56.460
and really bothered her.

02:01:56.460 --> 02:01:58.860
And so we worked
out this approach

02:01:58.860 --> 02:02:01.340
where we got a kind
of matte surface

02:02:01.340 --> 02:02:04.495
with a patina that came
close to the original clays.

02:02:07.940 --> 02:02:15.190
In the next slide I'm going
to now talk about some

02:02:15.190 --> 02:02:17.190
of the problems with scale.

02:02:17.190 --> 02:02:22.320
These are 35-foot figures
in Rosslyn outside

02:02:22.320 --> 02:02:24.390
of Washington, DC.

02:02:24.390 --> 02:02:28.930
It's what you see as you
come up to this building.

02:02:28.930 --> 02:02:32.950
The thing that goes on
when an artist is faced

02:02:32.950 --> 02:02:38.000
with making a
large-scale monument,

02:02:38.000 --> 02:02:41.010
and, as Professor
Morrison talked about,

02:02:41.010 --> 02:02:43.780
the hands and the
eye working together.

02:02:43.780 --> 02:02:48.210
Artists invariably work at a
size somewhere between a foot

02:02:48.210 --> 02:02:50.170
and 3 feet, something like that.

02:02:50.170 --> 02:02:52.890
I think the hands and
the eyes coordinate well,

02:02:52.890 --> 02:02:54.740
and that's the size.

02:02:54.740 --> 02:02:57.100
Now you've got to make
something, though,

02:02:57.100 --> 02:02:59.640
that can be outdoors, something
that can go in the world.

02:02:59.640 --> 02:03:01.670
And the world is a big place.

02:03:01.670 --> 02:03:04.720
Well, typically, if I took
a piece of paper, crushed it

02:03:04.720 --> 02:03:08.030
in my hand, and threw
it on the table,

02:03:08.030 --> 02:03:13.100
you would sense the force
that had crushed it.

02:03:13.100 --> 02:03:16.350
You would see the folds, the
lines, the kind of energy

02:03:16.350 --> 02:03:20.760
that's in that piece of
paper, in that crumpled paper.

02:03:20.760 --> 02:03:23.900
I now take it, and
an artist says, well,

02:03:23.900 --> 02:03:26.360
make it 10 feet by 10 feet.

02:03:26.360 --> 02:03:31.340
What happens is that somehow now
all of those folds and creases

02:03:31.340 --> 02:03:33.970
and lines have moved apart.

02:03:33.970 --> 02:03:39.360
And there's a lot of information
that has to be put back in

02:03:39.360 --> 02:03:40.870
to make that work.

02:03:40.870 --> 02:03:44.060
And what is possible
to have happen

02:03:44.060 --> 02:03:52.540
is that this large model, it
becomes an imitation thing

02:03:52.540 --> 02:03:55.370
and it really doesn't work.

02:03:55.370 --> 02:04:00.370
In this case-- the
next slide, please-- we

02:04:00.370 --> 02:04:03.310
were taking as a starting
point a painting,

02:04:03.310 --> 02:04:08.270
and we're working with kind of
big geometric simple shapes.

02:04:08.270 --> 02:04:11.350
The next slide.

02:04:11.350 --> 02:04:14.740
We have here again in
the bay of the foundry,

02:04:14.740 --> 02:04:17.900
and we are making
this structure.

02:04:17.900 --> 02:04:21.430
We've got the problem
of hurricane winds.

02:04:21.430 --> 02:04:25.520
You'll see that it's quite
delicately poised on its toes,

02:04:25.520 --> 02:04:28.880
so we have to put a lot
of structure inside this.

02:04:28.880 --> 02:04:30.900
The next slide, please.

02:04:30.900 --> 02:04:33.020
Here we are in the main
bay of the foundry.

02:04:33.020 --> 02:04:35.990
The figure is standing
together for the first time.

02:04:35.990 --> 02:04:38.090
You can see all of
these are really

02:04:38.090 --> 02:04:42.380
weld points where the surface
skin is welded to the structure

02:04:42.380 --> 02:04:43.450
inside.

02:04:43.450 --> 02:04:48.170
And there indeed is a copy
of the original painting.

02:04:48.170 --> 02:04:52.090
And that is the artist
admiring her work.

02:04:52.090 --> 02:04:54.440
The next slide.

02:04:54.440 --> 02:04:56.880
This is what you see as
you leave the building.

02:04:56.880 --> 02:05:03.680
And I think this is just
a very successful project.

02:05:03.680 --> 02:05:07.670
It's a wonderful kind of
thing to see coming and going.

02:05:07.670 --> 02:05:13.220
In the next slide we have a
sculpture by Roy Lichtenstein.

02:05:13.220 --> 02:05:16.130
And this is a brush stroke.

02:05:16.130 --> 02:05:20.380
The next slide shows
this sculpture at,

02:05:20.380 --> 02:05:22.870
I think it's 22
feet in the foundry.

02:05:22.870 --> 02:05:26.800
And then the next slide shows it
going into a building in Paris,

02:05:26.800 --> 02:05:28.600
of all places.

02:05:28.600 --> 02:05:34.070
But again, you can see that
they're big, simple forms.

02:05:34.070 --> 02:05:37.460
The colors are well defined.

02:05:37.460 --> 02:05:42.640
And this sculpture goes up to
a larger scale quite readily.

02:05:42.640 --> 02:05:43.480
The next slide.

02:05:46.090 --> 02:05:48.750
This is a
two-dimensional painting

02:05:48.750 --> 02:05:51.250
by an artist named
Richard Pousette-Dart.

02:05:51.250 --> 02:05:55.850
And I was really
quite amazed to see

02:05:55.850 --> 02:05:59.800
in one of Mr.
Pei's slides, which

02:05:59.800 --> 02:06:03.600
showed a topographic
map of Paris,

02:06:03.600 --> 02:06:05.130
how this related to that.

02:06:05.130 --> 02:06:08.950
It seems close to me.

02:06:08.950 --> 02:06:13.110
One of the things-- I don't
know if that was Mr. Dart's, Mr.

02:06:13.110 --> 02:06:14.970
Pousette-Dart's inspiration.

02:06:14.970 --> 02:06:18.920
But one of the
things that we had

02:06:18.920 --> 02:06:21.490
to do now in consultation
with the artist

02:06:21.490 --> 02:06:24.120
was to figure out what's
up and what's down.

02:06:24.120 --> 02:06:26.170
How do we make this
three dimensional?

02:06:26.170 --> 02:06:30.020
And so we made it
into a clay model.

02:06:30.020 --> 02:06:32.820
Next slide, please.

02:06:32.820 --> 02:06:35.300
And you can see now, this
is the modeling clay.

02:06:35.300 --> 02:06:39.390
It's gone from a painting about,
I think it was 5 feet by 6 feet

02:06:39.390 --> 02:06:41.630
to a 12 by 12 door.

02:06:41.630 --> 02:06:49.470
This door was for the museum at
the Indianapolis Museum of Art.

02:06:49.470 --> 02:06:53.370
And in the next slide
you see the door

02:06:53.370 --> 02:06:55.980
set in a limestone wall.

02:06:55.980 --> 02:07:00.650
The next slide shows the
inside of the building,

02:07:00.650 --> 02:07:03.890
and the door is
pivoted in the middle,

02:07:03.890 --> 02:07:08.460
and so it rotates
on a central hinge.

02:07:08.460 --> 02:07:13.590
And I think we have one more
slide, which shows again

02:07:13.590 --> 02:07:15.140
the door set in the building.

02:07:15.140 --> 02:07:19.520
Again, I think a very
successful enlargement.

02:07:19.520 --> 02:07:22.480
Next slide.

02:07:22.480 --> 02:07:26.560
This is a sculpture
by Tom Otterness,

02:07:26.560 --> 02:07:28.330
which went to Germany.

02:07:28.330 --> 02:07:29.470
Here is the maquette.

02:07:29.470 --> 02:07:34.170
This is about 3 feet high, and
we're enlarging it to 8 meters.

02:07:34.170 --> 02:07:37.790
The construction
here is quite simple,

02:07:37.790 --> 02:07:43.680
stainless steel tubes with
bronze elements inside them.

02:07:43.680 --> 02:07:46.620
The next slide, please.

02:07:46.620 --> 02:07:49.820
Here you see it in the
main bay of the foundry

02:07:49.820 --> 02:07:51.020
at its full height.

02:07:51.020 --> 02:07:53.490
I think that is the model there.

02:07:53.490 --> 02:07:57.630
And again, here
is the sculpture.

02:07:57.630 --> 02:08:02.680
Again, this sculpture
lent itself readily

02:08:02.680 --> 02:08:04.970
to being enlarged.

02:08:04.970 --> 02:08:07.680
Next slide.

02:08:07.680 --> 02:08:10.940
Here we are in Washington,
and what's going on here

02:08:10.940 --> 02:08:13.790
is that this is the
installation of the Joel Shapiro

02:08:13.790 --> 02:08:18.000
sculpture at the Holocaust
Museum in Washington.

02:08:18.000 --> 02:08:21.740
This sculpture was 23 feet high.

02:08:21.740 --> 02:08:27.560
The next slide shows the
sculpture in the foundry.

02:08:27.560 --> 02:08:30.426
Shapiro uses wooden forms.

02:08:33.800 --> 02:08:35.750
The sculptures are
almost minimum.

02:08:35.750 --> 02:08:39.910
They're these simple wooden
forms that go together.

02:08:39.910 --> 02:08:45.360
This is a figure that is a
double figure that represents

02:08:45.360 --> 02:08:48.780
a kind of resurrection theme.

02:08:48.780 --> 02:08:57.350
You see here the actual size
of the elements that we cast.

02:08:57.350 --> 02:09:00.920
There was a real
problem here in that,

02:09:00.920 --> 02:09:04.390
when the sculpture
got this big and we

02:09:04.390 --> 02:09:07.900
used wooden beams
to cast off of,

02:09:07.900 --> 02:09:10.600
the wood grain did not carry.

02:09:10.600 --> 02:09:18.170
And Shapiro finally went back
to the sawmill and dinged one

02:09:18.170 --> 02:09:21.570
of the teeth of the saw and
then ran it through the sawmill

02:09:21.570 --> 02:09:24.490
and left a pattern
that was strong enough

02:09:24.490 --> 02:09:27.230
to come through at this scale.

02:09:27.230 --> 02:09:33.520
The next slide shows Joel--
that's Joel the artist there

02:09:33.520 --> 02:09:37.810
now-- adjusting
locating the sculpture.

02:09:37.810 --> 02:09:40.570
And this is just
interesting that it

02:09:40.570 --> 02:09:45.580
shows the kind of involvement
that goes on between artist

02:09:45.580 --> 02:09:49.720
and fabricator engineer.

02:09:49.720 --> 02:09:52.580
Next slide.

02:09:52.580 --> 02:09:55.750
This is a sculpture
by Gifford Proctor.

02:09:55.750 --> 02:09:59.090
And this sculpture is
about 30 inches high.

02:09:59.090 --> 02:10:04.210
And it's Washington at
Valley Forge, very dramatic.

02:10:04.210 --> 02:10:06.330
It was on its way to 20 feet.

02:10:06.330 --> 02:10:14.030
In the next slide you
see the enlarging machine

02:10:14.030 --> 02:10:14.930
at the foundry.

02:10:14.930 --> 02:10:17.620
This is a pointing device.

02:10:17.620 --> 02:10:21.630
The original sits on this table.

02:10:21.630 --> 02:10:25.520
The thing you're making
in clay works over here.

02:10:25.520 --> 02:10:26.375
There are two styli.

02:10:29.130 --> 02:10:34.730
The center line of the tables
and the center line of this arm

02:10:34.730 --> 02:10:36.340
is on the same point.

02:10:36.340 --> 02:10:37.820
There's a pivot back there.

02:10:37.820 --> 02:10:40.790
And so this makes
one similar triangle

02:10:40.790 --> 02:10:42.320
that's similar to this.

02:10:42.320 --> 02:10:44.800
So if this distance is
one and this is five,

02:10:44.800 --> 02:10:47.640
you can get a five
times magnification.

02:10:47.640 --> 02:10:50.610
This technology goes
back to the Renaissance.

02:10:50.610 --> 02:11:02.040
So it's being displaced by
lasers and devices like that.

02:11:02.040 --> 02:11:05.320
But computers
[CLEARING THROAT] excuse me.

02:11:05.320 --> 02:11:10.670
Computer-driven cutting
machines at this scale,

02:11:10.670 --> 02:11:14.060
it's just very difficult. The
equipment is so expensive.

02:11:14.060 --> 02:11:16.730
Next slide.

02:11:16.730 --> 02:11:18.480
Here we have the body.

02:11:18.480 --> 02:11:20.160
These are Washington's elbows.

02:11:20.160 --> 02:11:23.330
And what happens
now is we really

02:11:23.330 --> 02:11:25.670
start to get into
trouble because we've

02:11:25.670 --> 02:11:30.490
got this enormous expanse
of fabric or cloth

02:11:30.490 --> 02:11:35.540
or whatever it is, and
it's a real problem

02:11:35.540 --> 02:11:38.890
as to what to do to keep
the sculpture interesting,

02:11:38.890 --> 02:11:41.870
to keep your eye moving.

02:11:41.870 --> 02:11:45.520
This sculpture was really
never cast at that scale.

02:11:45.520 --> 02:11:48.440
Let's go on to the
next slide, please.

02:11:48.440 --> 02:11:51.380
Here we have an artist,
Willem de Kooning.

02:11:51.380 --> 02:11:53.730
And de Kooning did
this sculpture,

02:11:53.730 --> 02:11:56.700
which is 9 inches
high, and then it

02:11:56.700 --> 02:12:01.170
was enlarged four times to
3 feet and then four times

02:12:01.170 --> 02:12:04.300
to 12 feet.

02:12:04.300 --> 02:12:08.800
In the next slide,
the kind of technique

02:12:08.800 --> 02:12:12.150
that de Kooning was
using was really

02:12:12.150 --> 02:12:14.300
working these
things in his hand,

02:12:14.300 --> 02:12:16.280
squeezing clay in his hand.

02:12:16.280 --> 02:12:19.900
And you can see
in the next slide,

02:12:19.900 --> 02:12:25.860
you can see this kind of very
tactile thing that's going on.

02:12:25.860 --> 02:12:31.100
In the next slide again,
you see it even better.

02:12:31.100 --> 02:12:35.040
Now these sculptures, when
they were first enlarged,

02:12:35.040 --> 02:12:37.790
the first enlargements
were really

02:12:37.790 --> 02:12:43.820
scrapped because the
information from there

02:12:43.820 --> 02:12:47.240
to there was not there,
and the artist had

02:12:47.240 --> 02:12:52.500
to come back and work very
closely with the enlarger

02:12:52.500 --> 02:12:57.620
to get something that
kept your eye moving, that

02:12:57.620 --> 02:12:59.410
kept your interest.

02:12:59.410 --> 02:13:00.530
The next slide, please.

02:13:03.840 --> 02:13:06.000
This is Frank Stella.

02:13:06.000 --> 02:13:12.640
And Frank Stella
is certainly one

02:13:12.640 --> 02:13:16.150
of the top artists of our time.

02:13:16.150 --> 02:13:18.809
And Stella's here
working on a clay.

02:13:18.809 --> 02:13:20.600
And he looks like he's
working with a rake.

02:13:20.600 --> 02:13:22.430
Well, he is working with a rake.

02:13:22.430 --> 02:13:31.310
And Stella is a really
cerebral, available guy

02:13:31.310 --> 02:13:36.460
who will discuss with you and
tell you what his problems are.

02:13:36.460 --> 02:13:39.830
And he's got a clay here.

02:13:39.830 --> 02:13:44.480
The total clay is
about 12 feet across,

02:13:44.480 --> 02:13:47.770
and he's trying to make
a mark on it that's

02:13:47.770 --> 02:13:49.310
appropriate to this scale.

02:13:49.310 --> 02:13:57.072
And Stella feels that the
hands are too small, too weak.

02:13:57.072 --> 02:14:01.540
The gestures they
make are too timid.

02:14:01.540 --> 02:14:07.210
I mean, what he's looking for
is something that will show

02:14:07.210 --> 02:14:09.760
force and movement and energy.

02:14:09.760 --> 02:14:13.700
And so I've actually
seen him go in there

02:14:13.700 --> 02:14:15.860
and jump on the
clay with both feet.

02:14:15.860 --> 02:14:19.190
I mean, it's a real problem.

02:14:19.190 --> 02:14:24.540
And in the next slide
you see how Stella

02:14:24.540 --> 02:14:28.360
is starting to approach it.

02:14:28.360 --> 02:14:35.140
This is part of a sculpture,
which you'll see in a minute.

02:14:35.140 --> 02:14:41.010
It's about 25 feet
high, this one element.

02:14:41.010 --> 02:14:49.580
And in it we have an
enormous number of new forms,

02:14:49.580 --> 02:14:54.630
of new surfaces, of
things that Frank

02:14:54.630 --> 02:14:58.120
is trying to do to
keep you interested,

02:14:58.120 --> 02:15:04.280
to have a surface that's dynamic
and changing and interesting.

02:15:04.280 --> 02:15:09.035
In the next slide,
here's another sculpture,

02:15:09.035 --> 02:15:11.160
and I'm going to talk a
little bit more about this.

02:15:11.160 --> 02:15:14.415
But this dumpster is actually
part of the sculpture.

02:15:14.415 --> 02:15:18.365
[LAUGHTER]

02:15:18.365 --> 02:15:18.865
I agree.

02:15:22.620 --> 02:15:25.310
But I'm going to talk
about that, about where

02:15:25.310 --> 02:15:26.730
an artist gets his images.

02:15:26.730 --> 02:15:30.125
And one of the
things that Tallix is

02:15:30.125 --> 02:15:33.210
is a resource image
bank for artists.

02:15:33.210 --> 02:15:36.690
And almost everything
we use in the foundry,

02:15:36.690 --> 02:15:40.400
Frank has put in one
sculpture or another.

02:15:40.400 --> 02:15:43.320
The next slide.

02:15:43.320 --> 02:15:45.730
This is another sculpture.

02:15:45.730 --> 02:15:51.480
And these are also
painterly things.

02:15:51.480 --> 02:15:54.740
I mean, Stella started
out as a painter.

02:15:54.740 --> 02:15:58.610
And a painter can take
a 12-inch-wide brush

02:15:58.610 --> 02:16:05.420
and go in and cut out
big parts of his painting

02:16:05.420 --> 02:16:06.680
by overpainting.

02:16:06.680 --> 02:16:11.670
And Stella is looking for
that same kind of energy

02:16:11.670 --> 02:16:13.750
and possibility for change.

02:16:13.750 --> 02:16:14.991
The next slide, please.

02:16:18.582 --> 02:16:19.540
This is that sculpture.

02:16:22.570 --> 02:16:24.165
One reviewer described
the sculpture

02:16:24.165 --> 02:16:28.240
as looking like
an airplane crash.

02:16:28.240 --> 02:16:34.230
And that's funny in a
way, but it's also true.

02:16:34.230 --> 02:16:41.469
I mean, Frank is
looking for gestures.

02:16:41.469 --> 02:16:49.230
And you take a big steel
tank, and you crush it,

02:16:49.230 --> 02:16:53.459
and so you put the history
of that event in there.

02:16:53.459 --> 02:16:57.440
And we respond to
it, and sense it.

02:16:57.440 --> 02:17:03.430
In the next slide, again,
some of these things that

02:17:03.430 --> 02:17:04.129
are going on.

02:17:04.129 --> 02:17:12.709
And then closer, in the next
slide, closer to my heart,

02:17:12.709 --> 02:17:16.709
here we are trying to
make one of these forms.

02:17:16.709 --> 02:17:22.010
And Stella is so
experimental and so open

02:17:22.010 --> 02:17:25.530
that I'm going to talk some
more about him in a minute.

02:17:25.530 --> 02:17:27.379
But here we are.

02:17:27.379 --> 02:17:30.379
He has assembled
this pile of junk.

02:17:30.379 --> 02:17:33.209
And sometimes there's
no other word for it.

02:17:33.209 --> 02:17:34.690
It really is a lot of junk.

02:17:34.690 --> 02:17:40.440
But we are pouring 5,000 pounds
of aluminum on this sculpture,

02:17:40.440 --> 02:17:44.454
on this stuff, to make
a new kind of form.

02:17:44.454 --> 02:17:48.490
I mean, this is a kind
of casting without molds.

02:17:48.490 --> 02:17:52.680
And what happens is, we
get directional patterns.

02:17:52.680 --> 02:17:54.639
The things get knit together.

02:17:54.639 --> 02:17:59.980
And we have here really
a new mode of expression.

02:17:59.980 --> 02:18:03.049
And again, going back to
Professor Morrison's talk

02:18:03.049 --> 02:18:08.139
about the real world,
this is very real world,

02:18:08.139 --> 02:18:10.920
but it's also dreamland.

02:18:10.920 --> 02:18:14.629
So I think that's
the last slide?

02:18:14.629 --> 02:18:17.500
No, I have some other slides.

02:18:17.500 --> 02:18:22.639
And again, going back to
Professor Morrison's talk.

02:18:22.639 --> 02:18:24.870
And we did not discuss
each other's talk.

02:18:24.870 --> 02:18:30.510
But here, one of the
things that really binds me

02:18:30.510 --> 02:18:36.280
as an engineer to the casting
process is the process itself.

02:18:36.280 --> 02:18:42.730
And in early times people who
worked with metals were shaman,

02:18:42.730 --> 02:18:45.490
they were magicians.

02:18:45.490 --> 02:18:49.660
I mean, after all, the first
metals, meteors from heaven

02:18:49.660 --> 02:18:54.540
and Mother Earth
disgorging gold nuggets.

02:18:54.540 --> 02:19:00.809
So metals have always had a
very powerful role in our lives.

02:19:00.809 --> 02:19:05.480
And these are ceramic
shells that we're

02:19:05.480 --> 02:19:07.090
going to fill with metal.

02:19:07.090 --> 02:19:11.110
And we preheat them because
they're so impermeable.

02:19:11.110 --> 02:19:14.820
And we have to keep the metal
molten long enough to fill out.

02:19:14.820 --> 02:19:19.120
The next slide shows
us pigging off-- not

02:19:19.120 --> 02:19:23.110
pigging off-- tapping off into
a crucible of molten steel.

02:19:23.110 --> 02:19:27.959
And then the next slide is
the actual casting process,

02:19:27.959 --> 02:19:31.719
which is violent and
wonderful and dangerous.

02:19:31.719 --> 02:19:34.430
Next slide.

02:19:34.430 --> 02:19:35.780
Again, more of that.

02:19:35.780 --> 02:19:41.430
And then the last slide is
the molds have been poured,

02:19:41.430 --> 02:19:42.799
and they're set aside to cool.

02:19:42.799 --> 02:19:48.280
And the next slide is
just entropy taking over

02:19:48.280 --> 02:19:51.690
and everything getting back
to the same temperature.

02:19:51.690 --> 02:19:54.730
OK, I think that's
it for the slides.

02:19:54.730 --> 02:19:58.310
I want now to talk a little bit.

02:19:58.310 --> 02:20:00.690
After now we've seen
how the foundry looks.

02:20:00.690 --> 02:20:03.060
I want to talk
about how it works.

02:20:03.060 --> 02:20:04.460
But first it should
be understood

02:20:04.460 --> 02:20:07.880
that the art being discussed
is limited to the plastic arts

02:20:07.880 --> 02:20:10.250
and sculpture in particular.

02:20:10.250 --> 02:20:13.670
We'll begin by making a clear
distinction between creating,

02:20:13.670 --> 02:20:16.610
fabricating, and engineering.

02:20:16.610 --> 02:20:18.550
Creating, while
difficult to understand,

02:20:18.550 --> 02:20:20.860
is fairly easy to describe.

02:20:20.860 --> 02:20:22.760
It's what the artist
does when he works,

02:20:22.760 --> 02:20:24.700
when he expresses himself.

02:20:24.700 --> 02:20:29.390
What it is not is something
made as a means to an end,

02:20:29.390 --> 02:20:32.150
something made to a
preconceived plan,

02:20:32.150 --> 02:20:36.900
or something made by imposing
a new form on a given material.

02:20:36.900 --> 02:20:39.740
In other words, art is made
deliberately and responsibly

02:20:39.740 --> 02:20:42.200
by people who know
what they're doing,

02:20:42.200 --> 02:20:43.980
even though they
don't know in advance

02:20:43.980 --> 02:20:45.840
what is going to come of it.

02:20:45.840 --> 02:20:47.360
To create a work
of art, the artist

02:20:47.360 --> 02:20:49.990
must have in him certain
unexpressed feelings

02:20:49.990 --> 02:20:52.960
and the wherewithal
to express them.

02:20:52.960 --> 02:20:55.160
Making a sculpture,
then, has two stages.

02:20:55.160 --> 02:20:57.550
Making the plan,
which is creating,

02:20:57.550 --> 02:21:00.430
and then imposing that
plan on certain materials,

02:21:00.430 --> 02:21:02.890
which is fabricating.

02:21:02.890 --> 02:21:05.760
Thus, when the engineer makes
a plan, he, like the artist,

02:21:05.760 --> 02:21:07.260
is creating.

02:21:07.260 --> 02:21:11.460
It is not art, however, because
it has a preconceived goal.

02:21:11.460 --> 02:21:16.000
Remember, in art you don't
have a preconceived problem

02:21:16.000 --> 02:21:17.090
to solve.

02:21:17.090 --> 02:21:19.230
You're trying to
express something.

02:21:19.230 --> 02:21:22.500
Sometimes the engineer, as
in my case, wears two hats.

02:21:22.500 --> 02:21:26.170
He makes the plan, but then he
also serves as the fabricator.

02:21:26.170 --> 02:21:28.130
Let's look at fabricating.

02:21:28.130 --> 02:21:31.880
The fabricator needs an
idea of what is to be made.

02:21:31.880 --> 02:21:35.700
Typically, the artist presents
an artifact in a plastic medium

02:21:35.700 --> 02:21:38.220
and wants it transformed
into metal, perhaps

02:21:38.220 --> 02:21:40.060
at a larger scale.

02:21:40.060 --> 02:21:43.000
In the simplest case, the
engineer's not needed at all.

02:21:43.000 --> 02:21:46.370
The deal is really between
artist and fabricator.

02:21:46.370 --> 02:21:49.550
In this case, the artist
has a very specific problem,

02:21:49.550 --> 02:21:52.340
which can be solved by the
application of an existing

02:21:52.340 --> 02:21:53.620
technology.

02:21:53.620 --> 02:21:56.110
So when does the artist
need the engineer?

02:21:56.110 --> 02:22:00.040
Well, under at least
five instances.

02:22:00.040 --> 02:22:02.070
No artist, and
probably no engineer,

02:22:02.070 --> 02:22:05.470
can be familiar with all the
material processes available

02:22:05.470 --> 02:22:08.710
and the number of materials
an artist can use.

02:22:08.710 --> 02:22:11.660
In my own case, I listen
to the artist's intention

02:22:11.660 --> 02:22:13.770
and then describe a
number of different ways,

02:22:13.770 --> 02:22:17.340
different processes, to
achieve the artist's goal.

02:22:17.340 --> 02:22:19.680
This conversation
often occurs very early

02:22:19.680 --> 02:22:22.880
in the process of
creating a work of art.

02:22:22.880 --> 02:22:26.270
Then there is the near
obsession to be novel,

02:22:26.270 --> 02:22:28.450
to do something
never done before.

02:22:28.450 --> 02:22:31.990
Casting and fabricating are
really fairly straightforward.

02:22:31.990 --> 02:22:34.820
New technologies
appear and are used,

02:22:34.820 --> 02:22:37.610
but for the most part
solutions of this type

02:22:37.610 --> 02:22:40.920
combine unusual or offbeat
ways of doing things.

02:22:40.920 --> 02:22:46.340
For example, casting aluminum or
bronze around stainless steel,

02:22:46.340 --> 02:22:48.490
stopping the anodizing
process at a point

02:22:48.490 --> 02:22:51.150
where the artist can paint
colors rather than dipping,

02:22:51.150 --> 02:22:54.700
using plating
techniques selectively,

02:22:54.700 --> 02:22:57.990
welding metals together with
different color welding rods.

02:22:57.990 --> 02:23:01.590
These suggestions sound simple,
but they can be of great use

02:23:01.590 --> 02:23:04.850
to an artist in achieving
a unique effect.

02:23:04.850 --> 02:23:06.730
Then there's the
exchange of information

02:23:06.730 --> 02:23:09.020
between artist and engineer.

02:23:09.020 --> 02:23:11.540
The artist wants to know
why something happened,

02:23:11.540 --> 02:23:14.850
what things can't or
won't work, and perhaps,

02:23:14.850 --> 02:23:18.080
most importantly, which
things might work.

02:23:18.080 --> 02:23:20.360
It is sometimes
difficult for an artist

02:23:20.360 --> 02:23:22.530
to explain what
he's trying to do,

02:23:22.530 --> 02:23:25.530
for it may not be
completely clear to himself.

02:23:25.530 --> 02:23:28.630
The engineer fabricator
must be a good listener,

02:23:28.630 --> 02:23:33.540
able to pay attention, and
intuit what the artist wants.

02:23:33.540 --> 02:23:35.120
Providing a source of imagery.

02:23:35.120 --> 02:23:38.500
Tallix is visually
a fantastic place.

02:23:38.500 --> 02:23:41.150
Artists come to Tallix to see
all the different work being

02:23:41.150 --> 02:23:43.320
made, feel the
energy of the place,

02:23:43.320 --> 02:23:46.200
and are enormously
stimulated and motivated

02:23:46.200 --> 02:23:49.210
to create new works.

02:23:49.210 --> 02:23:51.200
And finally, make it
all cost effective.

02:23:51.200 --> 02:23:55.050
In the end, everyone has
to deal with the costs.

02:23:55.050 --> 02:23:57.410
Let's return to the
fabricator who wants and needs

02:23:57.410 --> 02:23:59.860
an image of what is to be made.

02:23:59.860 --> 02:24:02.420
The artist as creator
sometimes does not

02:24:02.420 --> 02:24:04.850
have a strong sense
of how important it

02:24:04.850 --> 02:24:09.730
is for the fabricator to have
that final idea available.

02:24:09.730 --> 02:24:12.870
Minus it, confusion
and muttering result

02:24:12.870 --> 02:24:15.850
as artist and fabricator
slip past each other

02:24:15.850 --> 02:24:19.290
pursuing their common
but different goals.

02:24:19.290 --> 02:24:22.120
Fabricating is important to the
artist but in a different way

02:24:22.120 --> 02:24:23.760
than for the fabricator.

02:24:23.760 --> 02:24:26.430
Fabricating gives the
artist the opportunity

02:24:26.430 --> 02:24:30.170
to identify elements he may
want to use in his composition.

02:24:30.170 --> 02:24:33.360
The fabricator works from a
model, a drawing, a sketch,

02:24:33.360 --> 02:24:36.870
some kind of guide, while
the artist has none of these

02:24:36.870 --> 02:24:39.570
and only gets exact
when he's completed,

02:24:39.570 --> 02:24:42.150
when the sculpture is finished.

02:24:42.150 --> 02:24:45.200
Another sticking point in the
push-pull between fabricating

02:24:45.200 --> 02:24:48.140
and creating is that the
fabricator cannot provide

02:24:48.140 --> 02:24:51.430
the artist with
elements of imagery.

02:24:51.430 --> 02:24:54.940
Only the artist can decide
what images he wants.

02:24:54.940 --> 02:24:56.900
He may recognize
and use some images

02:24:56.900 --> 02:24:59.310
from the work of the
fabricator, but only

02:24:59.310 --> 02:25:02.580
after he has understood
what has been made can

02:25:02.580 --> 02:25:04.860
it have a place in his work.

02:25:04.860 --> 02:25:07.690
To summarize, the
artist is expressing

02:25:07.690 --> 02:25:11.030
an emotion, a feeling he is
not conscious of until it

02:25:11.030 --> 02:25:12.530
is expressed.

02:25:12.530 --> 02:25:15.070
The engineer participates
in the process

02:25:15.070 --> 02:25:18.440
by making a plan to accomplish
the artist's vision.

02:25:18.440 --> 02:25:21.700
The fabricator follows a
plan with a clear objective,

02:25:21.700 --> 02:25:23.245
an artifact as the goal.

02:25:25.930 --> 02:25:28.720
I want now to talk about
how different artists work.

02:25:28.720 --> 02:25:31.330
Most artists come to the
foundry looking for advice

02:25:31.330 --> 02:25:35.590
and consultation on how to
achieve a specific result.

02:25:35.590 --> 02:25:37.640
These artists have mostly
resolved their work

02:25:37.640 --> 02:25:40.350
in the studio and
have well-formed ideas

02:25:40.350 --> 02:25:41.720
of what they want.

02:25:41.720 --> 02:25:44.640
Often they will have discussed
with the foundry particular

02:25:44.640 --> 02:25:49.750
aspects of a project long
before it is completed.

02:25:49.750 --> 02:25:51.820
In fact, the
discussions often affect

02:25:51.820 --> 02:25:53.760
how the sculptures
are fabricated,

02:25:53.760 --> 02:25:57.470
as technical advice becomes
a compositional element which

02:25:57.470 --> 02:26:00.890
the artist may use or discard.

02:26:00.890 --> 02:26:04.910
Some typical foundry responses
for artists are as follows.

02:26:04.910 --> 02:26:07.860
Roy Lichtenstein,
the pop artist,

02:26:07.860 --> 02:26:10.580
wanted his sculpture to
have an industrial finish

02:26:10.580 --> 02:26:14.110
like a galvanized trashcan
to keep the pop image even

02:26:14.110 --> 02:26:18.230
while using the very permanent
and expensive process

02:26:18.230 --> 02:26:19.590
of casting.

02:26:19.590 --> 02:26:22.960
Nancy Graves loves color.

02:26:22.960 --> 02:26:25.710
Nancy comes to
sculpture as a painter.

02:26:25.710 --> 02:26:29.410
And so we did enameling
of the cast bronzes.

02:26:29.410 --> 02:26:32.930
And she also liked
direct casting.

02:26:32.930 --> 02:26:39.350
And so we figured out ways to
cast lobster claws and flowers

02:26:39.350 --> 02:26:43.060
and other flora and
fauna with her signature

02:26:43.060 --> 02:26:47.280
touch of fragility and
[? metastability. ?]

02:26:47.280 --> 02:26:48.660
For Richard
Prousette-Dart, we've

02:26:48.660 --> 02:26:51.940
already seen how we took
that two-dimensional painting

02:26:51.940 --> 02:26:56.370
and turned it into a 12
foot by 12 foot door.

02:26:56.370 --> 02:26:58.940
These are some of the things
we've done for various artists.

02:26:58.940 --> 02:27:00.836
But the artist I'm
going to focus on

02:27:00.836 --> 02:27:08.990
is the afore-- [AUDIO OUT] with
his assistant, Earl Childress,

02:27:08.990 --> 02:27:13.740
chooses to work directly
and at scale at the foundry.

02:27:13.740 --> 02:27:17.120
This is a bold, expensive,
complicated work.

02:27:17.120 --> 02:27:19.680
But what it does is give
Frank the possibility

02:27:19.680 --> 02:27:23.850
to assert artistic influence
at every possible stage

02:27:23.850 --> 02:27:27.380
of fabrication and
therefore clarifies how

02:27:27.380 --> 02:27:31.120
one artist can and does work.

02:27:31.120 --> 02:27:34.960
For Stella, a major goal is to
include as much information as

02:27:34.960 --> 02:27:38.050
possible in his work to
make it as rich and full

02:27:38.050 --> 02:27:39.910
of character as possible.

02:27:39.910 --> 02:27:42.790
He wants the complexity
of history and chance,

02:27:42.790 --> 02:27:46.020
and he is not bothered by
the source of the richness.

02:27:46.020 --> 02:27:49.390
For example, human beings
always bring the promise

02:27:49.390 --> 02:27:53.000
of enriching work, not
only by their actions

02:27:53.000 --> 02:27:55.400
but by accident as well.

02:27:55.400 --> 02:27:57.330
Frank will ask for
something to be done.

02:27:57.330 --> 02:28:01.170
Bend that piece of metal, cut
that piece into four elements.

02:28:01.170 --> 02:28:04.060
The logical question is, okay,
what angle is the bend to be?

02:28:04.060 --> 02:28:05.850
Do you want it symmetrical?

02:28:05.850 --> 02:28:08.350
Are the cut pieces
to be equal sizes?

02:28:08.350 --> 02:28:10.440
These questions
Frank never answers.

02:28:10.440 --> 02:28:12.830
He says, bend it, cut it.

02:28:12.830 --> 02:28:14.630
He's looking for richness.

02:28:14.630 --> 02:28:18.600
Frank is open to everyone's
ideas and suggestions, looking

02:28:18.600 --> 02:28:21.960
for that idea he could
never think of himself.

02:28:21.960 --> 02:28:23.930
Perhaps the cutting or
bending of the material

02:28:23.930 --> 02:28:25.930
will be done in some
new and interesting way.

02:28:25.930 --> 02:28:29.600
If it doesn't work, if it
isn't interesting, he dumps it.

02:28:29.600 --> 02:28:32.440
He will not compromise.

02:28:32.440 --> 02:28:34.930
Working for some artists
is like making a product.

02:28:34.930 --> 02:28:39.850
This group of artists is trying
to achieve a specific result.

02:28:39.850 --> 02:28:43.580
But Frank Stella is composing
images, building the sculpture,

02:28:43.580 --> 02:28:45.260
feeling it go together.

02:28:45.260 --> 02:28:48.590
When Frank is at work, making
images, accepting some,

02:28:48.590 --> 02:28:50.860
rejecting others,
there's a real sense

02:28:50.860 --> 02:28:53.520
of fitting the specific
elements to each other,

02:28:53.520 --> 02:28:57.120
making them feel right
as well as look right.

02:28:57.120 --> 02:29:00.310
Historicity of elements
is what Stella wants,

02:29:00.310 --> 02:29:03.100
not in the traditional
way but in a new way.

02:29:03.100 --> 02:29:05.760
The old way was go to the
studio and do trial and error

02:29:05.760 --> 02:29:07.000
experiments.

02:29:07.000 --> 02:29:08.900
Now there are too many
possibilities, too

02:29:08.900 --> 02:29:10.360
many complexities.

02:29:10.360 --> 02:29:13.830
The artist needs help to
understand the possibilities,

02:29:13.830 --> 02:29:17.180
so he goes to a
fabricator like Tallix.

02:29:17.180 --> 02:29:19.930
Even so, Stella is very unusual.

02:29:19.930 --> 02:29:22.840
In his quest for richness,
he is eager to use

02:29:22.840 --> 02:29:27.300
any possible imagery the
process of fabricating offers.

02:29:27.300 --> 02:29:30.100
This is not what
most artists seek.

02:29:30.100 --> 02:29:32.530
Frank thinks that the
process of testing

02:29:32.530 --> 02:29:34.280
does not give people ideas.

02:29:34.280 --> 02:29:36.820
People get ideas by thinking.

02:29:36.820 --> 02:29:40.675
Frank says, if the artist has
a novel idea, he feels good.

02:29:40.675 --> 02:29:43.690
If he has an original
idea, he is happy.

02:29:43.690 --> 02:29:45.740
People value expression.

02:29:45.740 --> 02:29:47.360
They don't have enough of it.

02:29:47.360 --> 02:29:49.360
Art gives back
something, a notion

02:29:49.360 --> 02:29:52.000
of the nature of the
material, of casting,

02:29:52.000 --> 02:29:56.640
and man using tools to
shape and form materials.

02:29:56.640 --> 02:29:59.980
A sense of all the forces,
a lot of them at work.

02:29:59.980 --> 02:30:02.570
To make something
is to act like God.

02:30:02.570 --> 02:30:06.910
To make, you must use
force and your mind.

02:30:06.910 --> 02:30:08.680
One of the big
differences for Stella

02:30:08.680 --> 02:30:11.950
between art and engineering is
the value of an original idea.

02:30:14.520 --> 02:30:16.670
An idea in art is
only good once.

02:30:16.670 --> 02:30:18.640
Thereafter, it is repetition.

02:30:18.640 --> 02:30:20.290
But a good engineering
solution can

02:30:20.290 --> 02:30:23.550
be used many times over
and over until a better

02:30:23.550 --> 02:30:25.840
solution presents itself.

02:30:25.840 --> 02:30:27.710
Sometimes engineers
feel artists are

02:30:27.710 --> 02:30:30.080
capricious and
self-indulgent when

02:30:30.080 --> 02:30:32.410
really what they
are responding to

02:30:32.410 --> 02:30:37.570
is this fact that they
must avoid repetition.

02:30:37.570 --> 02:30:39.550
What are some of the ways
Stella works that can

02:30:39.550 --> 02:30:41.700
be applied to engineering?

02:30:41.700 --> 02:30:45.250
For starters, take his openness
to ideas other than his own

02:30:45.250 --> 02:30:47.630
and his own quest for richness.

02:30:47.630 --> 02:30:49.830
Stella doesn't care
where ideas come from.

02:30:49.830 --> 02:30:52.120
He wants work rich in ideas.

02:30:52.120 --> 02:30:55.350
To some, this might sound
like plagiarism, but it isn't.

02:30:55.350 --> 02:30:56.780
There's a difference
between using

02:30:56.780 --> 02:30:59.320
what exists as a
springboard for your work

02:30:59.320 --> 02:31:01.150
than as the basis for your work.

02:31:01.150 --> 02:31:04.180
In my personal experience
over the past 10 years,

02:31:04.180 --> 02:31:06.070
I can report that
everything we use

02:31:06.070 --> 02:31:09.840
at the foundry-- tools,
cutting tables, work benches,

02:31:09.840 --> 02:31:13.740
dumpsters, drops; that's what's
left after a form has been

02:31:13.740 --> 02:31:16.060
cut out of a metal
sheet-- all of it

02:31:16.060 --> 02:31:18.260
included in Frank's imagery.

02:31:18.260 --> 02:31:20.730
Of course, the ultimate drop
is another artist's scrap

02:31:20.730 --> 02:31:23.600
sculpture, which, of
course, no one can use,

02:31:23.600 --> 02:31:25.340
but it is tempting.

02:31:25.340 --> 02:31:28.060
For what could be a better
foil, a better alternative view,

02:31:28.060 --> 02:31:31.460
than another artist's sculpture?

02:31:31.460 --> 02:31:34.380
Next is Frank's effort
to stay uncommitted,

02:31:34.380 --> 02:31:37.840
to keep as many possibilities
open as possible,

02:31:37.840 --> 02:31:40.280
no matter how many
problems this causes

02:31:40.280 --> 02:31:43.990
the fabricator with his
need for a final vision,

02:31:43.990 --> 02:31:45.910
a goal to be achieved.

02:31:45.910 --> 02:31:47.800
Remember, the
fabricator's skills

02:31:47.800 --> 02:31:51.370
are his knowledge of the means
necessary to achieve a given

02:31:51.370 --> 02:31:52.270
end.

02:31:52.270 --> 02:31:56.110
There the artist is,
substituting images, testing,

02:31:56.110 --> 02:31:58.910
trying to see what works
best, sometimes unsure

02:31:58.910 --> 02:32:02.680
until it is done while
the fabricator writhes.

02:32:02.680 --> 02:32:05.000
How can one work for
someone, he thinks,

02:32:05.000 --> 02:32:07.180
who doesn't seem to
know what he's doing?

02:32:07.180 --> 02:32:10.170
While in fact the artist is
doing exactly what he must

02:32:10.170 --> 02:32:13.440
do to complete the project.

02:32:13.440 --> 02:32:15.580
In conclusion, I want
to do two things.

02:32:15.580 --> 02:32:18.700
The first is to review
what the artist as typified

02:32:18.700 --> 02:32:22.360
by Frank Stella does, and then
to draw some conclusions as

02:32:22.360 --> 02:32:24.910
to what might be
applicable to the education

02:32:24.910 --> 02:32:26.600
of the young engineer.

02:32:26.600 --> 02:32:28.880
First, what I see
Frank doing, what I

02:32:28.880 --> 02:32:31.990
will call the artist's way.

02:32:31.990 --> 02:32:35.390
Get as much history and richness
as possible into the art,

02:32:35.390 --> 02:32:38.080
including the processing
and the evidence

02:32:38.080 --> 02:32:40.670
of man working with tools.

02:32:40.670 --> 02:32:42.220
Do not commit too soon.

02:32:42.220 --> 02:32:45.990
Stay as open as possible
as long as possible

02:32:45.990 --> 02:32:48.500
to other solutions,
for it is in the making

02:32:48.500 --> 02:32:51.490
that one sees
fulfillment of his ideas.

02:32:51.490 --> 02:32:55.810
Be aware of changes and the
effect those changes have.

02:32:55.810 --> 02:32:56.640
A brief tale.

02:32:56.640 --> 02:32:58.710
We were making a lot
of stainless steel

02:32:58.710 --> 02:33:01.440
castings for Stella in 304.

02:33:01.440 --> 02:33:05.520
A project for another artist
came in that required 316.

02:33:05.520 --> 02:33:07.570
As an engineer, I'm
accustomed to looking

02:33:07.570 --> 02:33:10.380
at these materials finished.

02:33:10.380 --> 02:33:14.530
And finished, it's almost
impossible to tell them apart,

02:33:14.530 --> 02:33:17.370
and the differences in
composition are not great.

02:33:17.370 --> 02:33:21.470
So to give someone
316 is like being

02:33:21.470 --> 02:33:23.860
moved into first-class
seats if the tourist

02:33:23.860 --> 02:33:25.970
section has been overbooked.

02:33:25.970 --> 02:33:28.480
We made castings
in 316 for a while

02:33:28.480 --> 02:33:30.770
and then switched back to 304.

02:33:30.770 --> 02:33:32.480
Almost immediately
a cry went up.

02:33:32.480 --> 02:33:33.790
What's going on?

02:33:33.790 --> 02:33:37.250
It turns out that 316
in the as-cast condition

02:33:37.250 --> 02:33:40.540
looks very different
from 304, something

02:33:40.540 --> 02:33:45.210
we engineers had been totally
unaware of, but not the artist.

02:33:45.210 --> 02:33:47.070
Finally, invest self.

02:33:47.070 --> 02:33:50.380
If the artist's idea
fails, the artist fails.

02:33:50.380 --> 02:33:52.120
If you want to get
more out of something,

02:33:52.120 --> 02:33:54.450
give more of yourself.

02:33:54.450 --> 02:33:55.980
That's the way of the artist.

02:33:55.980 --> 02:33:59.690
All of them make sense to me and
deserve serious consideration

02:33:59.690 --> 02:34:01.120
by young engineers.

02:34:01.120 --> 02:34:02.610
But there's more.

02:34:02.610 --> 02:34:05.020
Bright young people
come to MIT to learn,

02:34:05.020 --> 02:34:08.640
to learn the latest and the
newest technology, what works

02:34:08.640 --> 02:34:11.140
and how it works.

02:34:11.140 --> 02:34:13.660
In the study of science
and engineering,

02:34:13.660 --> 02:34:16.670
a discarded or
disproved idea is just

02:34:16.670 --> 02:34:20.110
that, discarded,
interesting, perhaps, only

02:34:20.110 --> 02:34:22.010
in where it was wrong.

02:34:22.010 --> 02:34:25.250
In engineering, there's
also a real-world test.

02:34:25.250 --> 02:34:28.040
Questions totally
inappropriate to works of art

02:34:28.040 --> 02:34:31.430
get asked of the engineer,
like, what's it for?

02:34:31.430 --> 02:34:32.440
Did it work?

02:34:32.440 --> 02:34:34.320
Did the bridge fall down?

02:34:34.320 --> 02:34:38.100
Contrast that to art, where
discarded ideas and artists can

02:34:38.100 --> 02:34:39.430
and do reappear.

02:34:39.430 --> 02:34:42.710
And certainly in art,
there is no sure test

02:34:42.710 --> 02:34:44.456
for the quality of art.

02:34:44.456 --> 02:34:47.100
What can happen, then, to the
young engineering student which

02:34:47.100 --> 02:34:48.980
has not happened
to the artist is

02:34:48.980 --> 02:34:52.020
that he may overvalue the
contribution of his teachers

02:34:52.020 --> 02:34:56.260
and either ignore or not develop
awareness of his feelings

02:34:56.260 --> 02:34:59.210
and the importance of
expressing those feelings.

02:34:59.210 --> 02:35:03.880
We need to, and I hope are doing
so, as evidenced by all of us

02:35:03.880 --> 02:35:08.060
here today, make young engineers
aware of the importance

02:35:08.060 --> 02:35:11.730
of including their own
feelings, their own expression

02:35:11.730 --> 02:35:13.910
in the solutions
to problems they

02:35:13.910 --> 02:35:16.100
will face in their careers.

02:35:16.100 --> 02:35:19.710
We can make that more possible
by providing opportunities

02:35:19.710 --> 02:35:22.310
and making sure that
these opportunities are

02:35:22.310 --> 02:35:25.230
important to our
community for our students

02:35:25.230 --> 02:35:26.990
to express themselves.

02:35:26.990 --> 02:35:30.170
Or failing that, provide them
with frequent opportunities

02:35:30.170 --> 02:35:32.930
for aesthetic experiences.

02:35:32.930 --> 02:35:36.110
Whether or not this will make
better engineers can and will

02:35:36.110 --> 02:35:37.350
be debated.

02:35:37.350 --> 02:35:39.590
But art and aesthetic
experience will

02:35:39.590 --> 02:35:41.520
do what it always has done.

02:35:41.520 --> 02:35:44.890
Enrich the individual and give
a historical and aesthetic

02:35:44.890 --> 02:35:47.580
perspective, an
artistic dimension,

02:35:47.580 --> 02:35:52.400
to our students'
understanding and aspirations.

02:35:52.400 --> 02:35:55.860
And given the ever-growing
power of engineering,

02:35:55.860 --> 02:35:57.870
perhaps it's not
too wild to suggest

02:35:57.870 --> 02:36:01.710
that this new engineering
will give creators like Stella

02:36:01.710 --> 02:36:06.460
compositional liberties
unimaginable not so long ago.

02:36:06.460 --> 02:36:09.380
Perhaps one can even
imagine students

02:36:09.380 --> 02:36:11.950
being taught, as I
have learned working

02:36:11.950 --> 02:36:16.443
as an engineer for artists,
function can follow form.

02:36:16.443 --> 02:36:18.407
Thank you.

02:36:18.407 --> 02:36:21.844
[APPLAUSE]

02:36:38.090 --> 02:36:39.760
HARRIS: As we
continue our journey

02:36:39.760 --> 02:36:42.430
through the schools at
MIT, we have arrived

02:36:42.430 --> 02:36:44.880
at the School of Humanities.

02:36:44.880 --> 02:36:47.280
And my five years
at MIT are nicely

02:36:47.280 --> 02:36:51.720
bounded by the MIT performance
of Professor Tod Machover's

02:36:51.720 --> 02:36:56.210
opera "Valleys" in June
of 1989 and the premiere

02:36:56.210 --> 02:36:59.440
of John Harbison's cello
concerto at the Boston Symphony

02:36:59.440 --> 02:37:03.970
Orchestra in just April 1994.

02:37:03.970 --> 02:37:06.430
And speaking of the
Boston Symphony Orchestra,

02:37:06.430 --> 02:37:09.428
let me take this opportunity
to offer them a special thanks

02:37:09.428 --> 02:37:12.940
today to the orchestra,
to the players,

02:37:12.940 --> 02:37:15.510
and to the Boston Musicians
Association of the American

02:37:15.510 --> 02:37:20.890
Federation of Musicians Local
9535 for their permission

02:37:20.890 --> 02:37:25.750
to tape the final rehearsal
of John Harbison's concerto

02:37:25.750 --> 02:37:27.690
and to play portions
of that this morning.

02:37:30.430 --> 02:37:33.440
Both Tod and John represent
the broad spectrum

02:37:33.440 --> 02:37:36.930
that exists not just
across the arts at MIT

02:37:36.930 --> 02:37:39.570
but even within single arts.

02:37:39.570 --> 02:37:41.670
While Tod works
with and develops

02:37:41.670 --> 02:37:43.700
new interactive
technologies that

02:37:43.700 --> 02:37:46.690
have included a new
series of hyperinstruments

02:37:46.690 --> 02:37:49.820
and a wired conductor's glove
that interacts electronically

02:37:49.820 --> 02:37:54.450
with the players instruments,
John works with pen and paper

02:37:54.450 --> 02:37:57.260
and conducts the
traditional way,

02:37:57.260 --> 02:38:00.250
as recently when he
conducted Handel's "Messiah"

02:38:00.250 --> 02:38:03.300
for the Handel
and Haydn Society.

02:38:03.300 --> 02:38:07.850
But both composers have
written viola concertos

02:38:07.850 --> 02:38:10.680
for the same artist,
Kim Kashkashian,

02:38:10.680 --> 02:38:14.870
and both have written cello
concertos for Yo-Yo Ma.

02:38:14.870 --> 02:38:18.050
Both explore and create
sounds and structures

02:38:18.050 --> 02:38:20.590
that have never before existed.

02:38:20.590 --> 02:38:23.760
And both are steeped in the
continuum of historical music

02:38:23.760 --> 02:38:25.800
making.

02:38:25.800 --> 02:38:28.820
Helping to tie this
discussion together today

02:38:28.820 --> 02:38:31.410
is Lloyd Schwartz,
professor of English

02:38:31.410 --> 02:38:33.880
at University of
Massachusetts Boston

02:38:33.880 --> 02:38:37.890
and classical musical editor
of the Boston Phoenix.

02:38:37.890 --> 02:38:41.550
He's a regular commentator for
classical music on National

02:38:41.550 --> 02:38:44.330
Public Radio's Fresh Air.

02:38:44.330 --> 02:38:47.930
Just this April Mr. Schwartz
was awarded a Pulitzer Prize

02:38:47.930 --> 02:38:49.720
in music criticism.

02:38:49.720 --> 02:38:51.900
John previously has
won a Pulitzer Prize

02:38:51.900 --> 02:38:53.890
for his oratorio.

02:38:53.890 --> 02:38:58.640
And this spring, I have to say,
won the Killian Faculty Award

02:38:58.640 --> 02:39:01.890
at MIT, the highest award
given to faculty members

02:39:01.890 --> 02:39:04.620
at the Institute, an
award that was previously

02:39:04.620 --> 02:39:08.070
won by Professor Morrison.

02:39:08.070 --> 02:39:10.960
And I am delighted that John is
the first professor in the arts

02:39:10.960 --> 02:39:12.350
to have won that award.

02:39:12.350 --> 02:39:14.370
And it gives me great
pleasure to introduce

02:39:14.370 --> 02:39:18.168
Professors Machover,
Harbison, and Mr. Schwartz.

02:39:18.168 --> 02:39:21.626
[APPLAUSE]

02:39:23.709 --> 02:39:25.084
MACHOVER: Which
side to you want?

02:39:25.084 --> 02:39:26.417
SCHWARTZ: I'll just [INAUDIBLE].

02:39:33.490 --> 02:39:35.700
Good morning.

02:39:35.700 --> 02:39:36.820
MACHOVER: Just, I guess.

02:39:36.820 --> 02:39:40.210
SCHWARTZ: This is-- part of
this is the entertainment

02:39:40.210 --> 02:39:42.690
part of the program
today because you'll

02:39:42.690 --> 02:39:45.450
get to hear some
brief selections

02:39:45.450 --> 02:39:48.520
of some wonderful music.

02:39:48.520 --> 02:39:52.450
Certainly one of the
joys of being a music

02:39:52.450 --> 02:39:55.550
critic in this city
has been to experience

02:39:55.550 --> 02:40:00.790
the new works of
these two marvelous

02:40:00.790 --> 02:40:06.775
composers, who also happen to
both be on the faculty at MIT.

02:40:09.800 --> 02:40:16.190
We'll hear two clips
of recent pieces.

02:40:16.190 --> 02:40:23.130
John's brand-new cello concerto
performed by the Boston

02:40:23.130 --> 02:40:29.500
Symphony Orchestra, and
Tod's cello solo piece,

02:40:29.500 --> 02:40:36.130
which is solo and for cello and
computer, "Begin Again Again, "

02:40:36.130 --> 02:40:41.950
which had its premiere
at Tanglewood in 1991.

02:40:41.950 --> 02:40:45.555
And I'll ask each of them to
introduce the brief clips.

02:40:48.314 --> 02:40:49.730
HARBISON: In the
talk show format,

02:40:49.730 --> 02:40:51.771
I'm supposed to be very
surprised there's a clip.

02:40:51.771 --> 02:40:56.560
But actually I-- I
know that there is one.

02:40:56.560 --> 02:41:00.710
What you're going
to see and hear

02:41:00.710 --> 02:41:05.380
is three minutes, no
more, I assure you,

02:41:05.380 --> 02:41:07.770
by our contract with
the Boston Symphony,

02:41:07.770 --> 02:41:12.650
of-- actually less-- of a
segment from the first section

02:41:12.650 --> 02:41:16.850
of my cello concerto, a
rehearsal tape, which we were

02:41:16.850 --> 02:41:19.860
allowed to take with available
sound and available light

02:41:19.860 --> 02:41:22.030
and one camera angle.

02:41:22.030 --> 02:41:24.450
That being the
case, and this being

02:41:24.450 --> 02:41:27.215
a much more visual
than oral world

02:41:27.215 --> 02:41:29.260
that we live in, as
has been pointed out

02:41:29.260 --> 02:41:32.640
by various of the
previous speakers,

02:41:32.640 --> 02:41:36.700
there's attached to it right
at the beginning 50 seconds

02:41:36.700 --> 02:41:41.730
of somewhat more lively visual
material from a documentary

02:41:41.730 --> 02:41:43.260
from Wisconsin Public Radio.

02:41:43.260 --> 02:41:45.590
Which I guess sort
of quickly flashes

02:41:45.590 --> 02:41:48.650
by you, various parts of my
life to the accompaniment

02:41:48.650 --> 02:41:51.720
of another concerto, "Concerto
for Brass and Orchestra,"

02:41:51.720 --> 02:41:53.750
on the Los Angeles
Philharmonic recording.

02:41:53.750 --> 02:41:56.860
So you'll hear all
together four minutes

02:41:56.860 --> 02:42:00.430
a little sampler of this
and that and then a bit

02:42:00.430 --> 02:42:03.006
of the Boston
Symphony rehearsal.

02:42:03.006 --> 02:42:05.009
Guess that's it.

02:42:05.009 --> 02:42:06.488
SCHWARTZ: Can we have that clip?

02:42:10.259 --> 02:42:10.925
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

02:42:10.925 --> 02:42:11.277
[MUSIC - JOHN HARBISON,
"CONCERTO FOR DOUBLE BRASS

02:42:11.277 --> 02:42:13.883
CHOIR AND ORCHESTRA"]

02:42:22.760 --> 02:42:25.660
-I think the artists can't
be only on their own side.

02:42:25.660 --> 02:42:28.730
They have to look
for a voice that

02:42:28.730 --> 02:42:30.695
says what's not being said.

02:42:41.430 --> 02:42:45.680
We have to be terribly
connected to the strongest

02:42:45.680 --> 02:42:48.549
things in the tradition in order
to be able to make something

02:42:48.549 --> 02:42:49.296
that's new.

02:43:07.722 --> 02:43:10.710
[APPLAUSE]

02:43:19.176 --> 02:43:22.164
[MUSIC - JOHN HARBISON]

02:46:07.002 --> 02:46:08.496
[END PLAYBACK]

02:46:10.986 --> 02:46:11.647
[APPLAUSE]

02:46:11.647 --> 02:46:12.480
MACHOVER: Beautiful.

02:46:20.448 --> 02:46:22.090
SCHWARTZ: Tod.

02:46:22.090 --> 02:46:26.160
MACHOVER: Now we'll see a
clip of about equal length

02:46:26.160 --> 02:46:28.660
of the piece that I wrote for
Yo-Yo Ma about three years ago

02:46:28.660 --> 02:46:30.650
called "Begin Again Again."

02:46:30.650 --> 02:46:33.830
Yo-Yo is, well, he's
almost alone on stage.

02:46:33.830 --> 02:46:37.010
You'll notice that
there's only one musician,

02:46:37.010 --> 02:46:40.120
but there are a series of
computer technicians on stage.

02:46:40.120 --> 02:46:43.930
And Yo-Yo is playing something
like a cello, but not exactly.

02:46:43.930 --> 02:46:46.310
One of my interests
in general has

02:46:46.310 --> 02:46:49.330
been to make technology
be in the service

02:46:49.330 --> 02:46:51.950
of human expression, to
make it easier for people

02:46:51.950 --> 02:46:53.660
to communicate and
express themselves

02:46:53.660 --> 02:46:54.970
because of technology.

02:46:54.970 --> 02:46:58.120
And we designed here
at the MIT Media Lab

02:46:58.120 --> 02:47:01.070
a special cello for
Yo-Yo for this project.

02:47:01.070 --> 02:47:03.330
The general category is
called hyperinstruments,

02:47:03.330 --> 02:47:06.400
and this is therefore
a hypercello.

02:47:06.400 --> 02:47:08.620
The cello itself is
somewhat different.

02:47:08.620 --> 02:47:10.090
It's made out of
wood, but it needs

02:47:10.090 --> 02:47:11.730
to be amplified to be heard.

02:47:11.730 --> 02:47:15.740
And you'll notice Yo-Yo has
cables coming out of his bow.

02:47:15.740 --> 02:47:17.310
He has a funny
thing on his wrist.

02:47:17.310 --> 02:47:19.920
And there are other little
antennas poking out.

02:47:19.920 --> 02:47:24.560
The idea of all of this work
is to amplify and expand

02:47:24.560 --> 02:47:27.780
what can be done with a normal
instrument by using technology

02:47:27.780 --> 02:47:31.250
and to allow the musician
to be as natural as possible

02:47:31.250 --> 02:47:32.880
in using this new
instrument to use

02:47:32.880 --> 02:47:35.580
all the skill and musicianship
that a performer like Yo-Yo

02:47:35.580 --> 02:47:36.890
has.

02:47:36.890 --> 02:47:39.200
That musicianship
is then interpreted

02:47:39.200 --> 02:47:42.200
by the instrument itself, and
different layers are added.

02:47:42.200 --> 02:47:43.910
So what you hear
is a solo piece.

02:47:43.910 --> 02:47:45.560
But through Yo-Yo's
playing, the notes

02:47:45.560 --> 02:47:47.180
he plays, the way
he phrases them,

02:47:47.180 --> 02:47:48.877
the color he gives
to his instrument,

02:47:48.877 --> 02:47:51.460
the computer responds and adds
to the various different layers

02:47:51.460 --> 02:47:52.140
that you'll see.

02:47:52.140 --> 02:47:54.390
At the end of the clip you'll
notice that he goes back

02:47:54.390 --> 02:47:55.900
to his normal instrument.

02:47:55.900 --> 02:47:57.660
In this piece, part
of the idea was

02:47:57.660 --> 02:48:00.300
that there's kind of a
struggle between the artist

02:48:00.300 --> 02:48:02.780
and the technology,
which becomes reconciled.

02:48:02.780 --> 02:48:06.710
We monitor his instrument at
the end just with a microphone.

02:48:06.710 --> 02:48:11.454
So this is a little bit
from "Begin Again Again."

02:48:11.454 --> 02:48:12.120
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

02:48:12.120 --> 02:48:15.900
-And last month they premiered
their piece "Begin Again Again"

02:48:15.900 --> 02:48:17.288
at Tanglewood.

02:48:17.288 --> 02:48:20.252
[MUSIC - TOD MACHOVER, "BEGIN
 AGAIN AGAIN"]

02:48:43.470 --> 02:48:46.010
-The whole goal of
my artistic work

02:48:46.010 --> 02:48:48.250
is to make connections
between things

02:48:48.250 --> 02:48:49.870
which seem to clash
when you first

02:48:49.870 --> 02:48:51.808
look at them without
making the effort.

02:48:51.808 --> 02:48:53.792
And in some ways
about how hard it is.

02:48:53.792 --> 02:48:56.768
[MUSIC - TOD MACHOVER, "BEGIN
 AGAIN AGAIN"]

02:49:39.095 --> 02:49:41.322
-And listening to
any piece of music

02:49:41.322 --> 02:49:43.680
is like going into
a different country.

02:49:43.680 --> 02:49:47.060
You go there, you're
stimulated because it sounds

02:49:47.060 --> 02:49:48.570
like a different language.

02:49:48.570 --> 02:49:50.520
But yet some things
are the same.

02:49:50.520 --> 02:49:55.690
So you figure out what the
values are in that new place.

02:49:55.690 --> 02:49:58.620
And essentially you
go on an adventure.

02:49:58.620 --> 02:50:02.850
And this piece is not
so far out that it's

02:50:02.850 --> 02:50:04.490
incomprehensible to anybody.

02:50:04.490 --> 02:50:08.350
Anybody that likes music,
that responds to it, I think,

02:50:08.350 --> 02:50:12.420
is going to be able to
find something in it

02:50:12.420 --> 02:50:15.450
to hold onto as a
point of departure.

02:50:15.450 --> 02:50:18.464
And then the adventure
begins for them.

02:50:18.464 --> 02:50:21.950
[MUSIC - TOD MACHOVER, "BEGIN
 AGAIN AGAIN"]

02:51:18.645 --> 02:51:21.120
[END PLAYBACK]

02:51:21.120 --> 02:51:24.585
[APPLAUSE]

02:51:31.560 --> 02:51:38.400
SCHWARTZ: Yo-Yo Ma is
another Boston-area resident.

02:51:38.400 --> 02:51:42.610
And I think until just
a few years ago he

02:51:42.610 --> 02:51:46.770
was really far better
known for his performances

02:51:46.770 --> 02:51:51.230
of traditional classical
music written for the cello.

02:51:51.230 --> 02:51:54.580
I think that
reputation is changing.

02:51:54.580 --> 02:51:57.720
One of the things that
I've been eager to ask

02:51:57.720 --> 02:52:04.100
you is, what was working with
or for Yo-Yo Ma really like?

02:52:04.100 --> 02:52:08.110
And maybe the more serious
side of that question

02:52:08.110 --> 02:52:16.590
is, how much of the work of
composition in these pieces

02:52:16.590 --> 02:52:21.440
was inspired by the fact that
you knew you were writing it

02:52:21.440 --> 02:52:25.810
for a performer like Yo-Yo?

02:52:25.810 --> 02:52:26.335
John?

02:52:26.335 --> 02:52:29.080
HARBISON: Well, we were
joking before we came on

02:52:29.080 --> 02:52:31.720
that, just once in these talk
shows, someone should say,

02:52:31.720 --> 02:52:33.440
he's a terrible
fellow and he was

02:52:33.440 --> 02:52:37.410
uncooperative and difficult.
Because usually they say,

02:52:37.410 --> 02:52:39.740
it's just-- life on
the set was great

02:52:39.740 --> 02:52:40.910
and we all loved each other.

02:52:40.910 --> 02:52:43.370
Well, in truth with Yo-Yo
he is a wonderful colleague.

02:52:43.370 --> 02:52:49.030
He's very genuine and direct
and tremendously committed

02:52:49.030 --> 02:52:49.950
to what he does.

02:52:49.950 --> 02:52:55.505
And it is a very
wonderful experience

02:52:55.505 --> 02:52:57.380
to write for a performer
who's eager for what

02:52:57.380 --> 02:52:58.010
he's going to get.

02:52:58.010 --> 02:52:59.551
I'm sure Tod had
that experience too.

02:52:59.551 --> 02:53:00.887
I mean, where is the piece?

02:53:00.887 --> 02:53:01.470
Let's have it.

02:53:01.470 --> 02:53:03.500
Let's talk about it, is
definitely the attitude

02:53:03.500 --> 02:53:04.710
that he takes.

02:53:04.710 --> 02:53:09.680
And by the time I wrote my
piece for him, he had played,

02:53:09.680 --> 02:53:13.250
I think, five or
six, maybe more,

02:53:13.250 --> 02:53:19.040
cello concerto premieres
and was clearly very

02:53:19.040 --> 02:53:23.405
gripped by the idea of
spiriting pieces into the world,

02:53:23.405 --> 02:53:25.280
as you say, rather
different from the earlier

02:53:25.280 --> 02:53:28.790
part of his career, where
this was a very rare event.

02:53:28.790 --> 02:53:34.900
So just his excitement at
that part of his musical life

02:53:34.900 --> 02:53:38.780
is, I think, very
infectious to a composer.

02:53:38.780 --> 02:53:44.230
SCHWARTZ: How much of what's on
the page, what's in the score

02:53:44.230 --> 02:53:49.270
do you hear as Yo-Yo
Ma, if anything?

02:53:49.270 --> 02:53:51.890
HARBISON: In this case,
I'm sure in Tod's case,

02:53:51.890 --> 02:53:53.430
that Yo-Yo was the whole thing.

02:53:53.430 --> 02:53:55.320
It was extremely direct.

02:53:55.320 --> 02:53:58.480
In my case, this concerto
is much more centered

02:53:58.480 --> 02:54:01.700
around the soloist than other
concerti that I've written.

02:54:01.700 --> 02:54:05.880
Probably only my violin
concerto is equally so.

02:54:05.880 --> 02:54:08.707
I've been more
interested in fitting

02:54:08.707 --> 02:54:11.040
the orchestra together with
the soloist in other pieces.

02:54:11.040 --> 02:54:14.642
Here I really wanted the
soloist to be the central focus.

02:54:14.642 --> 02:54:16.100
And I think it was
partly because I

02:54:16.100 --> 02:54:17.850
knew that, no matter
what happened,

02:54:17.850 --> 02:54:20.920
he would hold the
stage, both on virtue

02:54:20.920 --> 02:54:23.060
of his presence and
the way he plays.

02:54:23.060 --> 02:54:26.700
So I really thought I
should just go with that.

02:54:26.700 --> 02:54:30.790
And I, in fact,
even in the themes

02:54:30.790 --> 02:54:34.550
thought I was dealing with
him somewhat biographically.

02:54:34.550 --> 02:54:36.300
But he didn't particularly
like that idea,

02:54:36.300 --> 02:54:39.890
so I dropped it rather
quickly in terms of talking.

02:54:39.890 --> 02:54:43.050
But I think there is
some element of that.

02:54:43.050 --> 02:54:47.780
SCHWARTZ: Do you think his sound
is in the contours of the music

02:54:47.780 --> 02:54:51.079
that you've written for
the cello in that piece?

02:54:51.079 --> 02:54:52.120
HARBISON: It probably is.

02:54:52.120 --> 02:54:53.540
I think at the
same time you also

02:54:53.540 --> 02:54:55.310
hope that other
cellists will play it

02:54:55.310 --> 02:54:58.300
so that I think you
go not so totally

02:54:58.300 --> 02:55:02.350
into the particularities
of the player

02:55:02.350 --> 02:55:05.120
that you rule out
other approaches.

02:55:05.120 --> 02:55:06.530
But I think very much, yes.

02:55:06.530 --> 02:55:10.080
And his sound has also had some
influence on other cellists

02:55:10.080 --> 02:55:12.360
by now.

02:55:12.360 --> 02:55:15.240
SCHWARTZ: You were writing
something for Yo-Yo

02:55:15.240 --> 02:55:18.830
that was completely new to
him because he was actually

02:55:18.830 --> 02:55:22.500
playing an instrument,
the hypercello, which

02:55:22.500 --> 02:55:23.780
he hadn't played before.

02:55:23.780 --> 02:55:25.900
What was your
experience with that?

02:55:25.900 --> 02:55:27.430
MACHOVER: Well, many things.

02:55:27.430 --> 02:55:30.120
One of the reasons that Yo-Yo
was interested in the project

02:55:30.120 --> 02:55:34.097
was because he was very
concerned about thinking

02:55:34.097 --> 02:55:35.680
about the future of
string instruments

02:55:35.680 --> 02:55:37.790
and thinking about
how they could evolve.

02:55:37.790 --> 02:55:41.080
He was actually quite interested
in knowing about new things

02:55:41.080 --> 02:55:42.440
about computer programming.

02:55:42.440 --> 02:55:43.990
Just hanging around
the Media Lab,

02:55:43.990 --> 02:55:47.810
for instance, and finding out
what the students were doing.

02:55:47.810 --> 02:55:50.620
And at that time he was
interested in CD-ROMs

02:55:50.620 --> 02:55:52.420
and where that would go.

02:55:52.420 --> 02:55:53.900
I agree totally
with John that one

02:55:53.900 --> 02:55:55.858
of the things that struck
me about this project

02:55:55.858 --> 02:55:58.504
with Yo-Yo is, here we
were starting from scratch,

02:55:58.504 --> 02:55:59.920
designing a totally
new instrument

02:55:59.920 --> 02:56:02.910
with new ways of playing it,
took longer than we expected.

02:56:02.910 --> 02:56:05.210
But from the very
beginning of the project,

02:56:05.210 --> 02:56:08.960
Yo-Yo would always come and
say, so what's the piece about?

02:56:08.960 --> 02:56:11.070
What am I meant to convey?

02:56:11.070 --> 02:56:13.070
The instrument's fine,
that'll be great.

02:56:13.070 --> 02:56:16.190
We'll make it, but what will
it leave the audience with?

02:56:16.190 --> 02:56:20.290
And actually got me to
talk about that earlier

02:56:20.290 --> 02:56:22.930
in the process, while I was
still planning and writing it,

02:56:22.930 --> 02:56:25.957
earlier than I usually talk
about that to other people.

02:56:25.957 --> 02:56:27.040
Another thing about Yo-Yo.

02:56:27.040 --> 02:56:30.230
A lot has been said today about
different styles of thinking

02:56:30.230 --> 02:56:35.050
and engineering and
intuitive things.

02:56:35.050 --> 02:56:37.210
Yo-Yo is one of the
very rare performers,

02:56:37.210 --> 02:56:40.020
maybe-- I can't think of
too many other people-- who

02:56:40.020 --> 02:56:42.620
is both incredibly
gifted physically, just

02:56:42.620 --> 02:56:43.764
like a great athlete.

02:56:43.764 --> 02:56:45.680
I think he probably
started playing the cello.

02:56:45.680 --> 02:56:48.010
And I would guess he
probably didn't have

02:56:48.010 --> 02:56:49.690
to practice incredibly hard.

02:56:49.690 --> 02:56:51.090
I'm sure he did practice hard.

02:56:51.090 --> 02:56:52.756
But he just has an
amazing natural gift.

02:56:52.756 --> 02:56:55.330
He can pick up an instrument
and learn things very quickly.

02:56:55.330 --> 02:56:56.540
It's just phenomenal.

02:56:56.540 --> 02:56:58.700
At the same time, he's
truly an intellectual

02:56:58.700 --> 02:57:01.480
and has always been very
interested in figuring out

02:57:01.480 --> 02:57:04.910
why physical gestures bring
out certain results, how

02:57:04.910 --> 02:57:05.920
music works.

02:57:05.920 --> 02:57:08.080
And for a project
like this where

02:57:08.080 --> 02:57:10.500
we're trying to take a lot of
things which musicians often

02:57:10.500 --> 02:57:13.320
take for granted, how
do you express phrases

02:57:13.320 --> 02:57:15.600
through the bowing,
how do changes in pitch

02:57:15.600 --> 02:57:17.485
convey little different
bits of meaning?

02:57:17.485 --> 02:57:19.360
It's not obvious when
you stop to think about

02:57:19.360 --> 02:57:21.552
that how you can
break that down, let's

02:57:21.552 --> 02:57:23.760
say, in an engineering way
so that you can teach that

02:57:23.760 --> 02:57:24.720
to a computer.

02:57:24.720 --> 02:57:26.290
And Yo-Yo is one
of the few people

02:57:26.290 --> 02:57:28.430
I know who is such
a great performer

02:57:28.430 --> 02:57:30.910
and also is both interested
and capable of thinking

02:57:30.910 --> 02:57:31.880
through those problems.

02:57:31.880 --> 02:57:33.670
One of the side
problems of that,

02:57:33.670 --> 02:57:36.860
because of the fact
that he's so naturally

02:57:36.860 --> 02:57:39.510
gifted, is that in the early
stages of the project when

02:57:39.510 --> 02:57:42.300
our hypercello was pretty lousy,
actually, it didn't feel good,

02:57:42.300 --> 02:57:44.762
didn't sound good, we
could give him anything

02:57:44.762 --> 02:57:45.720
that we'd come up with.

02:57:45.720 --> 02:57:48.720
The original things looked
like black pieces of plastic

02:57:48.720 --> 02:57:50.430
and they sounded like
electric guitars.

02:57:50.430 --> 02:57:52.340
And he'd come into the
studio and we'd say,

02:57:52.340 --> 02:57:53.715
okay, Yo-Yo, is
this good enough?

02:57:53.715 --> 02:57:54.839
What do you think about it?

02:57:54.839 --> 02:57:57.240
And he'd sort of play it and
play some Bach suites on it

02:57:57.240 --> 02:57:59.724
and say, oh, yeah, it's great.

02:57:59.724 --> 02:58:00.390
Feels wonderful.

02:58:00.390 --> 02:58:02.969
So in some ways he
wasn't so demanding

02:58:02.969 --> 02:58:04.260
because he could play anything.

02:58:04.260 --> 02:58:07.530
But he was very, very
easy to trade ideas with.

02:58:07.530 --> 02:58:09.830
SCHWARTZ: So did
he actually affect

02:58:09.830 --> 02:58:13.040
the composition of the piece
while you were working on it?

02:58:13.040 --> 02:58:15.632
Or was that separate
in some way?

02:58:15.632 --> 02:58:17.090
MACHOVER: I actually
don't think he

02:58:17.090 --> 02:58:19.010
affected the actual composition.

02:58:19.010 --> 02:58:21.270
But I think since we were
talking about it while I

02:58:21.270 --> 02:58:24.517
was writing it, I think he did
make me aware of certain things

02:58:24.517 --> 02:58:25.600
that I'd put in the piece.

02:58:25.600 --> 02:58:27.960
For instance, when we had
the first rehearsal where

02:58:27.960 --> 02:58:30.700
there was actually a bit of
a few themes here and there.

02:58:30.700 --> 02:58:33.114
I'm a cellist
myself, and when he

02:58:33.114 --> 02:58:34.530
started playing
through the piece,

02:58:34.530 --> 02:58:37.249
he said, you know, this
reminds me-- maybe you

02:58:37.249 --> 02:58:38.790
didn't think of
this-- but it reminds

02:58:38.790 --> 02:58:42.780
me of the second solo suite
by Bach for cello in D minor.

02:58:42.780 --> 02:58:45.680
And when he said that, I
realized that in many ways

02:58:45.680 --> 02:58:46.260
he was true.

02:58:46.260 --> 02:58:48.790
It wasn't just in the
kind of feel of it.

02:58:48.790 --> 02:58:51.952
But in the tonality my piece
also centered around D.

02:58:51.952 --> 02:58:54.160
In the sense the piece is
called "Begin Again Again,"

02:58:54.160 --> 02:58:55.368
and it's a set of variations.

02:58:55.368 --> 02:58:57.870
It's kind of about
the performer trying

02:58:57.870 --> 02:59:00.539
to break out of a certain mold.

02:59:00.539 --> 02:59:02.330
He keeps trying to have
the melodies go up,

02:59:02.330 --> 02:59:04.163
and they keep turning
around and going down.

02:59:04.163 --> 02:59:06.780
And there's a kind of circular
process like that in the Bach.

02:59:06.780 --> 02:59:08.740
And when he put
his finger on that,

02:59:08.740 --> 02:59:11.240
I don't know that it changed
the rest of the way I wrote it,

02:59:11.240 --> 02:59:13.874
but it definitely clarified
what I was working on.

02:59:13.874 --> 02:59:16.290
And I never in a million years
would have thought of that,

02:59:16.290 --> 02:59:17.600
for instance.

02:59:17.600 --> 02:59:21.650
SCHWARTZ: Have you performed
this piece now at this point?

02:59:21.650 --> 02:59:25.640
MACHOVER: Yeah, not for
too many people, but.

02:59:25.640 --> 02:59:27.750
Also, I must say, I've
written other cello pieces,

02:59:27.750 --> 02:59:28.781
which I do perform.

02:59:28.781 --> 02:59:30.780
One thing about this piece
is that, because he's

02:59:30.780 --> 02:59:34.290
such a great virtuoso, I
didn't kind of restrain myself

02:59:34.290 --> 02:59:36.520
in terms of how
difficult the piece was,

02:59:36.520 --> 02:59:39.710
which was really a problem when
I decided to perform it myself.

02:59:39.710 --> 02:59:43.750
It was quite a bit harder
than what I usually write.

02:59:43.750 --> 02:59:46.900
And another thing is that,
since I knew it was for Yo-Yo,

02:59:46.900 --> 02:59:49.459
somebody who tours a
lot and also learns

02:59:49.459 --> 02:59:52.000
many different pieces, I really
wanted to write a piece where

02:59:52.000 --> 02:59:54.800
this hyperinstrument would
really do something new

02:59:54.800 --> 02:59:56.580
but also would feel
incredibly natural,

02:59:56.580 --> 02:59:58.514
would not be so hard
for him to learn

02:59:58.514 --> 03:00:00.680
how to make the transition
from normal cello playing

03:00:00.680 --> 03:00:03.600
to this different
kind of instrument.

03:00:03.600 --> 03:00:05.942
So it was a certain
way that I changed it

03:00:05.942 --> 03:00:06.900
because it was for him.

03:00:10.110 --> 03:00:13.500
SCHWARTZ: The reason
we're here, you're here,

03:00:13.500 --> 03:00:17.760
is that you're both
on the faculty at MIT.

03:00:17.760 --> 03:00:22.360
And John, how do
you see yourself

03:00:22.360 --> 03:00:30.745
fitting into musical,
cultural, academic life at MIT?

03:00:30.745 --> 03:00:33.010
HARBISON: Well, the simplest
answer is, of course,

03:00:33.010 --> 03:00:34.568
that I don't fit in.

03:00:34.568 --> 03:00:38.054
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

03:00:45.530 --> 03:00:50.180
There's more to it, though.

03:00:50.180 --> 03:00:54.730
The idea of fitting in is,
in some situations, terrific.

03:00:54.730 --> 03:00:57.150
And for someone like
Barry Vercoe or Tod,

03:00:57.150 --> 03:01:00.560
the resources of MIT are
obviously phenomenal.

03:01:00.560 --> 03:01:04.040
For me, the resource
of MIT was really

03:01:04.040 --> 03:01:07.470
the difference, that is,
the sense of not fitting in

03:01:07.470 --> 03:01:09.760
was, at least for
a lot of my life,

03:01:09.760 --> 03:01:11.880
tremendously salutary for me.

03:01:11.880 --> 03:01:14.814
I've always loved that feeling.

03:01:14.814 --> 03:01:17.690
And even probably
begins back at the time

03:01:17.690 --> 03:01:21.980
I told my parents I wanted to
compose an orchestra piece one

03:01:21.980 --> 03:01:23.840
summer and they sent
me to baseball camp.

03:01:26.840 --> 03:01:28.680
MACHOVER: Did you
write the piece anyway?

03:01:28.680 --> 03:01:30.410
HARBISON: I did.

03:01:30.410 --> 03:01:32.540
I did.

03:01:32.540 --> 03:01:36.550
But I think some of us who
started out here in music

03:01:36.550 --> 03:01:41.680
at MIT a long time ago in Klaus
[? Lipan's ?] days were aware

03:01:41.680 --> 03:01:45.870
that though the core of us
were the kind of musicians

03:01:45.870 --> 03:01:48.560
that Ellen described of sort
of Stone Age technology,

03:01:48.560 --> 03:01:53.520
with not even mechanical
pencils, you know.

03:01:53.520 --> 03:01:55.630
We were aware that
MIT would need

03:01:55.630 --> 03:01:59.150
to develop in directions
appropriate to the resources

03:01:59.150 --> 03:01:59.732
of MIT.

03:01:59.732 --> 03:02:01.940
And that was part of what--
one of the first things I

03:02:01.940 --> 03:02:04.940
did at MIT was participate
in the search for Barry

03:02:04.940 --> 03:02:08.565
Vercoe, who came and started
the computer music at MIT.

03:02:08.565 --> 03:02:10.190
And I think Tod's
first appearance here

03:02:10.190 --> 03:02:14.730
was in the summer
course that Barry held

03:02:14.730 --> 03:02:16.550
for a number of summers here.

03:02:16.550 --> 03:02:18.135
We also saw fairly
early on that we

03:02:18.135 --> 03:02:20.010
needed to go in the
direction of world music.

03:02:20.010 --> 03:02:24.220
And we began, actually, in the
mid-'70s and that direction.

03:02:24.220 --> 03:02:29.192
So I think some of us who are of
the dead white male persuasion,

03:02:29.192 --> 03:02:37.900
of which I've been told I was
already part, have in some more

03:02:37.900 --> 03:02:42.890
or less visionary way tried to
see how the resources of MIT

03:02:42.890 --> 03:02:45.210
could connect to the arts
as well as preserving

03:02:45.210 --> 03:02:50.699
a place for some of us who are
obviously involved in doing

03:02:50.699 --> 03:02:53.240
essentially what I was thinking
of when I was five years old,

03:02:53.240 --> 03:02:57.170
which is adding some pieces to
a repertoire, which at that age

03:02:57.170 --> 03:02:59.170
I already thought
was extraordinary.

03:02:59.170 --> 03:03:01.260
And I've stayed so
interested, really,

03:03:01.260 --> 03:03:02.920
in string quartets
and orchestral music

03:03:02.920 --> 03:03:07.385
that that's really where I
expect I'll probably wind up.

03:03:07.385 --> 03:03:11.570
But MIT is incredibly
hospitable to all kinds

03:03:11.570 --> 03:03:12.640
of activity in the arts.

03:03:12.640 --> 03:03:14.790
And our students are
extraordinarily diverse.

03:03:14.790 --> 03:03:16.630
Some of them really
just want to learn

03:03:16.630 --> 03:03:19.400
how to play a Schubert
trio, and others really

03:03:19.400 --> 03:03:23.520
want to find out on day one
how the computer can help them

03:03:23.520 --> 03:03:24.070
as artists.

03:03:24.070 --> 03:03:26.335
And I think that that
diversity is something

03:03:26.335 --> 03:03:29.830
that we're trying to, with
the resources we have,

03:03:29.830 --> 03:03:32.202
we're trying to respond to here.

03:03:32.202 --> 03:03:34.160
SCHWARTZ: Do you have
any impulse or temptation

03:03:34.160 --> 03:03:37.070
to take advantage of the
electronic facilities

03:03:37.070 --> 03:03:39.420
here in your own music?

03:03:39.420 --> 03:03:43.160
HARBISON: Well, in
1971, I think it was,

03:03:43.160 --> 03:03:47.580
I rented the largest
Hammond organ I could find,

03:03:47.580 --> 03:03:50.100
and I wrote a piece
called "Bermuda Triangle"

03:03:50.100 --> 03:03:54.090
for amplified cello, Hammond
organ, and amplified saxophone.

03:03:54.090 --> 03:03:56.680
And using those draw
bars, I felt like I

03:03:56.680 --> 03:03:58.160
was in my own computer studio.

03:03:58.160 --> 03:03:59.670
And I was very excited.

03:03:59.670 --> 03:04:03.300
I really thought that was the
direction I was going to go.

03:04:03.300 --> 03:04:07.335
But my one early experience
in the very early days

03:04:07.335 --> 03:04:08.710
of the computer
studio, I think I

03:04:08.710 --> 03:04:11.630
was environmentally somewhat
averse to the smallness

03:04:11.630 --> 03:04:13.090
of the rooms and so forth.

03:04:13.090 --> 03:04:14.560
I don't think
that's true anymore.

03:04:14.560 --> 03:04:16.130
People work in
nicer environments.

03:04:16.130 --> 03:04:19.360
But I may be at
this point so filled

03:04:19.360 --> 03:04:24.630
with projects in what we call
the acoustic world, which

03:04:24.630 --> 03:04:26.680
when I started out
was-- I was amazed

03:04:26.680 --> 03:04:29.225
to be told I played an
acoustic piano a few years ago.

03:04:29.225 --> 03:04:34.560
I always thought it was
a-- that I may be occupied

03:04:34.560 --> 03:04:37.861
that way to such a degree
that I may never wind up.

03:04:37.861 --> 03:04:39.360
I always have in
the back of my mind

03:04:39.360 --> 03:04:41.030
I should really try this out.

03:04:41.030 --> 03:04:43.600
And every once in a while when
I see Tod he says, come over

03:04:43.600 --> 03:04:44.572
and we'll plug you in.

03:04:47.344 --> 03:04:54.010
SCHWARTZ: I think your
role here is more expected

03:04:54.010 --> 03:04:56.860
and understandable.

03:04:56.860 --> 03:04:57.700
What do you do?

03:04:57.700 --> 03:04:59.920
Tell us some of the
things that you do here.

03:04:59.920 --> 03:05:02.140
You do more than
just compose music.

03:05:02.140 --> 03:05:04.190
MACHOVER: Yeah,
well, most of you

03:05:04.190 --> 03:05:06.360
probably know about the
Media Laboratory, which is

03:05:06.360 --> 03:05:07.680
one of the newer parts of MIT.

03:05:07.680 --> 03:05:09.880
It opened up in 1985.

03:05:09.880 --> 03:05:13.490
And the Media Laboratory has
kind of as a central goal

03:05:13.490 --> 03:05:20.020
to find ways of making
technology so that it responds

03:05:20.020 --> 03:05:21.920
more to what human
beings want to do,

03:05:21.920 --> 03:05:23.980
either in communication
or in expression.

03:05:23.980 --> 03:05:26.480
And hopefully to find
ways of developing

03:05:26.480 --> 03:05:28.950
new forms of what
might be expressed

03:05:28.950 --> 03:05:31.490
when the technology
and the tools change.

03:05:31.490 --> 03:05:33.280
So it's true.

03:05:33.280 --> 03:05:35.750
I try to-- it's funny.

03:05:35.750 --> 03:05:39.450
As John says, he has
projects for quite a while

03:05:39.450 --> 03:05:42.180
now that, because of
what he's already written

03:05:42.180 --> 03:05:44.710
and because of his interests,
are acoustic projects.

03:05:44.710 --> 03:05:47.000
I think because I'm at MIT
and because I work so much

03:05:47.000 --> 03:05:49.460
with technology,
most of the projects

03:05:49.460 --> 03:05:51.120
that I see coming
my way are projects

03:05:51.120 --> 03:05:53.360
that involve more technology.

03:05:53.360 --> 03:05:58.720
I sometimes-- before I came
to MIT, I used to sort of work

03:05:58.720 --> 03:06:00.652
for a couple of years
with new technology.

03:06:00.652 --> 03:06:02.360
And then I always
wanted to take a break.

03:06:02.360 --> 03:06:04.310
In fact, I have a
new CD coming out

03:06:04.310 --> 03:06:06.950
soon with one piece
for piano and computer

03:06:06.950 --> 03:06:09.340
and another very large piece
for piano all by itself.

03:06:09.340 --> 03:06:10.840
And I don't do that
so much anymore,

03:06:10.840 --> 03:06:12.700
and I sometimes miss it.

03:06:12.700 --> 03:06:14.410
But one thing that we
do at the Media Lab

03:06:14.410 --> 03:06:18.950
is we do big projects to
make different kinds of music

03:06:18.950 --> 03:06:20.930
and art pieces that
involve technology.

03:06:20.930 --> 03:06:23.410
We also spend a lot
of time thinking

03:06:23.410 --> 03:06:26.620
about the technology,
the tools themselves,

03:06:26.620 --> 03:06:28.650
that can be used by musicians.

03:06:28.650 --> 03:06:31.960
For instance, I mean, for
John, I think in some ways

03:06:31.960 --> 03:06:33.670
this technology is
terrific right now,

03:06:33.670 --> 03:06:35.600
but it has so far to go.

03:06:35.600 --> 03:06:38.722
It really isn't
quite at a stage yet

03:06:38.722 --> 03:06:40.680
where John would probably
be satisfied with it,

03:06:40.680 --> 03:06:41.304
quite honestly.

03:06:41.304 --> 03:06:42.840
I mean, there's
just so much to do

03:06:42.840 --> 03:06:46.010
to make technology be as
sophisticated as traditional

03:06:46.010 --> 03:06:47.800
instruments, both
in the way they

03:06:47.800 --> 03:06:50.210
sound and the way they feel.

03:06:50.210 --> 03:06:53.520
One thing that-- I teach
at the Media Laboratory.

03:06:53.520 --> 03:06:55.040
I give a two-term course.

03:06:55.040 --> 03:06:56.917
The first course is
kind of everything

03:06:56.917 --> 03:06:59.500
you've always wanted to know or
maybe not know about computers

03:06:59.500 --> 03:07:01.210
and music and technology.

03:07:01.210 --> 03:07:03.042
And the second term
is a project course,

03:07:03.042 --> 03:07:04.250
which is kind of interesting.

03:07:04.250 --> 03:07:09.460
One thing that I find at MIT
is that all the students I --

03:07:09.460 --> 03:07:12.560
I really mean this-- the
students are the most

03:07:12.560 --> 03:07:15.220
open-minded, intelligent,
and kind of nice people that

03:07:15.220 --> 03:07:16.000
I've met anywhere.

03:07:16.000 --> 03:07:17.540
They're really-- no,
they're fabulous.

03:07:17.540 --> 03:07:19.539
They also have incredibly
different backgrounds.

03:07:19.539 --> 03:07:23.770
And especially when you find
people coming to a music class,

03:07:23.770 --> 03:07:25.870
nobody knows the same thing.

03:07:25.870 --> 03:07:28.217
Many people have bits of
things they've specialized in.

03:07:28.217 --> 03:07:30.050
But since they haven't
been to conservatory,

03:07:30.050 --> 03:07:31.930
they've often played
an instrument,

03:07:31.930 --> 03:07:33.670
and they've done a
lot of their science,

03:07:33.670 --> 03:07:36.520
they're often--
sort of, I see them

03:07:36.520 --> 03:07:37.840
almost as pieces of a puzzle.

03:07:37.840 --> 03:07:39.400
When you put them
all together, you

03:07:39.400 --> 03:07:41.164
have an enormous
amount of knowledge.

03:07:41.164 --> 03:07:42.580
So I sort of go
out of my way when

03:07:42.580 --> 03:07:45.860
I choose people for my
class to pick people who are

03:07:45.860 --> 03:07:47.170
very different from each other.

03:07:47.170 --> 03:07:49.360
I usually pick people who
are both graduate students

03:07:49.360 --> 03:07:51.970
and undergraduates, people who
are engineers, and hardware

03:07:51.970 --> 03:07:54.610
builders, and composers, and
performers, and sometimes

03:07:54.610 --> 03:07:55.760
visual artists.

03:07:55.760 --> 03:07:58.730
And in the second
semester we often end up

03:07:58.730 --> 03:08:00.874
doing things which
I don't think really

03:08:00.874 --> 03:08:02.040
could be done anywhere else.

03:08:02.040 --> 03:08:05.990
My students just this last
weekend put on-- the idea

03:08:05.990 --> 03:08:09.660
was to find a new
form of concert

03:08:09.660 --> 03:08:12.377
where there were no performers
and the public who came in

03:08:12.377 --> 03:08:13.210
were the performers.

03:08:13.210 --> 03:08:16.014
And we took a big, open
space at the Media Laboratory

03:08:16.014 --> 03:08:18.430
and designed something with a
whole set of new instruments

03:08:18.430 --> 03:08:21.910
where people came in with
no instructions, no guides

03:08:21.910 --> 03:08:22.970
to tell them what to do.

03:08:22.970 --> 03:08:25.860
And by using their voices
and by using their gestures,

03:08:25.860 --> 03:08:28.360
they became part of
this music piece.

03:08:28.360 --> 03:08:31.011
And it was incredible because
it was all done by students.

03:08:31.011 --> 03:08:32.510
All I really did
was teach them what

03:08:32.510 --> 03:08:34.200
they needed to know to do
it and then kind of managed

03:08:34.200 --> 03:08:34.740
the project.

03:08:34.740 --> 03:08:35.906
But all the work was theirs.

03:08:35.906 --> 03:08:38.252
And it really stacks up
to anything of that--

03:08:38.252 --> 03:08:39.960
if you think of
interactive entertainment

03:08:39.960 --> 03:08:41.670
and where Hollywood
is going, this

03:08:41.670 --> 03:08:45.090
is an example of how that can
be done with kind of substance.

03:08:45.090 --> 03:08:49.200
And I can't imagine that really
being done anywhere else.

03:08:49.200 --> 03:08:53.580
SCHWARTZ: How rapidly is the
technology itself changing?

03:08:53.580 --> 03:08:55.640
MACHOVER: It goes
in fits and starts.

03:08:55.640 --> 03:08:58.090
The huge change in
music technology

03:08:58.090 --> 03:09:00.580
happened about 10 years
ago, really literally

03:09:00.580 --> 03:09:03.210
10 years ago, when three things
happened at the same time.

03:09:03.210 --> 03:09:04.755
It was the big boom of
the personal computer,

03:09:04.755 --> 03:09:06.940
so all of a sudden you
could get your own computer

03:09:06.940 --> 03:09:08.340
without a lot of money.

03:09:08.340 --> 03:09:10.760
The second thing is
Yamaha in particular

03:09:10.760 --> 03:09:14.480
bought the patent for a way
of making computer music very

03:09:14.480 --> 03:09:15.392
cheaply.

03:09:15.392 --> 03:09:16.600
It was developed at Stanford.

03:09:16.600 --> 03:09:17.360
They bought the patent.

03:09:17.360 --> 03:09:19.500
And all of a sudden computer
synthesizers came out.

03:09:19.500 --> 03:09:22.290
Then instead of only being in
places like MIT and Stanford,

03:09:22.290 --> 03:09:23.650
you could buy for $1,000.

03:09:23.650 --> 03:09:25.400
And the third thing,
which was remarkable,

03:09:25.400 --> 03:09:27.280
is that all the people
making these computer

03:09:27.280 --> 03:09:30.040
music instruments developed
a standard way of basically

03:09:30.040 --> 03:09:33.410
a cable called MIDI
of hooking together

03:09:33.410 --> 03:09:35.980
any computer and any
synthesizer to send computer

03:09:35.980 --> 03:09:37.390
information back and forth.

03:09:37.390 --> 03:09:39.030
So all of a sudden
computer music,

03:09:39.030 --> 03:09:41.230
instead of becoming kind
of an abstruse thing,

03:09:41.230 --> 03:09:42.710
became something
which is so common

03:09:42.710 --> 03:09:44.840
that even if you don't know
it, probably all of you

03:09:44.840 --> 03:09:47.131
listen to computer music all
the time in Hollywood film

03:09:47.131 --> 03:09:52.010
scores and most advertising and
more and more classical music.

03:09:52.010 --> 03:09:53.760
What we all found in
the last few years

03:09:53.760 --> 03:09:57.090
is that that was also
very constraining.

03:09:57.090 --> 03:09:59.310
And now there's a
whole new revolution

03:09:59.310 --> 03:10:02.010
where basically,
especially because

03:10:02.010 --> 03:10:04.200
of interactive
entertainment, you know,

03:10:04.200 --> 03:10:06.920
what you see happening with
communication companies

03:10:06.920 --> 03:10:09.040
and cable companies
and computer companies

03:10:09.040 --> 03:10:10.930
and the whole
Hollywood industry, all

03:10:10.930 --> 03:10:15.060
trying to figure out what it is
they're going to do together.

03:10:15.060 --> 03:10:17.380
I've never seen a period
of such rapid change

03:10:17.380 --> 03:10:20.080
as in the last six months or
so, not only in terms of people

03:10:20.080 --> 03:10:21.880
investing in new
interactive entertainment,

03:10:21.880 --> 03:10:23.380
but trying to figure
out what to do.

03:10:23.380 --> 03:10:24.879
And it's one of the
first times I've

03:10:24.879 --> 03:10:29.060
seen where we at the Academy
have had to be incredibly

03:10:29.060 --> 03:10:30.820
fast on our feet to
think of new ideas

03:10:30.820 --> 03:10:33.700
because there are very smart
people in the commercial world

03:10:33.700 --> 03:10:35.810
who are thinking
of stuff every day.

03:10:35.810 --> 03:10:38.120
And a lot of it's at least
as good as what we're doing.

03:10:38.120 --> 03:10:40.400
So it's a very
stimulating time, I think.

03:10:40.400 --> 03:10:43.510
It's changing very,
very fast right now.

03:10:43.510 --> 03:10:47.250
SCHWARTZ: Do you have any
questions for each other?

03:10:47.250 --> 03:10:51.529
HARBISON: Well, we can talk
to each other here at MIT.

03:10:51.529 --> 03:10:52.695
SCHWARTZ: But not in public.

03:11:03.690 --> 03:11:08.090
In that case, I
think we're hungry,

03:11:08.090 --> 03:11:10.070
and we've had a rich morning.

03:11:10.070 --> 03:11:12.030
Thank you very much.

03:11:12.030 --> 03:11:13.500
MACHOVER: I guess you're right.

03:11:13.500 --> 03:11:16.930
[APPLAUSE]

03:11:24.300 --> 03:11:26.950
HARRIS: If I could have your
indulgence and attention just

03:11:26.950 --> 03:11:30.406
for a moment, please, we will
get to lunch, I promise you.

03:11:30.406 --> 03:11:32.155
I feel a little bit
like a camp counselor.

03:11:35.350 --> 03:11:37.060
However, there are
a few announcements

03:11:37.060 --> 03:11:39.650
that I would like to make about
this afternoon's programs.

03:11:39.650 --> 03:11:42.300
Very briefly, I promise.

03:11:42.300 --> 03:11:45.280
One is that-- now
remember, the workshops

03:11:45.280 --> 03:11:47.700
for those of you who will be
here are from 3:00 to 5:00

03:11:47.700 --> 03:11:49.800
this afternoon after lunch.

03:11:49.800 --> 03:11:55.520
On this stage will be
the sing-along with John

03:11:55.520 --> 03:11:58.550
Oliver, who will be conducting
"Jesu, meine Freude."

03:11:58.550 --> 03:12:03.730
All of you who have
acoustic voices

03:12:03.730 --> 03:12:05.980
are welcome to come
and give this a try.

03:12:05.980 --> 03:12:09.260
This is something that the alums
have asked us to do for years.

03:12:09.260 --> 03:12:12.250
We're going to do it this
year and hope that some of you

03:12:12.250 --> 03:12:13.270
will come on stage.

03:12:13.270 --> 03:12:16.950
In your program booklets it
says that, during this workshop,

03:12:16.950 --> 03:12:20.140
a videotape of John
Harbison's Yo-Yo Ma concerto

03:12:20.140 --> 03:12:21.550
will be played.

03:12:21.550 --> 03:12:23.420
Of course, you
have just seen it,

03:12:23.420 --> 03:12:25.960
and it will not be played
again this afternoon in Kresge.

03:12:25.960 --> 03:12:28.320
I wanted you to know that.

03:12:28.320 --> 03:12:35.640
I also must announce with
very deep and personal regret

03:12:35.640 --> 03:12:40.670
the sad news of Professor Muriel
Cooper's unexpected passing

03:12:40.670 --> 03:12:42.370
last week.

03:12:42.370 --> 03:12:46.170
She was one of the
speakers who was expected

03:12:46.170 --> 03:12:49.170
to speak at another
workshop this afternoon,

03:12:49.170 --> 03:12:54.030
the one at the Media Laboratory
entitled Arts [? Bytes. ?]

03:12:54.030 --> 03:12:55.800
However, members
of her laboratory

03:12:55.800 --> 03:12:59.180
have stepped forward
enthusiastically and generously

03:12:59.180 --> 03:13:02.430
so that the work from the
Visible Language Workshop

03:13:02.430 --> 03:13:06.200
will still be presented at
the Media Lab this afternoon.

03:13:06.200 --> 03:13:08.160
The other workshops
are a workshop

03:13:08.160 --> 03:13:12.210
on theater, which we will be
able to participate and view

03:13:12.210 --> 03:13:16.950
an ongoing rehearsal of "Who's
Afraid of Virginia Woolf?"

03:13:16.950 --> 03:13:19.890
Downstairs in Little
Theatre from 3:00 to 5:00,

03:13:19.890 --> 03:13:23.430
a creative writing
workshop in Killian Hall,

03:13:23.430 --> 03:13:27.370
where you will hear MIT faculty
read from their recent work,

03:13:27.370 --> 03:13:29.850
including Alan Lightman
reading from his New York Times

03:13:29.850 --> 03:13:31.345
bestseller Einstein's Dreams.

03:13:34.720 --> 03:13:38.060
And Bill Mitchell
will have a chance

03:13:38.060 --> 03:13:43.490
to talk this afternoon
at a workshop on design,

03:13:43.490 --> 03:13:48.640
New Directions, which will
be held across the street.

03:13:48.640 --> 03:13:50.700
Don't forget, for those
of you who are in town,

03:13:50.700 --> 03:13:53.630
there will be tours tomorrow
morning at 9:30 and 10:30

03:13:53.630 --> 03:13:55.820
at the MIT Museum and
the List Visual Arts

03:13:55.820 --> 03:13:57.610
Center of current exhibitions.

03:13:57.610 --> 03:13:59.330
There will be tours
of the new biology

03:13:59.330 --> 03:14:01.450
building at 9:30
and 10:30, leaving

03:14:01.450 --> 03:14:03.640
from the Wiesner Building.

03:14:03.640 --> 03:14:07.080
There will be a
tour at 10 o'clock

03:14:07.080 --> 03:14:09.410
of the outdoor sculpture
collection at MIT,

03:14:09.410 --> 03:14:12.200
which has been named
the best in New England.

03:14:12.200 --> 03:14:14.140
Tomorrow afternoon
from 2:00 to 4:00,

03:14:14.140 --> 03:14:16.600
there will be the opening of
the Center of Advanced Visual

03:14:16.600 --> 03:14:20.070
Studies' 25th anniversary
retrospective exhibition

03:14:20.070 --> 03:14:21.770
at the MIT Museum.

03:14:21.770 --> 03:14:24.730
And Sunday afternoon,
for those of you in town,

03:14:24.730 --> 03:14:27.730
the spectacular opportunity
to hear a performance

03:14:27.730 --> 03:14:30.500
of AR Gurney's love letters.

03:14:30.500 --> 03:14:33.710
Gurney, who has been a member
of our faculty for many years,

03:14:33.710 --> 03:14:38.590
with the parts read by Gurney
himself and Kitty Carlisle

03:14:38.590 --> 03:14:41.470
Hart, who has been a member of
the Council for the Arts at MIT

03:14:41.470 --> 03:14:43.930
since its founding in 1971.

03:14:43.930 --> 03:14:45.770
I thank you all
for participating

03:14:45.770 --> 03:14:47.411
in this View of the
Arts at MIT, and I

03:14:47.411 --> 03:14:49.410
hope you enjoy the rest
of your reunion weekend.

03:14:49.410 --> 03:14:52.160
[APPLAUSE]

